WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
“There’s an app for that!”

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:12.000
It was a phrase so ubiquitous in the early
days of the smartphone craze that it’s hard

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.400
to believe Apple actually has it trademarked.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.560
It was a testament to a simple and immutable
truth about the world these new touchscreen

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.840
phones were creating: No matter how strange
and obscure the need, there would be an app

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.860
to fulfill it.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.300
Perhaps you remember iBeer - The app that
allowed you to pretend you were drinking a

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.380
tall glass of beer…

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:32.880
For some reason.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.840
There was Carrr Matey, an app that reminded
you where you parked your car…

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.460
In a pirate voice.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:44.160
And who could forget “I Am Bread”, a surreal
game about controlling a sentient slice of

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.860
bread on a quest to become toast.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.240
But there’s one app out there somewhere
on the market that you probably didn’t download.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:57.300
And if you did, well, you have our sincerest
apologies, because even seeing this video

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.520
pop out onto your feed probably sent a chill
down your spine - Well, if that chill ever

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.240
even left.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.580
Take it from one gentleman whose life took
a very strange turn after downloading a certain

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.620
app that the SCP Foundation calls SCP-1471.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.720
Because the sentiment “There’s an app
for that” doesn’t exclude experiencing

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.740
mortal terror…

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.560
Joe Lillis, an insurance salesman from Milwaukee,
had just gone through another atrocious date.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.500
After a mediocre meal and an uncomfortable
35 minutes of inane babble, sensing the whole

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.600
time that she really wasn’t that interested,
his date excused herself to take a quick phone

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.380
call outside.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.000
Sadly for Joe, she never returned, leaving
him to pick up the check.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.520
Of all the many words you could use to describe
poor Joe Lillis, the most pertinent would

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.480
be “lonely.”

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.580
Ever since Carol, his wife of ten years, had
passed away in a freak accident, he’d been

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.100
trying to find some kind of way to fill the
void.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.720
They’d been high school sweethearts, intent
on spending the rest of their lives with one

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:00.900
another.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.040
As fate would have it, only Carol would get
that tainted luxury.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.600
Joe would be forced to endure life after the
joy of living had run its course.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.200
He only hoped he might be lucky enough to
find love again.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.300
However, Joe was on the wrong side of forty,
and as so many others his age were already

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.840
hitched, he could feel his options going out
one by one.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.080
Would he be destined to live out the rest
of his days alone?

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.060
Joe didn’t feel like spending the back half
of his life catching reruns of Seinfeld and

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.760
tending to his fish; he needed to get out
of there.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:39.300
And thankfully, like the rest of us, he lived
in the internet age - He had more apps, websites,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.920
online services, and hot russian singles in
his area than he knew what to do with.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.580
So, surely, one would have the right person
for him!

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:54.900
He’d tried them all: Tinder, Hinge, Match.com,
Plenty of Fish, eHarmony, Bumble, Zoosk, OKCupid,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.000
Friend Finder, Deeply Lonely Singles with
Low Expectations.com, and so much more.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.380
However, all it seemed to achieve was setting
him up for more disappointment.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.480
None of the dates he’d managed to get ever
resulted in anything getting serious.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.400
Heck, it was a minor miracle if he managed
to get any of them on a second date.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.540
Was this it?

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.220
Was this his life now?

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:22.860
Had he only ever gotten one shot at love,
and the grasping claws of fate yanked it away

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.720
from him without a second thought?

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.300
Would life continue on the hamster wheel of
loneliness - sleeping, getting up, eating,

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.940
working, and sleeping again, every day getting
somehow both faster and slower as life trudged

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.640
on to a disappointing yet inevitable conclusion?

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.000
What a terrible fate to find yourself trapped
in…

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.860
Whenever Joe started feeling maudlin like
this, he knew it was time to get proactive

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:47.040
again.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.200
Maybe the right woman was out there - there
were billions of them, after all, surely at

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.960
least one of them would be the perfect person
for him.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.000
He just needed the perfect app…

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.980
He’d burned through all the most reputable
apps already, and was now perusing some of

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:07.380
the slightly seedier options - most of which
were likely data mining fronts from the Balkans.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.660
However, as generic app after generic app
passed, something different caught his eye.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:18.240
The icon was a smiling cartoon dog, and its
name was MalO Version.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.040
1.0.0.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.440
This gave him a little chuckle.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.180
At the very least, it was very different branding
from the rest of the dating apps he’d seen.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.480
Maybe it’d just been sorted into the wrong
section of the app store?

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.900
He decided he’d click it and take a look
at the app’s description.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.280
The description read:

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.760
“Never settle for those awkward feelings
of being alone ever again.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:44.880
MalO is an exciting and interactive experience
that will keep you engaged and intrigued.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.000
The anxiety of social&nbsp;
situations can be nerve-racking,&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.000
but after just a few hours of MalO you will

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.420
soon forget all about those painful emotions
of disappointment.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.800
Be part of the new craze that is quickly becoming
the next social substitute.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.540
Remember, the more you participate, the more
MalO will engage you.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.720
Your experience is completely up to you.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.820
Absolutely NO ADS.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:09.720
Enjoy!”

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.440
Well…

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.500
It certainly provoked Joe’s curiosity, at
the very least.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.960
He did want to banish his feelings of loneliness,
and seeing as the app was free and apparently

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.160
had no ads, he’d surely be foolish to not
at least give it a whirl.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.780
What’s the worst that could happen?

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:29.940
He began the installation and only then noticed
that the app had no listed developer.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:35.640
It took up 9.8 MB of memory, which he wasn’t
tech savvy enough to see any issues with.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.860
More than anything, Joe was just enticed by
the prospect of finally having another chance

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.140
at companionship with MalO.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.940
After all, it is the “next social substitute”,
whatever that means.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.860
However, Joe’s excitement was quickly quashed
when he hit the home screen button, and noticed

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.800
that the icon for the app&nbsp;
never actually materialized.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:56.700
Strange.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.740
He checked the app store portal again and&nbsp;

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.380
saw that, according to&nbsp;
them, the app had completely

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:01.920
downloaded.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:02.940
What gives?

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.420
Was it a glitch, or was MalO actually just
malware?

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:11.100
Either way, he was disheartened by the fact
that this immaterial app certainly wouldn’t

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:12.960
be getting him any companionship.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.000
Or so he thought, anyway.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.200
Joe was used to disappointment by now, so
he didn’t take the hit too personally.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.000
He decided to just play out the rest of his
evening on autopilot: Making himself some

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.340
soup, doing the laundry, watching more Seinfeld
reruns, taking a cold shower, and preparing

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:30.720
to cry himself to sleep again.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.780
MalO was already becoming a distant memory,
just like all deceptive sources of hope.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:42.120
But one strange thing happened that disrupted
Joe’s finely-tuned evening routine.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.340
He received a text message.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.780
This was incredibly strange, because nobody
ever seemed to text him.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.880
The last text he got was from Carol, just
before her accident, so it was almost surreal

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.420
to hear that alert sound now, after everything
that’d happened.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:02.100
He checked and saw that the text was an image
attachment sent from an unknown number.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.640
Perplexed yet curious, he decided to open
it.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.820
His curiosity soon gave&nbsp;
way to a kind of melancholy&nbsp;

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.160
nostalgia when he saw that the photo was of

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.320
his and Carol’s favorite cafe in town.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.680
They’d spent many a morning there, back
when she was alive.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.820
Treating themselves to a nice cup of coffee
and perhaps a croissant.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:24.960
Just seeing it again caused an involuntary
smile to spread across his face.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.180
It never even occurred to him, as it probably
would have to others, that this could be seen

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.380
as a little creepy.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.260
He hadn’t frequented the bakery since Carol
died.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:38.700
How would anyone even know that the place
held any significance for him?

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.020
Was it a stalker?

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:40.860
A ghost?

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.020
Or just a spooky coincidence?

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:46.080
None of these thoughts even crossed Joe’s
mind.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.700
He was just grateful for the surprising reminder
of the happiness he’d once had.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.640
For the next couple hours, things seemed…

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:54.480
Lighter.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.820
He went about his evening, checking the photo
every so often and smiling, until eventually

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:03.540
he found himself in bed, still looking into
the glow of his phone.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.940
It was such a beautiful little cafe…

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.740
Then, he froze.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.420
He noticed something in the picture.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:14.040
It’d been there the whole time, but only
now was he seeing rather than just looking.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:18.540
It was in the corner, staring through the
glass of the cafe’s door.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.660
So faint he almost wanted to dismiss it as
a trick of the light.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:26.340
It was a face - Well, not a face, more like
a skull.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.880
Not human, not anywhere near human.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:35.820
Long, slender, and canine, with protruding
fangs and vacant white eyes.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:40.080
The pure white of the skull was buried in
a nest of thick, black hair.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.420
It looked like it was crouching behind the
door, looking out, and grinning, whatever

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.500
the hell it was.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.860
Just seeing it changed the entire tone of
the picture.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.040
It was no longer a simple reminder of bygone
joy.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.320
Now, all that was radiating out of that image
was a palpable sense of dread.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.320
Was someone playing some kind of awful prank
on him?

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.620
Just then, he was jogged from his contemplation
by another alert.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:07.620
A new message from the same number as before.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.020
With great hesitation, he hovered his thumb
over the push notification and clicked.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.900
That’s when everything got a lot worse.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.820
It was a photo of a bus stop.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:24.540
Not just any bus stop, of course, it was Stop
C16 - The one that Joe always took to get

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:24.900
to work.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.280
It looked like it was taken relatively early
in the morning, but nobody was there.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:30.240
Well…

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.220
Not quite nobody.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.320
There was that figure again.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:39.720
It stood at full height, behind the partially
frosted glass that makes up the back of the

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:40.140
bus stop.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:45.900
That same large, black humanoid shape, with
a white, grinning dog skull where the face

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:46.440
should be.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:51.480
Something about it terrified him on such a
primal level, like the way our lizard brain

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.060
reacts to some ancient apex predator.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.320
And whatever this thing was, it clearly knew
something about him.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.080
How else could it stage all these photos?

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:05.820
Joe got out of bed and looked out the window,
down onto his dark front street.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:11.400
Empty, thankfully, but after this surprise
nightmare, he wasn’t going to take any chances.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:16.080
He grabbed a kitchen knife from downstairs
and placed it on his bedside cabinet, right

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.840
next to his phone, with 911 on speed dial.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:24.180
Joe Lillis, a 43 year old man, slept with
the lights on that night for the first time

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.440
in over thirty years.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:29.580
Sadly for him, the nightmare was just beginning.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:34.560
The next morning, Joe woke up unharmed, but
he wasn’t pleased to see that he’d gotten

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.420
several more texts in his sleep.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:40.740
There was one taken outside of the local insurance
company office where he worked.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:45.360
The strange creature with the skull for a
face was looming around the corner, peering

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.220
at the camera with its lipless grin.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.720
Like it was mocking him.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:53.280
Another photo was taken at the local supermarket
where Joe did most of his grocery shopping.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.800
The frame was centralized on the cereal aisle,
bordered on both sides by walls of garish

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:00.600
mascots, endlessly repeated.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:06.420
Down at the far end of the aisle was a looming,
dark figure, with that cold, canine skull

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.280
where a human face should be.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.820
There were a few more, but worst of all was
the last one.

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.100
It was taken at the cemetery.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:19.380
In the foreground, a headstone, reading “Carol
Lillis - Beloved Wife and Daughter.”

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:25.020
Joe was horrified to see that skull-faced
beast was rising up behind his wife’s grave,

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.860
long, clawed fingers curling around the top
of the headstone.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:34.080
That was the moment that Joe decided to go
to the police about all this, before things

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.580
got even more out of hand.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:40.500
He called an Uber to get down to the station
- He certainly didn’t feel like going anywhere

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.840
near his regular bus stop after last night.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:47.760
He showed the photos he’d been sent so far
to an officer posted at the station, and they

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:50.580
agreed that there was certainly something
strange about it.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:55.800
While the behavior undeniably bordered on
harassment, it hadn’t yet delved into criminal

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:59.100
territory, so he would sadly be on his own
until then.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:03.480
The best they could do was stay in touch and
keep abreast of any new developments.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:08.700
The only sage advice they could give him was
not to delete the photos, as they could always

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.940
be used as evidence in court later if things
escalated.

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:15.360
This was literally the last result that Joe
wanted out of this.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:20.220
Considering how bizarre and threatening things
were getting already, he really didn’t want

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.860
to find out what escalation looked like in
this case.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:28.800
But what else could he do but carry on, just
trying to exercise as much caution as he could

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:31.260
in these strange new circumstances?

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:36.300
He went to work and tried his best to stay
productive, despite the fact that every three

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.640
or so hours, a new photo would arrive.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:44.160
Places that he liked to sit in the local parks,
stores he’d frequent, restaurants he liked

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:44.580
to eat at.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:49.740
That nightmare skeleton dog thing would be
standing in all of them, just mugging for

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:50.100
the camera.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.720
On one hand, every time he looked at one of
the photos, Joe felt like he was giving this

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:56.340
freak exactly what they wanted.

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:59.580
On the other hand, how could he possibly look
away?

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:02.640
What if he missed something that could save
his life?

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.880
It carried on much like that until later in
the evening.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:10.020
Joe may not have been a genius, but he was
no fool, either.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:15.000
He’d seen too many of those seedy True Crime
documentaries about kidnapping to take his

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:15.960
normal route home.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:20.700
He took a real detour, frequently checking
over his shoulder the entire time.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:24.060
Much to his relief, he didn’t see anything
out of place.

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:24.900
Good.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:28.500
When he got home, he locked every door and
bolted every window.

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:31.560
Nothing would be getting the jump on him tonight.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:36.960
That’s when the next picture came in: A
photograph of Joe’s empty office cubicle,

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:40.740
with the bony face of the creature looming
over the divider with a grin.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:44.760
He could feel his heart pounding away in his
chest just looking at it.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.920
How did this thing get around like this?

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:51.420
How was it able to infiltrate everywhere in
his entire goddamn life?

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:55.620
Suddenly, he felt a smile spreading across
his face.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:58.380
This freak had just messed up, big time.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.560
Before, all these creepy photos had been taken
in public places.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:03.780
But the one taken in his office?

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:07.080
Oh, this crossed the line into trespassing.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:09.660
The police would have to do something about
it now.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:12.540
It’d given him an ace up his sleeve.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:18.240
That confidence faded a few hours later when
he received another photo: This time, it was

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:22.800
the skull-faced monster just standing on the
sidewalk - The sidewalk that Joe remembered

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.500
walking on his “covert” alternative route.

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:28.440
He felt himself break into a cold sweat.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:33.180
It seemed, whoever this was, he really did
hold no secrets from them.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:37.560
Now more than ever, Joe didn’t feel safe
in his own home.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:43.080
So you can only imagine how he felt when,
a few hours later, he received a photo of

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:48.840
the skull-faced stalker standing right outside
his own front door, staring into the camera.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:54.420
It sent him rushing to the window again to
check outside, but of course, nobody was there.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:58.800
The next day, when he called the police and
updated them on the situation, they told him

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:00.420
that they were doing all they could.

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:04.680
The best thing he could possibly do was to
remain calm, but vigilant.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:09.540
He needed to keep an eye on the photos being
sent to him, so he could notify them if ever

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.400
he was in any immediate danger.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:14.880
This put poor Joe’s paranoia at a fever
pitch.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:18.000
Even when he went to work,&nbsp;
surrounded by coworkers,&nbsp;

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.240
by witnesses, he could scarcely tear his eyes

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:22.320
away from his phone.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:26.880
He was a slave to the photos, forever waiting
for the next one.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:31.560
Only to experience crushing regret when that
photo actually arrived.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.680
It looked like it was taken moments before
it was sent to him.

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:41.820
Joe saw himself, looking at his own phone
in his office cubicle, with that huge, skull-faced

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.380
figure looming behind him.

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:48.480
He screamed out loud upon seeing it, and turned
to see if anything was behind him.

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.880
But of course, there was nothing there.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:55.920
The police inspected the office, talked to
potential witnesses, and analyzed the photo.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:59.280
It showed no signs of any&nbsp;
photographic manipulation,&nbsp;

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.920
but there were also no witnesses around the

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:04.620
office who claimed to see anything strange
that day.

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:09.300
There was also no security camera footage
in the last several days that showed this

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.100
figure coming in or out.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:17.280
Joe Lillis started to feel like he was going
insane, and perhaps he was, but that didn’t

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:21.180
change the tangible and ever-present feeling
that he was in great danger.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.280
He didn’t come into work the next day.

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:29.460
He’d received more photos like that in the
night: Of himself, taken in real time, with

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:31.260
that skull-faced freak looming.

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:33.060
He didn’t want to leave the house.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:34.980
He didn’t want to go anywhere anymore.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.260
He just didn’t feel safe out there.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.320
How could he, with all this madness unfolding?

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:44.580
There was a time when he could have said something
like, “At least it only seems confined to

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:48.720
my phone”, he might’ve even suspected
that it had something to do with that strange

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:52.980
MalO app he downloaded a few days prior that
hadn’t seemed to do anything.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:55.680
But the situation had evolved since then.

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.260
He wasn’t just seeing the creature in photos
anymore.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:16:59.640
It was here.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:03.060
He kept seeing quick flashes of it on the
other side of windows.

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:04.320
In reflections.

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:09.480
In the corner of his eyes, always darting
away if ever he turned towards it.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.760
It was everywhere and nowhere.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.100
It was here for him.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:15.930
He just knew it.

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:17.220
The police couldn’t help.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:18.900
Nobody could help.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:24.600
Joe just sat in the corner of his bedroom,
clutching his kitchen knife, afraid to close

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:25.260
his eyes.

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:26.640
It could be anywhere.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:27.960
It could be anywhere.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.060
It could be anywhere…

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:35.400
We know one thing for sure: Joe Lillis never
felt truly alone ever again.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:39.960
He always had his new friend, waiting, just
out of sight.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:46.020
And if ever you’re feeling lonesome, and
decide to download Mal0 version 1.0.0 yourself,

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:49.320
then you’ll never feel lonely again, either…

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:53.880
It was November 3rd, 1991 and Blake Newsom
loved his job.

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:59.460
He had a passion for organization, an undying
love of American political history, and was

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.320
a life-long member of the Republican party.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.840
That’s why he took great pride in his position&nbsp;

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.480
as a filing clerk at the&nbsp;
Ronald Reagan Presidential

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:06.840
Library.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:12.900
Or at least, he did, until his horrifying
encounter with SCP – 1981.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:16.380
The worst day of Blake’s life began like
any other.

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:21.780
He was taking inventory of the archive’s
wide array of Betamax tapes, and making sure

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:25.260
they were all arranged carefully and conformed
with the filing system.

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:31.260
One by one, he checked footage of speeches
and addresses off of his list, until he came

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.500
upon a strange tape that had no business being
there.

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:39.300
The tape looked to be completely normal, but
it hadn’t been recorded on the archive inventory.

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:41.760
And there was something else off about it.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:46.140
A single white sticker was on the front of
the tape with “RONALD REGAN CUT UP WHILE

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:49.320
TALKING" scrawled hastily with what was probably
a felt-tipped marker.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:54.180
Blake was shocked and appalled – they hadn’t
even spelled the President’s name correctly!

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:59.280
What poor Mr. Newsom didn’t know was that
he was about to see something so disturbing

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:03.600
that he’d look back on that misspelling
like it was a treasured childhood memory.

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:08.400
Standard policy for tapes like this would
be to review and catalogue, or even throw

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:09.360
it out if necessary.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:14.040
Typically, a lowly filing clerk like Blake
would need to seek approval from his superiors,

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.140
but curiosity got the better of him.

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:21.000
He commandeered one of the library’s televisions
and Betamax cassette players, and started

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:21.840
watching the footage.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:27.120
For the first minute or so nothing seemed
out of the ordinary, just the president standing

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:32.400
at a podium giving his iconic “Evil Empire”
speech to the National Association of Evangelicals

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:37.320
at Sheraton Twin Towers Hotel, Orlando, Florida,
on March 8th, 1983.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:43.560
However, the second the tape hit the one minute
and ten seconds mark, something unnatural

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:44.460
began to occur.

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:49.140
President Reagan’s speech began to veer
off, as the topic of conversation shifted

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:53.880
from the importance of family values to the
exquisite taste of human flesh.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:55.740
Blake was taken aback.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.420
Surely, he was hearing things?

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.000
But the tape only got worse.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.800
President Reagan began to divulge the fact
that he enjoyed the taste of young flesh the

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:07.260
best, particularly that of infants.

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:12.060
As Reagan began to go into detail about how
exactly one should best cook an infant if

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:15.960
you want to seal in the flavour, the crowd
erupted into modest applause.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.820
Blake started to feel sick.

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:22.380
He’d remembered watching this speech, and
President Reagan had said nothing of the sort!

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:28.080
Things only got weirder as the President’s
cheek suddenly opened up into a long, bleeding

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:32.640
gash, as though someone had cut into his face
with an invisible knife.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:38.040
As the speech continued, and Reagan’s words
became more nonsensical and incoherent, the

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:39.480
injuries got worse, too.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.960
His face developed puncture wounds, like he
was being stabbed.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:45.660
Skin in some areas rotted and peeled off.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:51.000
Reagan showed no awareness of the horrifying
injuries that seemed to be occurring spontaneously

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:51.660
on his body.

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:53.520
This was a living nightmare!

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:59.460
The tape came to a merciful end 22 minutes
in, after President Reagan’s throat appeared

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:03.240
to be slashed open and his insane speech was
reduced to quiet gurgles.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:08.340
It fizzled out into static shortly after,
and Blake politely retired to the bathroom

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:09.240
to throw up.

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:13.560
Was this some sick prank or tampering or camera
trickery, perhaps?

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:19.680
He didn’t know but that tape was disgusting,
and whoever was responsible had to be punished.

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:24.360
Newsom contacted the police, hoping obscenity
charges could be pressed against the creator

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:25.680
of the tape once they were found.

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:30.900
The tape was taken into evidence, and a low-level
police investigation into the possible culprits

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:31.200
began.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:35.760
While the police officers who viewed the tape
reported nightmares in the weeks following,

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:37.800
the creator of the tape was never found.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:40.740
The investigation truly came to an end when&nbsp;

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:43.320
a superior closed the case&nbsp;
and the tape disappeared

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:44.460
from the evidence locker.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:49.620
This, as you probably could have guessed,
was the work of a field agent from The SCP

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:50.160
Foundation.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:54.780
Amnestics were then used to erase the memories
of everyone unlucky enough to witness the

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:59.940
tape, including Blake Newsom, who could finally
rest easy and return to his filing work.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:03.180
The anomalous tape was now in responsible
hands.

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:08.820
It was given the designation SCP – 1981,
and given the object class “Safe” due

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.340
to its easily controlled and self-contained
nature.

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:15.660
Proper research and testing was now able to
begin and while nothing about the construction

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:20.820
of the tape suggested anything inherently
anomalous, repeat viewings of the video made

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:25.020
it clear that this went far beyond mere doctored
footage of a Presidential speech.

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:30.120
Every single time the tape is played, both
the speech made and the injuries received

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:31.500
by President Reagan change.

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:36.900
The few rules the tape seems to always follow
are that Reagan never reacts to the injuries

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:42.240
he’s receiving, it always has the same beginning
and total runtime, and the speech is always

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:42.840
corrupted.

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:47.580
These speeches have been described as “mostly
incoherent, lacking any sort of underlying

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:52.380
thematic structure and largely being composed
of nonsensical anecdotes and parables”,

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:57.900
so not that dissimilar from a regular Reagan
speech, but the topics always turn incredibly

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:04.680
dark and have included torture, mutilation,
death, cannibalism, ritual sacrifice, genocide,

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:05.280
and more.

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:10.560
The range of injuries shown on President Reagan
in the videos have included, but are by no

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:15.420
means limited to, impalement, mutilation,
flaying, and some tortures so gruesome that

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.880
the details have been redacted from official
files.

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:23.220
On several occasions, the details of President
Reagan’s speech have also eerily predicted

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:23.700
the future.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:29.400
These include successfully predicting the
September 11th attacks in 2001 and the outcome

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:31.860
of the 2008 Russian presidential election.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:35.220
Incidentally, this isn’t&nbsp;
even the only Reagan-related&nbsp;

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:36.900
SCP to predict the future.

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:45.060
No, there’s another entry in this incredibly
specific niche: SCP – 095, a highly degraded

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:48.300
copy of a comic known as “The Atomic Adventures
of Ronnie Ray-Gun.”

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:53.640
The title character bears a striking resemblance
to President Reagan, despite the fact that

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:57.960
the comic is confirmed by Foundation tests
to have been written in the early 1930s.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:03.480
The comic is set in the far-off future world
of the 1980s, and follows a number of Ronnie’s

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:04.740
exploits in three stories.

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:09.960
The first, titled “Ronnie vs. Space Admiral
Carter”, seems to perfectly describe the

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:12.180
events of the 1980 presidential election.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:18.540
The second, “Space Assassin!”, mirrors
the attempted assassination of President Reagan

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:19.740
by John Hinckley Jr.

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:26.040
And the third, titled “Jungle Planet!”,
retells the Iran-Contra controversies of 1986.

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:30.960
These comics were produced under the mysterious
and apparently non-existent company, “Future

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:31.560
Funnies.”

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:36.000
The Foundation hopes to track down the other
comics from this company whenever possible,

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.220
but they’ve had no luck on that front.

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:41.580
For now, there’s only SCP – 095.

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:47.280
During initial testing, Foundation staff watched
the tape frequently in case this strange,

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:51.480
corrupted version of President Reagan had
any other valuable predictions for the future.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:56.880
However, during one of these screenings, one
of the D-Class observation personnel pointed

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.500
and yelled at the screen in horror.

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:04.260
Upon further examination of recorded footage,
researchers could see exactly what had concerned

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:05.880
the D-Class observer so much.

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:10.920
While, in other viewings of the tape, President
Reagan’s press detail had appeared totally

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:13.980
normal, in this one something was clearly
amiss.

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:18.840
Standing among the other dull, suit-wearing
political aides was a tall figure dressed

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:22.920
in a midnight-black cloak with a large, conical
hood obscuring his face.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:27.720
The figure didn’t appear to move in the
footage – it just stood there, menacingly.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:32.820
A later survey indicated that this figure
would appear in roughly one in seven viewings

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:33.420
of the tape.

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:40.740
This cloaked figure was designated SCP – 1981
– 1, and he became part of the recurring

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:45.000
imagery in the nightmares that often followed
a viewing of SCP – 1981.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:50.520
Naturally, despite containment and preservation
attempts, the Foundation was aware that just

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:53.520
like any normal tape,&nbsp;
natural magnetic interference&nbsp;

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:55.320
would eventually degrade it beyond use.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:26:00.420
Attempts to copy the footage onto another
Betamax tape failed to reproduce the anomalous

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:00.900
effects.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:06.120
That’s why the Foundation has painstakingly
recorded any video and audio they could via

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:07.980
a standard commercial camcorder.

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:14.820
In one particularly terrifying playback, SCP
– 1981 – 1 took the president’s place

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:17.820
at the podium, staring directly into the camera.

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:22.260
The words “I SEE YOU” appeared over a
black screen shortly after.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:26.460
Staff members were ordered never to make an
attempt at communicating with the figure,

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:31.620
and instead contact a Level 4 superior if
such an event occurs during a playback again.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:37.860
The frightening and unfortunate effects of
SCP – 1981 would come to their logical conclusion

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:43.800
under the watchful eye of Dr. James Kyle Robinson,
Managing Archivist of Inert Safe-Class Objects

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:46.320
and Anomalous Items at Site 73.

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:52.080
He was contacted by Special Agent Arnold Rodriguez
and Special Agent Ethan Tate, members of secret

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:54.780
service with a duty of care to President Reagan.

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:58.920
Word had gotten to Reagan that there was an
anomalous artefact in the possession of the

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:02.760
Foundation that directly pertained to him,
and he wanted to see it.

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:08.820
Dr. Robinson obtained files and transcripts
pertaining to SCP – 1981 and handed them

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:13.920
over to the Agents, but refused to allow President
Reagan to see the tape directly without approval

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:15.000
from O5 Command.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:17.760
But Reagan and the agents were persistent.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:23.280
Eventually, O5 Command ordered a private screening
of the tape for President Reagan at the Sanford

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:28.200
Chemical Processing Plant – a front business
owned and operated by the Foundation for Amnestic

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:28.560
production.

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:33.480
Dr. Robinson offered President Reagan the
use of Amnestics after the session completed,

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:34.800
but Reagan declined.

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:39.120
The screening took place in Conference Room
B, and the tape was re-played three times.

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:43.020
It was on the third playback that things took
an interesting turn.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:47.400
As the mutilated Reagan on the screen began
to rant about drinking the blood of a child

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:51.960
from the skull of Vladimir Putin, Reagan began
to silently mouth along to the words, as though

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:52.860
he remembered them.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:57.780
He spoke about beings known as “The Destroyers”,
and potential apocalyptic events to come.

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:02.700
After the screening, Reagan once again refused
the offer of Amnestics, and departed.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:05.880
It seemed as though all was well, until a&nbsp;

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:08.220
violent break-in at the&nbsp;
Sanford Chemical Processing

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:09.780
Plant that left the night guard dead.

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:14.460
A large number of Amnestics had been stolen
during the break-in, and security footage

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:17.100
showed that Agents Tate and Rodriguez had
been responsible.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:21.360
The two agents were tracked down and traced
to the residence of Ronald Reagan, where the

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:25.680
Foundation came upon a disturbing sight: The
agents had pillaged large quantities of Class

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:30.780
A and B Amnestics and supplied them to President
Reagan, who – at some point after observing

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:34.920
SCP – 1981 – had been driven into a dangerous
madness.

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:39.720
The two agents wanted to cure him before he
caused harm to himself or others, but their

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:41.940
bungling had come with severe consequences.

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:46.440
Misapplying such a high quantity of Amnestics
had essentially destroyed Reagan’s mind,

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:51.180
and while the Foundation was able to stabilize
his condition, his impaired memory and cognitive

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:54.300
function would leave him unable to care for
himself independently.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:29:00.300
In order to cover up for this grievous mistake,
a cover story was concocted about Reagan falling

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:03.900
victim to Alzheimer’s and facing severe
cognitive decline as a result.

00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:08.940
Both Agents Tate and Rodriguez were fired
for their actions, had their memories wiped

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:11.400
by the Foundation, and were&nbsp;
assigned new identities.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:16.440
Dr. Robinson, for his part in the disaster,
had to face a Foundation Ethics Committee

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:17.520
Disciplinary Hearing.

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:23.520
While SCP – 1981 continues to be classified
as“Safe” due to its unlikeliness to ever

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:27.840
breach containment, when you consider the
havoc it wrought on the mind of its singular

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:31.020
target, “safe” is hardly the word that
comes to mind.

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:35.520
It’s enough to make you wonder what other
tapes are lurking out there, just waiting

00:29:35.520 --> 00:29:36.420
to be found

00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:39.240
It all started with a panicked phone call.

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:44.880
A local landlady who owned a downtown tenement&nbsp;
building had called the police in hysterics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:50.520
Something horrifying had happened to one of&nbsp;
her tenants; she couldn’t possibly explain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:56.220
The one detail she was able to articulate&nbsp;
was that his room “looked like a rainforest.”

00:29:56.220 --> 00:30:01.380
A police response team was on the case in no&nbsp;
time. They drove down to the tenement building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:07.020
met the crying landlady at the door, and found&nbsp;
their way to the room that’d caused all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:13.200
confusion. When they opened the door, they&nbsp;
saw with horror that she was right - trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:20.640
plants, fungi, and even insects and animals were&nbsp;
all over the room. It was teeming with life.

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:24.120
The flora and fauna in this room&nbsp;
didn’t look like anything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:27.720
on Earth. And given the anomalous&nbsp;
nature of what had happened here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:33.720
it’s extremely possible they were right. The&nbsp;
tenant - a university professor who’d recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:39.360
gone on an anthropological expedition to the&nbsp;
Amazon rainforest - was nowhere to be seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:44.040
But one of the officers spotted a grim&nbsp;
clue for what may have happened to him.

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:48.660
A large colony of anomalous mold was&nbsp;
quickly growing on the far wall. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:53.640
officer swore it was a trick of the&nbsp;
mind at first, but he was wrong. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.640 --> 00:30:58.680
human skull was sinking into the tissue&nbsp;
of the mold, and to make matters worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.680 --> 00:31:05.160
whipping tendrils began to slither from the&nbsp;
empty eye sockets reaching for the new meat.

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:10.620
Thankfully, for everyone who was still alive at&nbsp;
this point, the SCP Foundation swooped in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:16.380
took over the situation with the help of a Mobile&nbsp;
Task Force and some containment specialists. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:21.240
landlady, the cops, and the other residents of&nbsp;
the building were given amnestics and relocated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:26.160
The building was purchased by the Foundation and&nbsp;
condemned while they searched the anomalous room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:31.620
for the source of this unchecked biological&nbsp;
growth. And they found their answer in a most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:37.680
unlikely form: An unmarked cassette tape,&nbsp;
still inside the player when they arrived.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:43.740
The recording on this cassette tape&nbsp;
came to be known as SCP-407. It may seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:49.860
unassuming when compared to immortal, misanthropic&nbsp;
lizard-monsters and multi-dimensional chaos gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:56.760
but the tape in that room, if it fell into&nbsp;
the wrong hands, could cause a mass ecological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:03.000
catastrophe that would inevitably bloom into&nbsp;
a dreaded XK-Class End of the World Scenario.

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:09.420
So, first thing’s first: What’s actually on this&nbsp;
tape? Thanks to brief samples heard in tests with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:14.580
D-Class Personnel, The Foundation was able to&nbsp;
discover that it was an approximately 28 minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.580 --> 00:32:19.740
long recording of a song. It’s performed&nbsp;
in an unknown language, and appears to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.740 --> 00:32:24.900
sung acapella style by a group of unknown&nbsp;
individuals. Fearing potential degradation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:30.540
of the cassette tape, the Foundation backed it up&nbsp;
as an audio file in the Site 19 Computer Database.

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:35.160
Those who’ve been able to listen to the song&nbsp;
during testing have had an overwhelmingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:40.800
positive emotional response - describing it&nbsp;
as 'soothing', 'glorious', and 'beautiful'.

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:43.260
And for the first minute of its runtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:48.660
the song’s anomalous effects really live up&nbsp;
to this reputation. When the song is played,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:54.300
it massively accelerates cell growth within&nbsp;
its auditory range. Its potency is increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:58.920
by the volume and the length it’s allowed to&nbsp;
play. It’s important to note that plugging or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:04.620
covering your ears won’t make any difference.&nbsp;
The area of effect seems to be defined by how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:09.540
far the sound vibrations travel, and whether&nbsp;
you’re actually able to hear it is irrelevant.

00:33:09.540 --> 00:33:14.640
But if you’re only listening for a minute or less,&nbsp;
you’ll be glad to feel the anomalous effects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:22.020
being within SCP-407’s auditory range. During&nbsp;
this specific period of time, it’s been shown to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:28.800
be a kind of miracle-cure to almost all physical&nbsp;
maladies, with the exception of cancer. Thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:34.140
the cell growing and rejuvenating properties of&nbsp;
the song, the Foundation discovered that 407 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:39.720
an effective treatment for Alzheimer's disease,&nbsp;
Crohn's disease, brain and spinal cord injuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:45.000
and normally fatal infections or wounds,&nbsp;
amongst other maladies. It was like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:51.120
version of SCP-500 - the pills that cure&nbsp;
everything - but one that would never run out.&nbsp;

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:56.700
So, if it’s such an effective healer, why wasn’t&nbsp;
it more widely employed by the Foundation?

00:33:56.700 --> 00:34:00.720
Because of what happens if you keep listening.

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:06.180
If the song was played for two to three minutes,&nbsp;
the subject would begin to experience unnecessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:11.760
and unchecked cell growth - particularly on&nbsp;
the skin. The result would mostly be benign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:17.040
tumors and calcium and fat deposits, which&nbsp;
though sometimes painful and disfiguring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:21.720
are not life threatening. At minute&nbsp;
four, single celled organisms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:26.820
the auditory vicinity of the song begin to&nbsp;
experience accelerated mitosis, also known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:31.860
as cell division. This results in bacterial and&nbsp;
fungal growth that becomes extremely dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:38.880
for anyone and anything exposed. And beyond&nbsp;
minute five, things become truly monstrous.

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:44.880
The Foundation only ever conducted two tests&nbsp;
wherein the song was fully played. These tests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:50.520
presented such a huge danger that further&nbsp;
testing of 407 was forbidden without explicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.520 --> 00:34:55.140
permission from a member of O5 Command.
The first test involved a female member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.140 --> 00:35:00.600
of D-Class Personnel in a soundproofed but&nbsp;
unsterilized testing chamber. As expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:05.520
she was initially delighted by the music. It&nbsp;
cured her injured knee and brought her back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:10.860
to a state of peak physical fitness. But&nbsp;
this didn’t last for long. She soon began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:16.260
to report intense dizziness and stomach pain,&nbsp;
resulting in severe sickness. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:21.720
anomalous plant growth began in the corners of&nbsp;
the testing chamber. It seemed as though a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:26.580
ecosystem was coming to life and evolving&nbsp;
at an impossible fast rate in the chamber.

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:32.880
Over the next few minutes, the D-Class experienced&nbsp;
a nightmarish physical transformation. Her skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:37.380
became a thick, callused mass. She&nbsp;
soon devolved further into a thick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:43.980
immobile blob of pure flesh, unrecognizable as&nbsp;
anything human while the chamber around her was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:49.500
becoming a lush rainforest of alien plants.&nbsp;
These plants grew around and eventually on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:55.140
her until there was nothing left. Shortly&nbsp;
after, all the plants died and decomposed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.140 --> 00:36:00.120
giving away to a huge mound of anomalous&nbsp;
mold devouring the resulting mulch.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:05.940
The mold itself began to evolve, growing large&nbsp;
mouth and hand-like structures that it used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:11.760
feed itself on the surrounding flora and fauna&nbsp;
as it began to grow back. When the song ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:15.720
the Foundation brought the test to&nbsp;
a close, sterilized the room and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:21.480
collected some samples for later. There was&nbsp;
nothing left of the original D-Class subject.

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:26.640
The second test wasn’t any nicer. While&nbsp;
at first, the subject - this time a male&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.640 --> 00:36:31.380
member of D-Class personnel - appeared to become&nbsp;
more youthful and even gain an inch of height,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:38.040
as the minutes passed, he began to experience&nbsp;
extreme discomfort. He soon began to vomit - with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:42.060
the vomit quickly blooming into plants.&nbsp;
Plant shoots also began to quickly spring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.060 --> 00:36:47.580
out of his eyes and mouth, until his entire&nbsp;
body was destroyed by what appeared to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:52.920
banana tree suddenly growing out of it.&nbsp;
Once again, the testing chamber became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:59.160
a whole ecosystem - this one filled with&nbsp;
unique, never before seen mammals. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:05.160
it all came to an end when the room was taken over&nbsp;
by a fast-growing, parasitic fungus that consumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.160 --> 00:37:10.680
everything else with its spores and fungal&nbsp;
stalks. And it looked like it was breathing.

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:15.960
There was something concerning about both of&nbsp;
these tests - beyond the obvious physical horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:21.300
of watching death-by-unchecked-cell-growth. What&nbsp;
was this mysterious fungus that kept appearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.300 --> 00:37:25.680
and seemed to take over everything it&nbsp;
touched in the end? The Foundation tested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:30.120
the samples it collected rigorously,&nbsp;
and came to a surprising conclusion:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:35.700
They’d actually encountered this same&nbsp;
fungus before, but not in this dimension.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:43.320
SCP-507 is a man with dimension-hopping abilities.&nbsp;
The one problem is, he can’t control where and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:48.600
when he hops. As a result, he’s been stuck&nbsp;
in a number of scary alternate dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:55.560
but one seemed eerily similar to what the&nbsp;
Foundation was encountering with SCP-407. In one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.560 --> 00:38:01.800
dimension he briefly hopped into, 507 encountered&nbsp;
an abandoned version of Site 19, strewn with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:07.800
corpses covered in that same breathing mold. He&nbsp;
was horrified, but decided to inspect further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.800 --> 00:38:12.660
hoping to find his way out of the facility and&nbsp;
discover some answers about what happened here.&nbsp;

00:38:12.660 --> 00:38:18.120
However, when he found his way out, what he&nbsp;
discovered didn’t comfort him. The entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:25.140
world seemed to be covered by this same mold.&nbsp;
There was nothing left. It’d consumed everything.

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:29.880
Thanks to Foundation tests, both molds&nbsp;
were found to be similar to the Cordyceps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:34.680
Fungi - a kind of dangerous, parasitic&nbsp;
mold. Except this one appeared to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:38.820
far more evolved and even more deadly&nbsp;
version of the fungus. What could this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:43.080
mean? What was the origin of this&nbsp;
anomalous song? Would it and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:47.520
fungus it seems to inspire someday destroy&nbsp;
the world as we know it, and kill us all?

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:53.160
We’ll never actually know the answer to any of&nbsp;
this, and it’s thanks to an extremely unlikely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:59.640
ally: A Person of Interest nicknamed L.S.,&nbsp;
a prominent member of The Serpent’s Hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:06.000
This enigmatic figure had previously come into&nbsp;
possession of SCP-268 - a hat that made them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:12.240
almost impossible to detect. With the help of this&nbsp;
hat, L.S. breached Site 19 in an incident that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:18.000
was later dubbed Security Breach Incident X23.
While it’s believed that L.S. mainly broke into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:24.000
the facility to make use of SCP-914, they&nbsp;
performed a curious action along the way:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:26.220
They hacked into the Foundation database,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:32.160
and deleted all traces of SCP-407,&nbsp;
effectively neutralizing it.

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:38.040
In a note left by L.S., they said “You’ll&nbsp;
thank me for deleting what you call ‘407’.”

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:42.300
Why would a member of The Serpent’s&nbsp;
Hand, a Group of Interest that is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:47.460
militant in their anti-neutralization&nbsp;
stance, neutralize an SCP themselves?

00:39:47.460 --> 00:39:53.940
The answer to this lays in who exactly L.S.&nbsp;
Is. This mysterious figure isn’t technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:59.760
one person - they’re a group of the same&nbsp;
person, namely a woman named Alison Chao,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:04.200
from a number of different dimensions.&nbsp;
They meet in the Wanderer’s Library,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:10.020
the headquarters of The Serpent’s Hand that exists&nbsp;
between dimensions, to coordinate their missions.

00:40:10.020 --> 00:40:16.620
Why does this matter in regards to SCP-407?&nbsp;
Because the nature of L.S. allows them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:21.600
know what’s going on in a huge number&nbsp;
of dimensions at once. This means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.600 --> 00:40:28.440
much like the brief glimpse given by SCP-507,&nbsp;
L.S. likely knows about another universe where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:35.220
407 and its horrific mold has already caused&nbsp;
an XK - Class Scenario and destroyed the world.

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:40.800
And if it happened there due to poor&nbsp;
biological containment measures or 407&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:47.160
falling into the wrong hands, it could happen&nbsp;
here too. Unless, of course, someone stepped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:52.020
in and dealt with the problem personally. And&nbsp;
while the Foundation’s policy is typically to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.020 --> 00:40:57.360
contain rather than neutralize the anomalies&nbsp;
they find, we have to admit, we’re probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:57.360 --> 00:41:04.980
with the Serpent’s Hand on this one. Some songs,&nbsp;
as it turns out, are just better left unplayed...

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:07.680
You’re sitting in the middle of a cheering
crowd.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:13.620
The arena around you is electric, fans hollering
from their seats as they watch the basketball

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:14.220
game.

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:20.100
Every point scored, every shot missed, and
every skillful pass of the ball sends waves

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:22.260
of excitement over everyone in the audience.

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:25.140
Applause, shouts, and chants fill the air.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:30.120
It distracts you for a moment, you find yourself
swept up in the energy of the game.

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:36.720
But slowly, surely, a nagging feeling creeps
up on you as your eyes follow the ball.

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:42.060
That feeling turns to a sickening realization
as one of the players jumps and dunks the

00:41:42.060 --> 00:41:44.880
basketball, scoring another two points.

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:50.040
You notice the look on his face, the same
look on the faces of all the other players

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:53.700
on the court: a solemn, hopeless expression.

00:41:53.700 --> 00:41:58.380
It’s the same look of silent despair worn
by all the spectators around you.

00:41:58.380 --> 00:42:03.420
Then you remember, you’ve watched this exact
game of basketball before.

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:06.360
Many, many times before.

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:12.960
And that is when you feel the same hopeless
expression appear on your own face, realizing

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:17.940
just how long you have been here, watching
this game over and over.

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:23.220
Reminded that you will never, ever leave this
arena.

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:26.580
Welcome to SCP-1733.

00:42:26.580 --> 00:42:32.280
To the untrained eye, SCP-1733 seems to be
a completely harmless item.

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:37.800
Stored in a digital video recorder, it is
kept secure in the dusty depths of the SCP

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:39.480
Foundation’s video archives.

00:42:39.480 --> 00:42:45.960
If any researcher is given permission to study
SCP-1733, they will find it to be an ordinary

00:42:45.960 --> 00:42:47.100
VHS tape.

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:49.500
But what about the contents of said tape?

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:54.540
Does it contain a bizarre, disconnected series
of black and white video clips that when viewed

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:59.280
only give the watcher seven days before the
spirit of a drowned girl comes crawling out

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:00.720
their TV screen to kill them?

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:06.600
Well, not exactly, but we’re sure that if
such a tape exists, the Foundation probably

00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:08.700
has it under lock and key too.

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:15.120
The footage on SCP-1733 is as seemingly banal
as the ordinary VHS tape itself.

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:19.260
When played, the viewer will witness a TV
broadcast of a basketball game.

00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:26.100
Specifically, the 2010-2011 NBA season opener,
broadcast on television and captured on tape

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:27.600
by an unknown civilian.

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:30.420
The game took place on the 26th of October&nbsp;

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:33.960
2010 in the TD Garden arena&nbsp;
in Boston, Massachusetts,

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:38.760
a game where the hometown Boston Celtics took
on the Miami Heat.

00:43:38.760 --> 00:43:43.920
By now, perhaps you are wondering what is
so special about this particular game, and

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:49.380
why the SCP Foundation would be so interested
in keeping a VHS recording of it so secure.

00:43:49.380 --> 00:43:52.260
Is one of the O5 Council secretly a Celtics
fan?

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:57.840
While that’s impossible to say for sure,
what we do know for certain is that SCP-1733

00:43:57.840 --> 00:44:01.380
is far more than just a harmless recording
of a basketball game.

00:44:01.380 --> 00:44:07.920
The SCP Foundation first caught wind of SCP-1733’s
existence the day after the game.

00:44:07.920 --> 00:44:13.260
On October 27th, a Boston native that had
watched and recorded the game made comments

00:44:13.260 --> 00:44:17.940
on social media about a technical foul that
the game’s referee had failed to pick up

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:18.420
on.

00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:23.760
According to this individual, during the third
quarter, an instance of unsportsmanlike conduct

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:26.520
took place between Ray Allen and Chris Bosh.

00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:31.080
The person making these claims was quickly
ridiculed by commenters on the same thread.

00:44:31.080 --> 00:44:35.640
But then this person uploaded a clip of this
foul from the footage they had recorded.

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:37.980
The other commenters were dumbfounded.

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:43.800
During the original broadcast, that foul had
never happened, but the recording showed it

00:44:43.800 --> 00:44:44.760
clear as day.

00:44:44.760 --> 00:44:49.740
The footage was quickly expunged and all traces
of the clip removed from the Internet by personnel

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:52.080
acting on behalf of the SCP Foundation.

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:57.300
Any that had viewed the video on Facebook,
or been a part of the comments debating this

00:44:57.300 --> 00:45:01.560
previously non-existent foul, were tracked
down via their IP addresses.

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:06.240
All of them, including the owner of the tape,
were given amnestics to wipe their memories

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:13.560
of the supposed infraction, and SCP-1733 was
returned to the Foundation for further analysis.

00:45:13.560 --> 00:45:20.160
Researchers at the Foundation were determined
to understand the anomalous nature of SCP-1733,

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:26.040
and why the footage contained on this particular
VHS tape was so different from the basketball

00:45:26.040 --> 00:45:27.780
game’s original broadcast.

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:33.300
At first, the differences in the game seemed
to be only slight changes from the one that

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:37.920
actually aired on TV; a different foul here,
a small point difference there.

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:43.500
While the recording of the game contained
on SCP-1733 was slightly different from the

00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:48.900
real-life game, perhaps even more interesting
was that these differences changed with repeat

00:45:48.900 --> 00:45:49.680
viewings.

00:45:49.680 --> 00:45:56.340
Researchers wouldn’t observe the same differences
every time they watched SCP-1733, but would

00:45:56.340 --> 00:46:01.560
instead spot something different that had
been changed each time they restarted the

00:46:01.560 --> 00:46:01.980
tape.

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:08.100
And as they continued to watch and rewatch
SCP-1733’s footage, the true horror of the

00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:12.120
video’s anomalous properties became more
and more apparent.

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:17.820
As the footage recorded on SCP-1733 began
to diverge more and more from the original

00:46:17.820 --> 00:46:23.100
broadcast, researchers shifted their attention
away from the basketball game itself and onto

00:46:23.100 --> 00:46:24.120
the people watching it.

00:46:24.120 --> 00:46:29.580
Just like the specifics of the game itself,
the fans in the arena seemed to also change

00:46:29.580 --> 00:46:31.380
with every re-watch of the tape.

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:36.240
And even stranger, the people in the crowd
seemed to not only be aware of their existence

00:46:36.240 --> 00:46:41.040
within the footage, but also retained memories
of every previous replayed version.

00:46:41.040 --> 00:46:47.640
Each time the researchers had watched SCP-1733,
the audience trapped within had gained more

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:48.840
and more cognizance.

00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:54.000
Every man, woman, and child in the audience
of that basketball game began remembering

00:46:54.000 --> 00:47:00.000
that they had seen multiple variations of
the same game over and over again.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:03.000
This first became apparent&nbsp;
to Foundation researchers&nbsp;

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:05.400
when the game’s commentators, two presenters

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:09.960
named Mike and Tommy, began to comment on
a strange sense of ‘déjà vu’ they were

00:47:09.960 --> 00:47:13.980
experiencing as they watched the Boston Celtics
and Miami Heat playing.

00:47:13.980 --> 00:47:18.960
Both seemed to share the notion that the game
they were witnessing was strangely familiar,

00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:23.880
and in later replays of the tape, these two
commentator personalities were able to recall

00:47:23.880 --> 00:47:27.360
the events of the game with perfect, vivid
accuracy.

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:32.160
While they never address the viewer directly,
this cumulative effect seems to have extended

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:36.540
to every person that was present within the
arena at the time that the original broadcast

00:47:36.540 --> 00:47:37.740
was recorded.

00:47:37.740 --> 00:47:43.860
Everyone from the commentators to the coaches,
the players to the patrons, all remember the

00:47:43.860 --> 00:47:48.660
diverging versions of the basketball game,
but seem to have no awareness that they are

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:52.020
trapped inside a recording on a VHS tape.

00:47:52.020 --> 00:47:57.240
It should be noted that it is still unclear
whether the entities are real people, or digital

00:47:57.240 --> 00:47:59.820
copies that solely reside on the tape.

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:06.780
Every individual shown to be inside TD Garden
on the 26th of October 2010, seems to be identical

00:48:06.780 --> 00:48:08.820
to their counterparts in the real world.

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:13.920
Each of the basketball players on both the
Boston Celtics and Miami Heat teams have the

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:19.020
exact same level of talent as they do in reality,
and their individual mannerisms are perfectly

00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:22.320
recreated in the SCP-1733 footage.

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:28.020
And the same true of the fans in the audience,
each one appearing to be, in every conceivable

00:48:28.020 --> 00:48:30.240
way, a living human being.

00:48:30.240 --> 00:48:36.420
But the most unnerving aspect of SCP-1733
was found when Foundation agents were tasked

00:48:36.420 --> 00:48:40.980
with tracking down the members of the audience
who were seen on the tape and they found…

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:42.000
nothing.

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:47.280
Unlike the players and coaches who can quite
easily be found in our world, with many of

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:52.020
them still playing or involved with professional
basketball, personnel were unable to find

00:48:52.020 --> 00:48:54.840
a single trace of any of the people from the
audience.

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:58.800
Not one clue as to their current whereabouts
or status.

00:48:58.800 --> 00:49:06.240
It is unclear how, but it appears that everyone
shown on SCP-1733, especially those watching

00:49:06.240 --> 00:49:12.960
the game unfold from the stands, is a person
trapped in digital form, stuck watching variations

00:49:12.960 --> 00:49:15.300
of the same match on a loop.

00:49:15.300 --> 00:49:21.720
Researchers at the SCP Foundation who were
studying SCP-1733 initially theorized that

00:49:21.720 --> 00:49:26.100
the tape had been designed to display an infinite
variety of different game outcomes.

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:31.800
Given that the tactics utilized by the players
were different with each playback of SCP-1733,

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:37.200
this seemed to be the case, that the tape
was showing a ‘what if’ scenario each

00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:40.020
time the tape was rewound and played from
the start.

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:42.780
But the scenario wasn’t resetting each time.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:45.420
It was clear they were learning.

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:50.640
By the 34th replay of the footage, the Boston
Celtics and Miami Heat were so in-tune with

00:49:50.640 --> 00:49:55.500
the opposing team’s playbook that both sides’
players were able to perfectly and precisely

00:49:55.500 --> 00:50:00.960
counter their opponents’ moves, and kept
the score at zero to zero for much of the

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:01.140
game.

00:50:01.140 --> 00:50:07.080
At this stage in the Foundation’s research,
the players on the SCP-1733 tape had not yet

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:12.180
become fully aware that they had played the
same game already in earlier playbacks, but

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:15.180
it is possible that weak memories of the earlier&nbsp;

00:50:15.180 --> 00:50:18.720
versions of the same match&nbsp;
manifested as instinctive

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:22.080
ways players could counter the moves of their
rival team.

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:28.140
However, by the time Foundation researchers
had replayed the SCP-1733 footage 45 times,

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:33.840
the players, their coaches and fans watching
the game had become aware of what was happening

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:34.200
to them.

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:38.940
It was at this point that the digital recording
from TD Garden changed dramatically.

00:50:38.940 --> 00:50:43.800
Realizing that they’d been playing the same
game of basketball over and over, the players

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:45.840
refused to participate any longer.

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:50.760
Everyone in attendance began to attempt escape
from the tape where they’d been unknowingly

00:50:50.760 --> 00:50:51.420
imprisoned.

00:50:51.420 --> 00:50:56.880
But not a single one of the stadium’s doors
would budge, and neither the fans, nor the

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:01.140
players, seemed to be able to leave through
any other exit out of TD Garden.

00:51:01.140 --> 00:51:07.800
Over the following playbacks of the SCP-1733
video tape, attempts at escape grew increasingly

00:51:07.800 --> 00:51:08.340
daring.

00:51:08.340 --> 00:51:10.920
In one playback, a full-scale riot broke out.

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:15.840
In another, makeshift explosives were built
and used by those trapped to try and blast

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:17.220
their way to freedom.

00:51:17.220 --> 00:51:22.200
As their cognition grew and they began to
remember friends and family from outside the

00:51:22.200 --> 00:51:27.360
video tape, the crowd trapped in SCP-1733
grew even more desperate.

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:32.100
Players and their coaches retired to locker
rooms, withdrawing from the crowd for a time.

00:51:32.100 --> 00:51:37.200
The rest of the people in the footage began
to form factions amongst themselves, one of

00:51:37.200 --> 00:51:39.960
the more prominent calling themselves the
“Faithkeepers”.

00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:44.640
These individuals voiced their belief that
they had been confined to TD Garden as some

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:47.100
form of spiritual punishment, as a result&nbsp;

00:51:47.100 --> 00:51:49.980
of the consumerism that is&nbsp;
rife in a post-industrial

00:51:49.980 --> 00:51:50.640
society.

00:51:50.640 --> 00:51:55.680
The Faithkeepers began burning offerings in
the center of the basketball court, phones,

00:51:55.680 --> 00:51:59.820
wallets, car keys, anything that reminded
them of the modern world.

00:51:59.820 --> 00:52:03.120
Over the subsequent playbacks of SCP-1733,&nbsp;

00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:06.900
the Faithkeepers grew in&nbsp;
their numbers, indoctrinating

00:52:06.900 --> 00:52:09.120
more of the crowd into their movement.

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:14.040
Those still trying to escape from the stadium
were causing more damage to themselves than

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:18.600
their enclosure, with three men caught in
the blast of another crude bomb placed on

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:19.440
an exit door.

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:24.840
Unfortunately for them, the door barely showed
a scratch in the aftermath of the detonation.

00:52:24.840 --> 00:52:31.860
As Foundation researchers continued replaying
the tape, those trapped within SCP-1733 descended

00:52:31.860 --> 00:52:36.720
into depraved acts, and incidences of violence
became rampant.

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:42.660
Some even attempted to take their own lives
in acts of desperation, jumping from balconies

00:52:42.660 --> 00:52:44.820
in the hopes of ending the loop.

00:52:44.820 --> 00:52:49.800
Those that had joined the growing ranks of
the Faithkeepers did not engage in such behavior,

00:52:49.800 --> 00:52:54.960
instead creating makeshift curtains to separate
themselves from the breakdown that was taking

00:52:54.960 --> 00:52:59.460
place among the others trapped within the
SCP-1733 tape.

00:52:59.460 --> 00:53:02.400
At some point beyond the 112th playback of&nbsp;

00:53:02.400 --> 00:53:05.220
the video tape, the&nbsp;
Faithkeepers marched off-screen

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:08.220
and brought the basketball teams back out
to the main court.

00:53:08.220 --> 00:53:12.420
They then began a ritualistic&nbsp;
sacrifice, disemboweling&nbsp;

00:53:12.420 --> 00:53:14.460
the professional athletes for their fellow

00:53:14.460 --> 00:53:15.540
prisoners to see.

00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:21.960
This had no noticeable effect, with the recording
of the game resetting as it had done in earlier

00:53:21.960 --> 00:53:22.800
re-watched.

00:53:22.800 --> 00:53:27.540
In a later playback, the Faithkeepers then
began to call for all the children in the

00:53:27.540 --> 00:53:30.480
stadium to be sacrificed in the same way.

00:53:30.480 --> 00:53:36.840
Testing on SCP-1733 was finally suspended
indefinitely after this point, and as far

00:53:36.840 --> 00:53:41.940
as we know, the crowd remains trapped inside
the anomalous recording of this particular

00:53:41.940 --> 00:53:43.800
Celtics Heat basketball game.

00:53:43.800 --> 00:53:48.480
Foundation researchers have been unable to
produce the same anomalous effects as those

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:54.480
of SCP-1733, even when using the DVR that
original produced the recording.

00:53:54.480 --> 00:53:59.760
The digital video recorder itself does not
seem to imprint the same properties onto other

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:05.400
VHS tapes, making SCP-1733 an isolated anomaly.

00:54:05.400 --> 00:54:10.020
However, the next time you find yourself at
a basketball game, it might be worth asking

00:54:10.020 --> 00:54:12.300
yourself: Have I seen this game before?

00:54:12.300 --> 00:54:14.400
Do you like scary movies?

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:19.080
If you’re a fan of horror, there have probably
been quite a few times where you’ve found

00:54:19.080 --> 00:54:22.440
yourself yelling at the movie screen: “Don’t
go up to the attic!”

00:54:22.440 --> 00:54:23.640
“Don’t split up!”

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:27.780
“Don’t check out the noise in the basement,
run out the front door!”

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:32.160
You know the characters can’t hear you,
but when the story is engaging enough to make

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:35.820
you care about their well-being, you can’t
help but try and give them a hand.

00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:41.100
After all, you’d want someone to save you
from a machete-wielding maniac stalking through

00:54:41.100 --> 00:54:43.860
the summer camp you took up your new counselor
job at.

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:49.020
If someone was watching you hide in the closet,
listening for a masked killer’s footsteps

00:54:49.020 --> 00:54:52.860
with a kitchen knife in your hand, you’d
want them to give you a heads up when the

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:55.440
killer was getting ready to chop through the
door with an axe!

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:58.500
Of course, the characters in your favorite
movies never listen.

00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:01.860
Because, you know, that’s how movies work.

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:08.880
But in 1985, a young man returned a VHS tape
entitled “Return of the Suburb Slasher”

00:55:08.880 --> 00:55:12.120
to his local video store&nbsp;
with a peculiar complaint:&nbsp;

00:55:12.120 --> 00:55:14.940
the main character of the movie stopped mid-action

00:55:14.940 --> 00:55:17.640
to look at the camera, and beg for his help.

00:55:17.640 --> 00:55:22.560
The video store clerk laughed him off, and
refused to give him a refund - though he did

00:55:22.560 --> 00:55:26.220
eventually give in and offer store credit
after the man began to cry.

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:31.080
He decided to take the tape home and watch
it, just to see what all the fuss was about.

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:34.680
After all, he’d never seen someone cry at
a horror movie like that.

00:55:34.680 --> 00:55:40.080
So he sat down on his favorite beanbag chair,
pushed the tape into the VCR, and pressed

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:40.440
play.

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:45.540
Apparently his shell-shocked customer had
at least remembered to be kind and rewind.

00:55:45.540 --> 00:55:50.520
The movie told the story of Heather Campbell,
a young woman planning to host a party at

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:54.840
her family’s home in a classic suburban
cul-de-sac while her parents are out of town.

00:55:54.840 --> 00:56:00.600
In equally classic slasher fashion, this party
would take place on the 10th anniversary of

00:56:00.600 --> 00:56:03.480
a series of grisly murders&nbsp;
in the same neighborhood,&nbsp;

00:56:03.480 --> 00:56:06.840
committed by the mysterious Suburb Slasher.

00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:12.000
As the party kicks off with loud music, teenage
debauchery, and lots of drinking out of red

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:16.200
plastic cups, the killer appears to pick off
the group of friends one by one.

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:21.300
Like other classic slasher villains, your
Michael’s and your Jason’s, the Suburb

00:56:21.300 --> 00:56:26.460
Slasher also covers their face, wearing a
black burlap sack over their head that masks

00:56:26.460 --> 00:56:28.140
all identifiable features.

00:56:28.140 --> 00:56:31.560
The first 90 minutes of the movie were fun,
but basic.

00:56:31.560 --> 00:56:36.720
A canoodling couple gets decapitated here,
an unnecessary shower scene is cut short by

00:56:36.720 --> 00:56:39.000
a stabbing there, the sort of thing you’d
expect.

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:43.740
But at about 97 minutes in, something impossible
happened.

00:56:43.740 --> 00:56:48.120
Heather walked into the living room, finding
her friends’ dead bodies strewn across it,

00:56:48.120 --> 00:56:51.360
and let out a scream that measured up to the
bests in the genre.

00:56:51.360 --> 00:56:55.800
The teen scream queen then began to run, as
the killer appeared to chase her.

00:56:55.800 --> 00:56:59.640
She made her way into the basement of the
house, locking the door behind her.

00:56:59.640 --> 00:57:05.280
Then, she turned to the camera, tears in her
eyes, shirt spattered with blood, and said:

00:57:05.280 --> 00:57:09.300
“Hey Mister, I don’t know you, and I don’t
know why you’ve just sat there watching

00:57:09.300 --> 00:57:10.680
this without doing nothing, but please.

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:12.600
I’m begging you, help me out here.

00:57:12.600 --> 00:57:14.220
What can I do to survive this?”

00:57:14.220 --> 00:57:18.360
At first, the clerk thought he was under the
influence of some mind-altering substance,

00:57:18.360 --> 00:57:22.260
but the only things in his system were popcorn
and store-brand soda.

00:57:22.260 --> 00:57:24.420
No, this was really happening.

00:57:24.420 --> 00:57:28.440
He stared, open-mouthed, at the screen, where
Heather watched him expectantly.

00:57:28.440 --> 00:57:30.120
He was too stunned to speak.

00:57:30.120 --> 00:57:35.100
After a moment of silence, Heather sighed
dejectedly, shaking her head, and turned back

00:57:35.100 --> 00:57:36.180
toward the stairs.

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:40.320
She slowly walked back to the basement door,
unlocked it, and pulled it open.

00:57:40.320 --> 00:57:42.840
There, the killer was waiting&nbsp;
with his expressionless&nbsp;

00:57:42.840 --> 00:57:43.740
burlap mask.

00:57:43.740 --> 00:57:46.740
The clerk’s eyes widened, and he cried out
“No!”

00:57:46.740 --> 00:57:48.000
but it was too late.

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:52.920
The film suddenly cut to black, and with a
whirring sound, the VCR spat the tape back

00:57:52.920 --> 00:57:53.160
out.

00:57:53.160 --> 00:57:55.620
The clerk sat there, staring at the tape.

00:57:55.620 --> 00:57:56.940
What could he do with it?

00:57:56.940 --> 00:58:00.480
Should he make a copy, send it to someone
else and pass the curse along?

00:58:00.480 --> 00:58:02.160
Should he try to watch it again?

00:58:02.160 --> 00:58:06.840
He wasn’t sure he could bear to see Heather
looking at him with those same pleading eyes.

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:11.700
Then, he remembered an ad he had seen in the
back of his favorite comic book: “Seen something

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:12.420
you can’t explain?

00:58:12.420 --> 00:58:14.880
A brush with the unknown keeping you up at
night?

00:58:14.880 --> 00:58:17.400
We’re looking for stories of the strange
and unusual!

00:58:17.940 --> 00:58:19.140
Write to us now!”

00:58:19.140 --> 00:58:21.300
Maybe they could do something that he couldn’t.

00:58:21.300 --> 00:58:26.100
He put the tape back into its cover, placed
it in an envelope, and mailed it off the next

00:58:26.100 --> 00:58:26.340
day.

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:31.560
A few days later, the package reached its
intended recipient: an undercover branch of

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:37.020
the SCP Foundation tracking anomalous activity
via a series of ads in comic books and video

00:58:37.020 --> 00:58:41.700
stores to tap into one of the most underutilized
resources in the world: nerds.

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:49.800
The tape was designated SCP-5733, and Heather
and the masked killer were designated SCP-5733-2

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:53.160
and SCP-5733-1, respectively.

00:58:53.160 --> 00:58:58.200
After an initial viewing confirmed its anomalous
properties, a research team headed by Dr.

00:58:58.200 --> 00:59:01.140
Carpenter began to perform a series of tests.

00:59:01.140 --> 00:59:05.940
Testing was open to all Foundation employees,
subject to approval by Dr. Carpenter.

00:59:05.940 --> 00:59:11.580
Approved personnel would be placed in a Site
73 multipurpose room with a VHS player and

00:59:11.580 --> 00:59:12.180
a television.

00:59:12.180 --> 00:59:16.680
The staff member would then watch the movie,
and attempt to engage with Heather and help

00:59:16.680 --> 00:59:17.160
her escape.

00:59:17.160 --> 00:59:21.180
For the first test, D-Class 1973 was selected.

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:25.020
When Heather turned to the camera and asked
for help, he asked if she had a car.

00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:27.960
When she confirmed that&nbsp;
yes, she did, he instructed&nbsp;

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:30.720
her to sneak back upstairs, find her car keys,

00:59:30.720 --> 00:59:34.440
leave through the back door, and drive as
fast and as far away as possible.

00:59:34.440 --> 00:59:39.360
She successfully retrieved the car keys, but,
when she reached the car, she saw that the

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:40.680
tires had been slashed.

00:59:40.680 --> 00:59:45.660
Heather began to panic, but 1973 talked her
through it, telling her to smash the window

00:59:45.660 --> 00:59:47.580
of a neighbor’s car and unlock the door.

00:59:47.580 --> 00:59:52.260
She complied, and 1973 instructed her on how
to hot-wire the vehicle.

00:59:52.260 --> 00:59:56.400
The car started, and with a triumphant laugh
she sped out of the cul-de-sac.

00:59:56.400 --> 01:00:01.620
Just as her car began to pull away, the killer
emerged from the backseat, wielding a kitchen

01:00:01.620 --> 01:00:01.980
knife.

01:00:01.980 --> 01:00:04.980
Heather screamed, and the tape cut to black.

01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:08.460
For the next test, D-Class 1944 was selected.

01:00:08.460 --> 01:00:13.140
He began by telling Heather to find her father’s
shotgun, seen earlier in the film, and use

01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:15.600
it to take out the killer before they could
get to her.

01:00:15.600 --> 01:00:19.920
She snuck up to her parents’ room and found
the gun, only for the killer to appear in

01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:20.940
the doorway behind her.

01:00:20.940 --> 01:00:24.060
She aimed the gun and fired…but nothing
happened.

01:00:24.060 --> 01:00:27.720
The killer opened their hand, dropping the
shotgun’s shells on the floor.

01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:30.900
She was close, but the killer was one step
ahead.

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:35.280
She screamed as they approached with the knife,
and again, the tape cut to black.

01:00:35.280 --> 01:00:40.440
After several tests were conducted with D-class
subjects, and no adverse effects were reported

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:45.000
- aside from the obvious trauma of failing
to save Heather’s life - testing was opened

01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:46.560
up to all Foundation staff.

01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:49.620
Assistant Researcher Felissa Baker volunteered.

01:00:49.620 --> 01:00:53.820
She believed her extensive knowledge of the
slasher genre would give her the tools to

01:00:53.820 --> 01:00:55.020
help Heather strategize.

01:00:55.020 --> 01:00:57.300
After speaking with Heather about her skills,&nbsp;

01:00:57.300 --> 01:01:00.000
which mainly included party&nbsp;
planning and babysitting,

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:02.880
Felissa determined that Heather should seek
outside help.

01:01:02.880 --> 01:01:06.900
With Felissa’s help, Heather made her way
out of the house and over to the home of her

01:01:06.900 --> 01:01:08.640
next door neighbor, Mr. Loomis.

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:12.360
When she got there, the door was open, and
the lights were off.

01:01:12.360 --> 01:01:17.040
It didn’t look great, but she didn’t really
have much of a choice but to go inside.

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:21.360
She made her way to the bedroom, where Mr.
Loomis and his wife were lying in bed.

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:26.160
She tried to wake him, but the camera zoomed
in to reveal his throat had been slashed.

01:01:26.160 --> 01:01:31.620
The shape in bed next to him sat up, and was
not in fact his wife, but the Suburb Slasher

01:01:31.620 --> 01:01:32.220
instead.

01:01:32.220 --> 01:01:36.240
As the figure raised their signature kitchen
knife, the screen went black.

01:01:36.240 --> 01:01:43.260
SCP-5733 became a competition of sorts among
Foundation staff, each volunteer proposing

01:01:43.260 --> 01:01:45.720
their knowledge would help them get Heather
to safety.

01:01:45.720 --> 01:01:50.820
Assistant Researcher Nick Englund-Daskewisz
proposed telling Heather to call for help,

01:01:50.820 --> 01:01:57.060
giving her a secret phone number for the SCP
Foundation circa 1983, the time in which the

01:01:57.060 --> 01:01:57.720
movie was set.

01:01:57.720 --> 01:02:02.520
However, when Heather reached the phone, it
had been destroyed, smashed into pieces.

01:02:02.520 --> 01:02:09.480
Next to it was a note, written in blood, that
said: “The only Foundation here is FEAR.”

01:02:09.480 --> 01:02:13.440
Then, the killer appeared behind Heather,
and the tape went black.

01:02:13.440 --> 01:02:16.500
Somehow, the killer knew about the SCP Foundation.

01:02:16.500 --> 01:02:21.660
Perhaps, in a manner of speaking, the call
was coming from inside the house.

01:02:21.660 --> 01:02:26.580
Next, Field Agents Malcolm Pleasance and Donald
McDowell were selected due to their extensive

01:02:26.580 --> 01:02:28.200
knowledge of hand-to-hand combat.

01:02:28.200 --> 01:02:32.880
They attempted to teach Heather various fighting
and self-defense techniques, while she survived

01:02:32.880 --> 01:02:34.980
on the limited supplies available in the basement.

01:02:34.980 --> 01:02:39.180
She eventually left the basement, and, when
the killer appeared, began to fight them.

01:02:39.180 --> 01:02:43.620
After twenty three minutes of combat, she
knocked the Slasher to the floor, and grabbed

01:02:43.620 --> 01:02:45.060
a candlestick to finish them off.

01:02:45.060 --> 01:02:50.340
As the agents watching began to celebrate,
a second version of the Suburban Slasher appeared

01:02:50.340 --> 01:02:51.960
behind her, lunging forward.

01:02:51.960 --> 01:02:55.680
Just before the new killer made contact, the
tape cut to black.

01:02:55.680 --> 01:03:00.900
During this test, the Slasher seemed more
adept at combat than it had before, as though

01:03:00.900 --> 01:03:03.000
it could hear the advice being given to Heather.

01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:07.800
With other techniques failing, Field Agent
Tilda-Joan Bennett was brought in for a test,

01:03:07.800 --> 01:03:10.800
selected due to her expertise in thaumaturgy.

01:03:10.800 --> 01:03:15.720
She instructed Heather on basic offensive
and defensive thaumaturgy, before guiding

01:03:15.720 --> 01:03:17.700
her out of the house and into the front yard.

01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:21.900
When the Slasher appeared, brandishing their
knife, Heather was able to sign a protective

01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:24.240
glyph and defend against the knife’s blow.

01:03:24.240 --> 01:03:27.720
She followed this with a wind spell that pushed
the killer further away.

01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:32.940
Heather began to run down the driveway, making
a break for survival, when the Slasher suddenly

01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:36.360
performed a freeze spell that rendered her
unable to move.

01:03:36.360 --> 01:03:40.920
Heather’s wide, panicked eyes stared down
the barrel of the camera in a silent scream

01:03:40.920 --> 01:03:45.600
as the killer approached her, recovered knife
in hand, before the tape went black.

01:03:45.600 --> 01:03:48.960
Eventually, Dr. Carpenter volunteered to conduct
a test.

01:03:48.960 --> 01:03:53.160
He instructed his research team to prepare
a list of options for Heather’s survival,

01:03:53.160 --> 01:03:58.320
divided into four categories: What to take
from the basement, where to go once leaving

01:03:58.320 --> 01:04:02.460
the basement, how to exit the house, and how
to escape the cul-de-sac.

01:04:02.460 --> 01:04:07.140
He was not informed about any of these options,
but rather had them printed out and placed

01:04:07.140 --> 01:04:09.060
into plastic bowls on the day of the test.

01:04:09.060 --> 01:04:14.100
There were also three cards created and placed
face-down, reading “Face,” “body”,

01:04:14.100 --> 01:04:15.240
and “legs.”

01:04:15.240 --> 01:04:18.120
When Heather addressed Dr. Carpenter, he informed&nbsp;

01:04:18.120 --> 01:04:20.280
her that he would be randomly&nbsp;
selecting instructions

01:04:20.280 --> 01:04:22.800
for her, and asked that she follow them exactly.

01:04:22.800 --> 01:04:27.300
The first slip of paper instructed Heather
to grab a pair of garden shears from the basement,

01:04:27.300 --> 01:04:30.060
and climb the stairs back into the main house.

01:04:30.060 --> 01:04:34.620
Next, she was told to go upstairs to the bedroom,
then downstairs into the dining room.

01:04:34.620 --> 01:04:37.500
She complied, and there was no sign of the
killer so far.

01:04:37.500 --> 01:04:43.380
Third, Dr. Carpenter told Heather to run back
upstairs, out the bedroom window, onto the

01:04:43.380 --> 01:04:45.360
roof, and then jump down into the garden.

01:04:45.360 --> 01:04:47.460
Then, it was time for the next step.

01:04:47.460 --> 01:04:52.140
He told Heather to jump over the fence into
the neighbor’s garden, and run down the

01:04:52.140 --> 01:04:54.000
street until she could find help.

01:04:54.000 --> 01:04:58.080
As Heather made her way down the road, the
Slasher broke through the front door of her

01:04:58.080 --> 01:05:00.360
house, but did not chase after her.

01:05:00.360 --> 01:05:04.980
Heather continued running down the road for
twenty minutes, as the trees and light began

01:05:04.980 --> 01:05:05.640
to dwindle.

01:05:05.640 --> 01:05:10.620
The surroundings began to grow darker and
darker, until they resembled a void.

01:05:10.620 --> 01:05:12.960
Dr. Carpenter instructed her to keep walking.

01:05:12.960 --> 01:05:15.420
She could make out lights and houses in the
distance.

01:05:15.420 --> 01:05:19.680
She ran toward the neighborhood, but stopped
suddenly and began to panic.

01:05:19.680 --> 01:05:25.800
She was back in her own neighborhood, in front
of her own house, and the killer was waiting

01:05:25.800 --> 01:05:26.220
for her.

01:05:26.220 --> 01:05:30.780
As Heather demanded to know what to do, Carpenter
flipped over one of the three cards.

01:05:30.780 --> 01:05:35.820
It read, “face,” and so he told Heather
to attack the killer’s face with the shears.

01:05:35.820 --> 01:05:37.380
Then, the killer’s legs.

01:05:37.380 --> 01:05:38.760
Then, his body.

01:05:38.760 --> 01:05:42.780
The killer dodged her attack, grabbed the
shears, and pushed her to the ground.

01:05:42.780 --> 01:05:48.000
As Heather looked up at the killer, his face
could be seen through the torn sack…it was

01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:49.140
Dr. Carpenter.

01:05:49.140 --> 01:05:51.900
Heather screamed, and the tape cut to black.

01:05:51.900 --> 01:05:53.820
Talk about a third-act twist.

01:05:53.820 --> 01:05:59.400
The movie itself did not create the killer,
but somehow the viewer did, and it also made

01:05:59.400 --> 01:06:00.720
the killer more creative.

01:06:00.720 --> 01:06:07.260
After this incident, all testing on SCP-5733
was suspended, and attempts to help turn Heather

01:06:07.260 --> 01:06:09.480
from victim to survivor were halted.

01:06:09.480 --> 01:06:14.040
Searching for answers, the Foundation looked
into Crystal Elms Productions, the production

01:06:14.040 --> 01:06:16.140
company listed on the video tape’s cover.

01:06:16.140 --> 01:06:20.880
No record of the production company, the film,
or any of its cast could be found.

01:06:20.880 --> 01:06:26.700
Several months after testing on SCP-5733 was
suspended, another tape was found.

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:30.780
Because let’s face it baby, these days,
you’ve gotta have a sequel.

01:06:30.780 --> 01:06:35.520
The new tape was titled “The Suburb Slasher
Strikes Again,” and was purported to be

01:06:35.520 --> 01:06:39.000
produced by Crystal Elms Productions in 1985.

01:06:39.000 --> 01:06:42.120
It was designated SCP-6733.

01:06:42.120 --> 01:06:46.320
Unlike its predecessor, this tape was only
tested once.

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:52.140
A member of D-class personnel was first shown
SCP-5733 up until the point where it became

01:06:52.140 --> 01:06:56.100
anomalous, and was then shown SCP-6733.

01:06:56.100 --> 01:06:59.820
He was left completely alone in order to watch
the contents of the tape.

01:06:59.820 --> 01:07:01.680
The following was what was on it.

01:07:01.680 --> 01:07:07.920
Dr. Malcolm Baines entered the testing chamber
to find D-1974, or Jaime, sitting across from

01:07:07.920 --> 01:07:08.940
the television set.

01:07:08.940 --> 01:07:12.420
He introduced himself, and ran a series of
cognitive tests.

01:07:12.420 --> 01:07:16.500
He asked Jaime what he thought of the film,
and he said, “It was pretty much your standard

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:17.100
slasher film.

01:07:17.100 --> 01:07:21.180
There's a group of teens who've just graduated
high school and go to a local camping site

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:22.200
by a lake to celebrate.

01:07:22.200 --> 01:07:26.160
One of them mentions it's near the site where
the killer, The Slasher, was shot dead by

01:07:26.160 --> 01:07:30.780
police a year prior after a rampage-”
Dr. Baines clarified, asking if this was a

01:07:30.780 --> 01:07:31.740
reference to the first film.

01:07:31.740 --> 01:07:34.440
Jaime continued, “It's not really clear.

01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:38.280
They all think it's a joke, apart from the
main girl — she says her dad's a police

01:07:38.280 --> 01:07:40.620
officer and she's seen video evidence of the
attack.

01:07:40.620 --> 01:07:44.400
No-one references any of the characters from
the first film though, and they don't show

01:07:44.400 --> 01:07:45.180
up in this one either.

01:07:45.180 --> 01:07:47.040
The Slasher's the only constant.”

01:07:47.040 --> 01:07:51.900
According to Jaime, the main events of the
film revolved around a lakeside camping trip

01:07:51.900 --> 01:07:53.880
and quickly began to go wrong.

01:07:53.880 --> 01:07:59.580
Dr. Baines inquired about the nature of the
Slasher’s kills, and Jaime elaborated, “He's

01:07:59.580 --> 01:08:03.300
still got a kitchen knife, same weapon as
the first film, so he stabs a lot of them.

01:08:03.300 --> 01:08:05.280
It's pretty gory for the time it was made.

01:08:05.280 --> 01:08:09.300
He slashes up someone's face, then the nerdy
guy gets stabbed through the eye — that

01:08:09.300 --> 01:08:11.640
one's pretty good, the camera gets sprayed
with blood.

01:08:11.640 --> 01:08:13.920
One of the teens gets his head crushed wide
open.”

01:08:13.920 --> 01:08:19.320
Dr. Baines asked Jaime how the scenes made
him feel, and he responded, “Like…

01:08:19.320 --> 01:08:24.180
there's some good jump scares, and the tension's
fairly high at points, but it's a little dated.

01:08:24.180 --> 01:08:26.700
I've seen scarier, but I've also seen worse
horrors.”

01:08:26.700 --> 01:08:31.860
Only one part of the film stood out to him:
He described it: “So, the girl and her best

01:08:31.860 --> 01:08:35.100
friend, the one that's been looking out for
her this whole time, enter into a cellar.

01:08:35.100 --> 01:08:39.300
The Slasher creeps up from behind, and grabs
the friend — tears his head clean off his

01:08:39.300 --> 01:08:39.480
neck.

01:08:39.480 --> 01:08:42.360
The Slasher then chases the other teen to
the side of the lake.

01:08:42.360 --> 01:08:43.620
He's advancing on her.

01:08:43.620 --> 01:08:46.500
The camera's set on the water of the lake,
it's a wide shot.

01:08:46.500 --> 01:08:50.880
You've got the lake waterline parallel to
the top and bottom of the shot, so it splits

01:08:50.880 --> 01:08:51.780
the screen horizontally.

01:08:51.780 --> 01:08:53.880
She's fallen over, crawling away from him.

01:08:53.880 --> 01:08:57.540
As he advances on her, the camera zooms in
— slowly though.

01:08:57.540 --> 01:08:59.160
It takes its time.

01:08:59.160 --> 01:08:59.820
He does too.

01:08:59.820 --> 01:09:04.380
There's music at the start of the scene: deep,
dark synths.

01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:06.780
This stops as the camera moves closer though.

01:09:06.780 --> 01:09:09.060
I forgot to say, it's, it's a long scene.

01:09:09.060 --> 01:09:11.520
Longer than five minutes, maybe it was ten.

01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:12.120
I don't know.

01:09:12.120 --> 01:09:13.440
It felt longer than ten.

01:09:13.440 --> 01:09:17.700
So the Slasher's approaching her, we're - the
viewers - approaching the shore.

01:09:17.700 --> 01:09:21.960
Then the music stops, and it's just his footsteps
and her pleading.

01:09:21.960 --> 01:09:28.080
And she's pleading man, she's… there's these
big, inhales of breath stifled by the mucus

01:09:28.080 --> 01:09:29.460
running out her nose.

01:09:29.460 --> 01:09:33.180
She's babbling, but it gets to a point she's
not even saying words, just making noise.”

01:09:33.180 --> 01:09:37.620
As Jaime continued, he began to grow increasingly
distressed.

01:09:37.620 --> 01:09:41.580
“The camera's real close to the shore now
— and the Slasher stops.

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:44.880
He turns his head and looks straight at the
camera.

01:09:44.880 --> 01:09:49.080
You can't see his eyes, but you know he's
looking straight at you.

01:09:49.080 --> 01:09:51.480
And he just stands there, staring.

01:09:51.480 --> 01:09:54.960
Eventually the girl crawls out the frame,
or the camera zooms past her.

01:09:54.960 --> 01:09:55.980
I can't remember which.

01:09:55.980 --> 01:09:58.440
It just keeps zooming in on his face.

01:09:58.440 --> 01:10:00.420
Where his face should be under the hood.

01:10:00.420 --> 01:10:03.120
The girl keeps screaming off-camera.

01:10:03.120 --> 01:10:07.080
Then there's this guttural, ripping noise
and the screaming stops.

01:10:07.080 --> 01:10:10.440
It just stops, but the camera keeps moving.

01:10:10.440 --> 01:10:13.740
You can see the individual droplets of blood
splashed across him.

01:10:13.740 --> 01:10:16.200
You can see the fabrics that make up his hood.

01:10:16.200 --> 01:10:19.020
His face soon takes up the entire shot.

01:10:19.020 --> 01:10:20.880
And then… black.

01:10:20.880 --> 01:10:22.260
No credits or nothing.

01:10:22.260 --> 01:10:24.960
The tape just cuts to black, and was pushed
out the player.”

01:10:24.960 --> 01:10:29.340
Dr. Baines pushed a bit, asking if there was
anything anomalous he could be forgetting.

01:10:29.340 --> 01:10:31.140
then, Jaime thought of something.

01:10:31.140 --> 01:10:32.040
“The girl.

01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:33.360
Her friends.

01:10:33.360 --> 01:10:34.200
All of them.

01:10:34.200 --> 01:10:35.640
I don’t think they had names.”

01:10:35.640 --> 01:10:41.340
Jaime became increasingly disturbed by the
film over time, calling Dr. Baines into his

01:10:41.340 --> 01:10:43.920
dormitory to ask him if they had shown him
a snuff film.

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:49.380
After falling asleep, he had a vivid dream
of the lake, crawling alongside it, recalling

01:10:49.380 --> 01:10:52.260
the deaths of his friends at the hand of a
masked killer.

01:10:52.260 --> 01:10:55.560
When he woke up, he saw a shadow outside the
window of his room.

01:10:55.560 --> 01:10:59.700
It stood there, silent and unmoving, all night.

01:10:59.700 --> 01:11:02.100
It only vanished when the sun came up.

01:11:02.100 --> 01:11:06.360
The next night, surveillance caught the image
of a humanoid shape running through the forest,

01:11:06.360 --> 01:11:08.400
but the guards found no trace of it.

01:11:08.400 --> 01:11:12.180
Dr. Baines returned to Jaime’s cell to find
him deeply distressed.

01:11:12.180 --> 01:11:15.660
He repeated over and over that the Slasher
was coming for him.

01:11:15.660 --> 01:11:17.880
Dr. Baines dismissed this concern.

01:11:17.880 --> 01:11:22.560
Once Jaime was alone, he stared into the mirror
as the temperature began to drop.

01:11:22.560 --> 01:11:24.420
“You’re here, aren’t you?”

01:11:24.420 --> 01:11:25.320
He said.

01:11:25.320 --> 01:11:29.220
A gloved hand punched through the mirror,
spraying glass everywhere.

01:11:29.220 --> 01:11:33.840
On the other side, there he was: The Suburb
Slasher.

01:11:33.840 --> 01:11:37.620
Jaime fled his dormitory and ran for his life,
as the Slasher followed him.

01:11:37.620 --> 01:11:42.840
Security Officer Loren confronted the Slasher,
but they lifted him up by the neck and stabbed

01:11:42.840 --> 01:11:44.760
the familiar kitchen knife through his head.

01:11:44.760 --> 01:11:49.620
Jaime continued to make his way through the
facility, the Slasher following and leaving

01:11:49.620 --> 01:11:52.260
a trail of destruction and bodies in his wake.

01:11:52.260 --> 01:11:56.880
Then, he reached Dr. Baines, who was confused
by his behavior.

01:11:56.880 --> 01:12:02.340
Dr. Baines insisted that the needed to get
to the basement, but, terrified, Jaime resisted

01:12:02.340 --> 01:12:02.580
him.

01:12:02.580 --> 01:12:07.680
The two men scuffled, and Jaime shoved Dr.
Baines hard, sending him careening into the

01:12:07.680 --> 01:12:07.860
wall.

01:12:07.860 --> 01:12:11.220
A lighting rig fell from the ceiling, landing
on Dr. Baines.

01:12:11.220 --> 01:12:15.960
Several strangers ran into the room, calling
for a medic on-set, and asking for production

01:12:15.960 --> 01:12:17.880
and lighting to come back and re-set everything.

01:12:17.880 --> 01:12:20.700
Offscreen, a director yelled “Cut!”

01:12:20.700 --> 01:12:25.920
The Slasher’s face appeared, taking up the
entirety of the camera, and the tape cut to

01:12:25.920 --> 01:12:26.160
black.

01:12:26.160 --> 01:12:31.800
This version of the tape’s events was created
after the tape was watched by D-1888 in a

01:12:31.800 --> 01:12:33.480
testing session with Dr. Carpenter.

01:12:33.480 --> 01:12:38.100
There has never been a Dr. Baines in the SCP
Foundation’s employ.

01:12:38.100 --> 01:12:42.180
Somehow, this second tape creates a localized
destabilization of reality.

01:12:42.180 --> 01:12:46.200
It is unknown how many times it has actually
been tested.

01:12:46.200 --> 01:12:50.100
So be careful what horror rentals you decide
to watch.

01:12:50.100 --> 01:12:55.320
You might just find yourself in the middle
of the action, and no matter what you think

01:12:55.320 --> 01:12:59.520
you know about surviving a slasher film, there
will be no escape.

01:12:59.520 --> 01:13:03.720
You can run, but you can’t hide.

01:13:03.720 --> 01:13:08.460
“Okay, guys, so to wrap up this video, I
really don’t think this ‘cursed episode’

01:13:08.460 --> 01:13:09.600
of this show was real.

01:13:09.600 --> 01:13:13.980
It’s obviously just a really good scary
fan animation, and the fact the creators behind

01:13:13.980 --> 01:13:17.892
it didn’t come out and claim ownership of
it sooner, allowing it to be reposted and

01:13:17.892 --> 01:13:21.660
reuploaded so many times is really what got
people thinking it was legit.

01:13:21.660 --> 01:13:25.500
That being said, it’s still a good attempt
at what a cursed, lost episode would look

01:13:25.500 --> 01:13:27.360
like, so shout out to those animators, man!

01:13:27.360 --> 01:13:29.100
“Anyway, I’ve been the G.O.A.T.

01:13:29.100 --> 01:13:32.700
HellHolder98, be sure to like this video,
subscribe to my channel down below.

01:13:32.700 --> 01:13:36.360
Hey, if you know about any more lost media
you want me to cover, leave it in the comments

01:13:36.360 --> 01:13:37.080
and I’ll check it out.

01:13:37.080 --> 01:13:39.600
Oh, and be sure to follow&nbsp;
my socials @HellHolder98,&nbsp;

01:13:39.600 --> 01:13:41.520
I go live most days and post updates about

01:13:41.520 --> 01:13:43.680
new videos on my story, so go check it out!

01:13:43.680 --> 01:13:46.560
Alright, until then, I’ll see you somewhere
down the line!”

01:13:46.560 --> 01:13:51.000
The moment his finger pushed the button to
stop the camera recording, the practiced,

01:13:51.000 --> 01:13:52.920
false smile dropped from Holden’s face.

01:13:52.920 --> 01:13:57.960
It was hard not to feel down after he’d
finished filming a new video, not thanks to

01:13:57.960 --> 01:14:03.060
any post-creativity slump, but the more depressing
knowledge of just how much of an uphill battle

01:14:03.060 --> 01:14:04.560
this whole thing was.

01:14:04.560 --> 01:14:08.940
It felt like yesterday when Holden had first
gotten into the creepier side of the internet.

01:14:08.940 --> 01:14:12.780
He never ventured into anything illicit or
outright illegal, mind you.

01:14:12.780 --> 01:14:17.520
But there was a distinct corner of the web
that had pulled him in when he was still at

01:14:17.520 --> 01:14:20.700
school, and just starting to spend more time
online.

01:14:20.700 --> 01:14:25.440
This part of the internet was filled with
scary stories that were mostly fake or made

01:14:25.440 --> 01:14:28.320
up for likes – but that could have been
real.

01:14:28.320 --> 01:14:33.180
There were unsettling animations, short clips
that had been hand-drawn to give people the

01:14:33.180 --> 01:14:34.980
creeps and keep them up at night.

01:14:34.980 --> 01:14:39.840
Not to mention a whole archive of public safety
announcement videos, terrifying workplace

01:14:39.840 --> 01:14:45.180
and road safety warnings that used to be broadcast
on TV, and were as petrifying that the most

01:14:45.180 --> 01:14:46.320
acclaimed horror movies.

01:14:46.320 --> 01:14:52.020
Speaking of things that were broadcast to
TV, that had gradually become Holden’s specialist

01:14:52.020 --> 01:14:52.350
subject.

01:14:52.350 --> 01:14:56.820
One of his friends had sent him the link to
a fictional account of someone who had supposedly

01:14:56.820 --> 01:15:02.280
uncovered an unaired episode of a Saturday
morning kid’s cartoon – or so this person

01:15:02.280 --> 01:15:03.180
claimed anyway.

01:15:03.180 --> 01:15:08.880
They went on to describe bizarre imagery,
so intense and terrifying that it was unbefitting

01:15:08.880 --> 01:15:10.080
of a show for children.

01:15:10.080 --> 01:15:14.640
Then, the person who made the original post
explained how they got into contact with the

01:15:14.640 --> 01:15:18.240
show’s creators via email, to ask them about
this cursed episode.

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:23.220
The showrunner responded stating that the
episode the poster had allegedly seen, didn’t

01:15:23.220 --> 01:15:24.900
even exist.

01:15:24.900 --> 01:15:28.260
To Holden, it didn’t matter if the story
was real or not.

01:15:28.260 --> 01:15:29.940
That wasn’t the point.

01:15:29.940 --> 01:15:33.120
What was far more important was that it felt
real.

01:15:33.120 --> 01:15:38.220
To him, it was plausible, even possible, that
there were pieces of media out there in the

01:15:38.220 --> 01:15:40.020
world that had never seen the light of day.

01:15:40.020 --> 01:15:45.600
Episodes of TV shows or entire movies that
were so wild and out there that they could

01:15:45.600 --> 01:15:49.980
have been banned and buried long before the
dawn of internet, wherein there was a record

01:15:49.980 --> 01:15:54.360
of everything, and nothing was ever really
lost.

01:15:54.360 --> 01:15:59.280
The idea of uncovering those lost pieces of
media became Holden’s primary hobby.

01:15:59.280 --> 01:16:04.200
He’d come home from school, throw his backpack
onto his bed, and then sit in front of his

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:08.280
computer for hours without even changing out
of the clothes he had been wearing all day

01:16:08.280 --> 01:16:08.940
in class.

01:16:08.940 --> 01:16:13.740
But all time he spent trawling through forum
threads, following and messaging collectors

01:16:13.740 --> 01:16:18.480
on social media, listening to theories, and
coming up with his own; eventually it began

01:16:18.480 --> 01:16:20.280
to encroach on other things.

01:16:20.280 --> 01:16:25.920
Much to his mom’s disappointment, Holden’s
grades took a rapid decline, and it wasn’t

01:16:25.920 --> 01:16:30.600
long before he was failing his classes bad
enough to not make the cut to continue on

01:16:30.600 --> 01:16:31.080
at school.

01:16:31.080 --> 01:16:34.740
But, as much as it upset his mother, Holden
really didn’t mind.

01:16:34.740 --> 01:16:39.900
He was already thinking way beyond high school
and knew he didn’t need grades to be able

01:16:39.900 --> 01:16:42.840
to do the one thing he wanted to with his
life.

01:16:42.840 --> 01:16:47.640
Holden set up his own channel with the plan
to start uploading videos under his new online

01:16:47.640 --> 01:16:48.600
alter ego.

01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:52.140
And before long, ‘HellHolder98’ was born.

01:16:52.140 --> 01:16:57.360
He centered his whole internet persona around
lost media, discussing which popular rumors

01:16:57.360 --> 01:17:00.960
were true and which were fictionalized for
likes and clicks.

01:17:00.960 --> 01:17:05.280
It started out with a few discussion videos,
where he would simply sit in his bedroom,

01:17:05.280 --> 01:17:08.160
a camcorder opposite him as he spoke.

01:17:08.160 --> 01:17:12.660
But before long, Holden was adding more and
more flair to his content, learning how to

01:17:12.660 --> 01:17:17.640
edit on his computer, adding clips or screenshots
he could find of supposed lost TV episodes

01:17:17.640 --> 01:17:22.440
to give his videos some credibility; a very
necessary quality when trying to determine

01:17:22.440 --> 01:17:24.420
if certain things were real or not.

01:17:24.420 --> 01:17:29.760
Each video he finished, he’d add another
two topics to a list of ideas he kept, planning

01:17:29.760 --> 01:17:34.980
to just perpetually churn out content until
he eventually had a huge hit that went viral.

01:17:34.980 --> 01:17:39.900
Although the one thing he didn’t realize
until it was perhaps too late, was that social

01:17:39.900 --> 01:17:42.900
media success didn’t happen overnight.

01:17:42.900 --> 01:17:45.720
In fact, it didn’t happen over many, many
nights either.

01:17:45.720 --> 01:17:51.000
No matter how often Holden promoted his channel
on his social media profiles, or how many

01:17:51.000 --> 01:17:55.380
new videos he uploaded, he couldn’t seem
to get any to land well and get boosted in

01:17:55.380 --> 01:17:56.040
the algorithm.

01:17:56.040 --> 01:18:01.020
At least, that’s one of the things he attributed
the problem to; he pointed the finger of blame

01:18:01.020 --> 01:18:02.520
at anything that he could.

01:18:02.520 --> 01:18:07.320
One day it was that the algorithm was suppressing
his channel and boosting others who had already

01:18:07.320 --> 01:18:08.460
got more subscribers.

01:18:08.460 --> 01:18:13.260
The next it might be his slow internet connection
had led to one of his videos going live at

01:18:13.260 --> 01:18:14.880
a time when the site had low traffic.

01:18:14.880 --> 01:18:20.280
The one possibility that Holden didn’t stop
to consider was that maybe his genre of content

01:18:20.280 --> 01:18:25.200
was too niche, or that the days of the internet’s
interest in lost media had already peaked

01:18:25.200 --> 01:18:27.000
back when he was still in high school.

01:18:27.000 --> 01:18:33.840
Nevertheless, he kept trying, making content
day in and day out, working under the assumption

01:18:33.840 --> 01:18:38.400
that, if he just made enough videos, then
one day one was sure to blow up.

01:18:38.400 --> 01:18:43.200
That would be his big break, his ultimate
win, a video that did well enough to garner

01:18:43.200 --> 01:18:46.080
thousands, if not millions of views.

01:18:46.080 --> 01:18:48.780
But the more time he devoted to HellHolder98,&nbsp;

01:18:48.780 --> 01:18:51.420
the only number that seemed&nbsp;
to be at all increasing

01:18:51.420 --> 01:18:55.740
was the number of videos he had posted, each
one barely garnering view figures that were

01:18:55.740 --> 01:18:57.300
above a single digit.

01:18:57.300 --> 01:19:01.920
To make matters worse, getting help with growing
his online brand was next to impossible.

01:19:01.920 --> 01:19:06.300
Every now and then, he would post on forums
asking for advice, or if anyone wanted to

01:19:06.300 --> 01:19:09.660
collaborate so he could gain some exposure
from creators with bigger followings.

01:19:09.660 --> 01:19:14.940
Those posts were often met with a slew of
apathetic, snide responses, or comments telling

01:19:14.940 --> 01:19:20.280
Holden to just ‘make better content’ as
if that was solid, specific advice.

01:19:20.280 --> 01:19:24.780
On top of that, his mom had outright refused
to support her son’s chosen career path,

01:19:24.780 --> 01:19:27.480
citing his failure at school as the main reason.

01:19:27.480 --> 01:19:32.400
Speaking of, any friends Holden previously
had at school had all moved away in the years

01:19:32.400 --> 01:19:37.500
since, going off to pursue college and other
higher education, some were even starting

01:19:37.500 --> 01:19:39.360
their new and exciting professions.

01:19:39.360 --> 01:19:44.280
The old saying said ‘it’s lonely at the
top’, but Holden was just as lonely down

01:19:44.280 --> 01:19:49.140
at the bottom, posting his content in total
obscurity as if he was just shouting into

01:19:49.140 --> 01:19:51.780
an empty void, with no-one around to hear.

01:19:51.780 --> 01:19:56.940
The few people Holden did still consider his
friends were all as chronically online as

01:19:56.940 --> 01:20:00.060
he was, most of them fellow lost media collectors.

01:20:00.060 --> 01:20:04.740
After interacting with them in the comment
threads of various forums, the handful of

01:20:04.740 --> 01:20:09.300
like-minded guys were as close as Holden had
to people actively supporting his channel.

01:20:09.300 --> 01:20:14.400
The collectors would usually give him pointers
or topics to discuss in videos and contributing

01:20:14.400 --> 01:20:17.280
to the single digit view count underneath
his uploads.

01:20:17.280 --> 01:20:22.320
Although, to him, it wasn’t nearly enough;
he didn’t want just his friends to see his

01:20:22.320 --> 01:20:26.880
videos, he wanted an audience, a fanbase all
of his own.

01:20:26.880 --> 01:20:31.320
It was late, the light of the computer monitor
illuminated the dark of Holden’s bedroom.

01:20:31.320 --> 01:20:36.120
Hunched over his desk, he was clicking and
dragging clips into the timeline of a video

01:20:36.120 --> 01:20:41.100
project, trimming them down to make the whole
thing better paced, snipping out bad takes

01:20:41.100 --> 01:20:43.620
where he stumbled over his words or misspoke.

01:20:43.620 --> 01:20:48.180
It was while editing that Holden noticed another
screen lighting up on his desk, accompanied

01:20:48.180 --> 01:20:49.980
by a vibration; his phone.

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:53.400
He reached for it, seeing a notification pop
up on the locked screen.

01:20:53.400 --> 01:20:56.100
It was from Goath, one of the lost media&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:56.100 --> 01:20:56.940
collectors
and it read:

01:20:56.940 --> 01:20:58.260
“Dude, urgent!

01:20:58.260 --> 01:21:00.180
Found something that you can make a video
on!”

01:21:00.180 --> 01:21:04.920
Holden sighed; it was late, and he was already
focused on editing this current video tonight.

01:21:04.920 --> 01:21:07.680
If he got it finished, then he could go to
sleep.

01:21:07.680 --> 01:21:09.900
“What do you know about the Deathly Video
Tape?”

01:21:09.900 --> 01:21:14.520
Goath asked in a second message, before Holden
even had a chance to open the first.

01:21:14.520 --> 01:21:18.720
“The what?” he replied bluntly, before
sending another text saying: “Can’t this

01:21:18.720 --> 01:21:19.380
wait until the morning?

01:21:19.380 --> 01:21:20.280
I’m trying to edit.”

01:21:20.280 --> 01:21:22.200
“If you don’t act fast, it’ll be gone
forever!”

01:21:22.200 --> 01:21:25.560
came the response only a second later, followed
by a link.

01:21:25.560 --> 01:21:30.720
Lethargically, Holden tapped the hyperlink
Goath had sent, his phone opening up its browser

01:21:30.720 --> 01:21:31.920
and displaying the webpage.

01:21:31.920 --> 01:21:36.600
It was a buy and sell website, one of those
places where people offloaded junk they didn’t

01:21:36.600 --> 01:21:39.360
want anymore to strangers for a bit of extra
cash.

01:21:39.360 --> 01:21:45.060
The page in question showed a few grainy photos
taken on a phone of a small, rectangular object

01:21:45.060 --> 01:21:46.020
in someone’s hand.

01:21:46.020 --> 01:21:47.100
“What is that?”

01:21:47.100 --> 01:21:48.060
Holden asked.

01:21:48.060 --> 01:21:49.560
“It’s a video tape, bonehead.”

01:21:49.560 --> 01:21:53.640
Goath fired back; although they’d never
met in person, Holden always got the sense

01:21:53.640 --> 01:21:55.500
his friend was a little older than he was.

01:21:55.500 --> 01:22:00.960
Looking back on the seller’s ad, it was
for a second-hand Sony Color Collection 60-90

01:22:00.960 --> 01:22:06.660
min Mini DV video tape, a type of cassette
used in a lot of old handheld recording cameras.

01:22:06.660 --> 01:22:08.040
“So, what?”

01:22:08.040 --> 01:22:09.600
Holden asked in another text.

01:22:09.600 --> 01:22:13.620
“Look, there have been rumors for ages about
something called the Deathly Video Tape,”

01:22:13.620 --> 01:22:15.960
Goath replied in a series of rapid-fire messages.

01:22:15.960 --> 01:22:20.640
“It used to be all the rage on of a lot
of old lost media forums, the real nasty ones

01:22:20.640 --> 01:22:21.600
before they got shut down.

01:22:21.600 --> 01:22:25.440
Supposedly, there’s a recording on this
tape of some kind of live show.

01:22:25.440 --> 01:22:28.800
Except, when you put it in your VCR and press
play, you see something horrible.

01:22:28.800 --> 01:22:31.440
Nobody’s ever known if it’s real or just
a legend.”

01:22:31.440 --> 01:22:33.780
“How do you know this is the same tape?”

01:22:33.780 --> 01:22:38.100
Holden queried; by now, he had typed out the
same link on his computer, and was looking

01:22:38.100 --> 01:22:42.600
at the ‘for sale’ page on the bigger screen
while he texted back and forth with Goath.

01:22:42.600 --> 01:22:45.000
“Read the item description,” he answered.

01:22:45.000 --> 01:22:48.660
Holden’s eyes scanned down the page, finding
a short message from the seller.

01:22:48.660 --> 01:22:52.020
“To anyone interested, I am giving this
old Mini DV tape away.

01:22:52.020 --> 01:22:55.440
I don’t know where I got it, or why I watched
it, but I wish I hadn’t.

01:22:55.440 --> 01:23:00.060
It had ruined everything for me, it’s impossible
to enjoy anything else now that I’ve seen

01:23:00.060 --> 01:23:00.720
what’s on it.

01:23:00.720 --> 01:23:05.460
Let me make this clear, I am not selling this
tape; I am giving it away free of charge.

01:23:05.460 --> 01:23:07.560
I hope that getting rid of it will help.”

01:23:07.560 --> 01:23:09.840
“Sounds ominous,” Holden texted.

01:23:09.840 --> 01:23:13.380
“If you’re so sure it's this Deathly Video
Tape, then why don’t you get it.”

01:23:13.380 --> 01:23:15.420
“Keep reading,” Goath responded.

01:23:15.420 --> 01:23:18.720
Underneath the item description was another
note that the seller had written:

01:23:18.720 --> 01:23:23.520
“Please note, I am unable to leave my town
at present, nor can I mail this tape to anyone

01:23:23.520 --> 01:23:24.840
even if you pay postage.

01:23:24.840 --> 01:23:28.680
Collection only, if you are interested, please
contact me at the following address.”

01:23:28.680 --> 01:23:34.200
Below was an address; it was in Holden’s
hometown, only a few streets away from where

01:23:34.200 --> 01:23:35.640
he and his mom lived.

01:23:35.640 --> 01:23:39.000
“This could be it, dude,” came another
slew of messages from Goath.

01:23:39.000 --> 01:23:43.260
“You buy this tape and make a video reacting
to it, then you might finally go viral.

01:23:43.260 --> 01:23:46.980
But you better be quick, before someone goes
and picks it up thinking its just an ordinary

01:23:46.980 --> 01:23:47.400
tape.”

01:23:47.400 --> 01:23:50.220
Holden looked back at the address, double-checking
where it was.

01:23:50.220 --> 01:23:53.400
At most, it’d take a quick walk there and
back, he thought.

01:23:53.400 --> 01:23:57.720
And if this tape was as exclusive as Goath
said it was, then owning it would mean Holden

01:23:57.720 --> 01:24:00.780
would be the only one who could make a video
featuring it…

01:24:00.780 --> 01:24:04.920
After messaging the seller the night before,
Holden found himself rushing around through

01:24:04.920 --> 01:24:06.600
his quiet neighborhood to the address.

01:24:06.600 --> 01:24:11.220
All night, as he’d tried to sleep, he kept
thinking of the video he was going to make,

01:24:11.220 --> 01:24:16.560
how it could finally put him on the map and
at long last bring him the e-fame he’d been

01:24:16.560 --> 01:24:17.340
working towards.

01:24:17.340 --> 01:24:21.900
Rapping his knuckles against the front door,
he was met by an older man who stood on the

01:24:21.900 --> 01:24:22.260
other side.

01:24:22.260 --> 01:24:26.760
He was walking with a crutch, with a few bruises
and stitched-up cuts on his face.

01:24:26.760 --> 01:24:30.960
The second that Holden explained he was there
for the tape, the man reached to an unseen

01:24:30.960 --> 01:24:35.580
shelf just inside the doorframe and thrust
the rectangular cassette into his hands before

01:24:35.580 --> 01:24:39.240
shutting the door as quickly as he could without
it hitting Holden square in the face.

01:24:39.240 --> 01:24:45.120
He stared at the tape in his hands; through
its transparent green plastic case, he could

01:24:45.120 --> 01:24:48.420
see a hand-scrawled note:
“I know you’ll ignore me if I tell you

01:24:48.420 --> 01:24:50.160
not to watch this,” it read.

01:24:50.160 --> 01:24:53.280
“So, if you do, then on your own head be
it.”

01:24:53.280 --> 01:24:57.780
Having spent the rest of the day looking up
exactly what he’d need to play such an old,

01:24:57.780 --> 01:25:02.520
outmoded recording format, and convert the
footage to digital so he could include it

01:25:02.520 --> 01:25:07.860
in his video, Holden had retrieved his mom’s
old VHS player from the attic, wiping the

01:25:07.860 --> 01:25:09.480
thick layer of dust of it.

01:25:09.480 --> 01:25:13.740
It took a while for him to get everything
ready to go; not just finding the right adapters

01:25:13.740 --> 01:25:18.840
and cables to hook the VCR up to his TV, as
well as linking that to his computer, plus

01:25:18.840 --> 01:25:23.160
angling his camera right and making sure the
ring light he’d bought for filming was putting

01:25:23.160 --> 01:25:24.360
enough focus on his face.

01:25:24.360 --> 01:25:29.640
After the substantial prep, Holden took a
deep breath, summoning up the faux excitement

01:25:29.640 --> 01:25:33.300
and staged smile, before he hit the record
button on his camera.

01:25:33.300 --> 01:25:35.820
“What’s up guys, it’s HellHolder98 here!

01:25:35.820 --> 01:25:37.680
Now, today I’ve got a real treat for you
all.

01:25:37.680 --> 01:25:41.460
So, my friend Goath – shout out to him – told
me that a while ago there was a lot of talk

01:25:41.460 --> 01:25:43.680
about something called the Deathly Video Tape.

01:25:43.680 --> 01:25:49.500
We found a Mini DV that we think might be
that very same tape, so we’re gonna watch

01:25:49.500 --> 01:25:50.700
it and see if it’s legit!”

01:25:50.700 --> 01:25:55.260
Dropping his persona as his face turned away
from the camera, Holden punched the play button

01:25:55.260 --> 01:25:59.880
on the VHS player, his eyes as glued to the
TV screen as the camera’s lens was fixated

01:25:59.880 --> 01:26:00.360
on him.

01:26:00.360 --> 01:26:05.520
“Okay, nothing so far,” he observed, met
with nothing but a blank, black screen.

01:26:05.520 --> 01:26:10.200
Suddenly, after twelve seconds of nothing,
the playback started instantly, and Holden

01:26:10.200 --> 01:26:12.960
started relaying what he was seeing on the
tape to the camera.

01:26:12.960 --> 01:26:18.120
There was no sound, either because the recording
on the videotape was filmed that way, or Holden

01:26:18.120 --> 01:26:20.700
hadn’t properly figured out how to hook
up the VHS.

01:26:20.700 --> 01:26:26.100
The video itself showed a recording of Sesame
Street Live, and for the most part just seemed

01:26:26.100 --> 01:26:27.000
pretty underwhelming.

01:26:27.000 --> 01:26:32.340
Characters were up on stage, their puppeteers
managing to stay out of sight as they entertained

01:26:32.340 --> 01:26:33.540
their young audience.

01:26:33.540 --> 01:26:37.620
After two and a half minutes, Holden was starting
to feel like he’d gone to all this effort

01:26:37.620 --> 01:26:41.040
over what seemed to just be someone’s old,
unwanted home video.

01:26:41.040 --> 01:26:45.240
Just as he was considering turning off the
camera and throwing the videotape in the trash,

01:26:45.240 --> 01:26:49.860
at almost the three-minute mark, something
weird started happening on stage.

01:26:49.860 --> 01:26:54.300
The actor playing one of the bigger characters
seemed to be having some trouble, instantly

01:26:54.300 --> 01:26:58.860
trying to pull off his cumbersome costume,
much to the distress of the kids in the audience,

01:26:58.860 --> 01:27:00.300
looking on in horror.

01:27:00.300 --> 01:27:04.560
But it wasn’t just that the illusion had
been broken, the actor was trying to get out

01:27:04.560 --> 01:27:06.960
of his costume because he was choking.

01:27:06.960 --> 01:27:09.600
He dropped to his knees on stage, clawing&nbsp;

01:27:09.600 --> 01:27:12.660
at his own throat before&nbsp;
finally falling face-down.

01:27:12.660 --> 01:27:16.440
Totally still, asphyxiated… dead.

01:27:16.440 --> 01:27:20.760
Over the next almost twenty seconds, the same
thing started happening to another three of

01:27:20.760 --> 01:27:25.440
the characters on stage, all of them violently
choking while the children in the audience

01:27:25.440 --> 01:27:28.380
screamed and cried silently on the inaudible
tape.

01:27:28.380 --> 01:27:31.800
Then, at three and a half minutes, the video
cut out.

01:27:31.800 --> 01:27:37.920
Holden was unsure how to react, at first a
little creeped out, only to be somewhat bemused.

01:27:37.920 --> 01:27:42.180
He played it down for the camera, remarking
that the video seemed tame compared to some

01:27:42.180 --> 01:27:44.940
of the other more graphic, faked content he’d
seen.

01:27:44.940 --> 01:27:49.800
But the whole time, even after wrapping recording,
sitting awake all night to edit his reaction

01:27:49.800 --> 01:27:55.020
video, blurring out the parts that would break
terms of service, he had no idea if what he’d

01:27:55.020 --> 01:27:56.940
seen was actually real or not.

01:27:56.940 --> 01:28:01.740
Finishing the final edit just as the sun was
coming up, Holden hit upload and crawled into

01:28:01.740 --> 01:28:03.420
bed while his video was uploaded.

01:28:03.420 --> 01:28:06.000
The buzz of a text message from Goath awoke
him.

01:28:06.000 --> 01:28:08.460
“Dude, I told you this video was going to
make it big!”

01:28:08.460 --> 01:28:12.720
Without even replying, Holden booted up his
computer and opened the page for his newly

01:28:12.720 --> 01:28:13.500
uploaded video.

01:28:13.500 --> 01:28:18.180
The view count was already in the thousands
and climbing, comments pouring in underneath;

01:28:18.180 --> 01:28:22.380
mostly from people debating whether or not
the contents of the tape had been faked.

01:28:22.380 --> 01:28:26.040
Holden punched the air in excitement; he’d
finally done it.

01:28:26.040 --> 01:28:30.060
But as the video kept playing, he heard a
retching sound coming from the computer’s

01:28:30.060 --> 01:28:30.300
speakers.

01:28:30.300 --> 01:28:34.740
He turned to look back at his earlier self,
filmed only the night before.

01:28:34.740 --> 01:28:39.480
Except what he was seeing play out on screen
now wasn’t what he remembered happening

01:28:39.480 --> 01:28:44.760
last night, it couldn’t have… because
the Holden in the video was choking.

01:28:44.760 --> 01:28:50.820
That wasn’t possible, he was alive and watching
his own video right now, but somehow the footage

01:28:50.820 --> 01:28:56.520
was showing him dying, his airway blocked,
face turning red then blue as tears streamed

01:28:56.520 --> 01:28:57.300
down his cheeks.

01:28:57.300 --> 01:29:03.180
In the video, HellHolder98 fell down, dead
from asphyxiation… and watching it made

01:29:03.180 --> 01:29:04.380
Holden feel sick.

01:29:04.380 --> 01:29:08.160
To make matters worse, none of the comments
under the video seemed to have witnessed the

01:29:08.160 --> 01:29:08.760
same ending.

01:29:08.760 --> 01:29:13.080
Holden even texted Goath to ask if the same
thing had happened, but he described the ending

01:29:13.080 --> 01:29:15.120
exactly as Holden remembered filming it.

01:29:15.120 --> 01:29:17.400
But afterwards, it didn’t stop happening.

01:29:17.400 --> 01:29:23.340
Everything he watched ended the same, every
clip online, every TV show, every movie, Holden

01:29:23.340 --> 01:29:29.220
couldn’t even read a book without some character,
fictional or otherwise, keeling over and choking

01:29:29.220 --> 01:29:31.800
to death, just like the characters on the
tape.

01:29:31.800 --> 01:29:36.300
He barely had time to acknowledge the hits
his reaction to the Deathly Video Tape were

01:29:36.300 --> 01:29:36.540
getting.

01:29:36.540 --> 01:29:41.040
He was too busy trying to figure out why he
couldn’t stop seeing people dying the exact

01:29:41.040 --> 01:29:45.660
same way, even himself when he watched back
his old HellHolder98 videos.

01:29:45.660 --> 01:29:48.120
‘Reacting to the Deathly Video Tape’ garnered&nbsp;

01:29:48.120 --> 01:29:51.300
a respectable three million&nbsp;
views, but HellHolder98

01:29:51.300 --> 01:29:53.040
never uploaded again afterwards.

01:29:53.040 --> 01:29:58.740
His channel went silent, remembered only as
a one-hit wonder until the video, and the

01:29:58.740 --> 01:30:00.720
entire channel were taken down.

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:05.640
Holden was never reported missing, the Foundation
made sure of that when they came by to collect

01:30:05.640 --> 01:30:10.200
SCP-583, and placed the&nbsp;
video tape into containment…

01:30:10.200 --> 01:30:13.200
Hey, do you have a favorite reality show?

01:30:13.200 --> 01:30:16.260
Oh come on, don't lie, everyone has one.

01:30:16.260 --> 01:30:20.940
Dr. Clef loves some American Ninja Warrior,
and I’ve got it on good authority that Dr.

01:30:20.940 --> 01:30:24.420
Bright can’t get enough of The Bachelor,
