As we were saying,
Laugh is Fun is a prank show that puts unknowing

01:33:43.560 --> 01:33:47.460
everyday people into ridiculous scenarios
and films their reactions.

01:33:47.460 --> 01:33:53.100
Each episode is about 10 to 12 minutes long
and features an opening and closing monologue

01:33:53.100 --> 01:34:00.840
from beloved, supposedly human TV presenter
Laughy McLaugherson, aka SCP-2030-1.

01:34:00.840 --> 01:34:05.820
You've probably seen him before, he's never
seen without his blue three-piece suit and

01:34:05.820 --> 01:34:07.140
black wing-tipped shoes.

01:34:07.140 --> 01:34:12.120
Funniest guy on TV, seriously, I think he
should replace James Corden on the Late Late

01:34:12.120 --> 01:34:12.420
Show.

01:34:12.420 --> 01:34:17.520
And maybe even take up a hosting gig on SNL,
if he’s still got time after.

01:34:17.520 --> 01:34:21.780
He had this amazing monologue at the end of
an episode in season 32.

01:34:21.780 --> 01:34:22.435
“Ha!

01:34:22.434 --> 01:34:24.120
What a ride, eh, folks?

01:34:24.120 --> 01:34:26.940
We've seen printers that eat, eaters that
print, and everything in between!

01:34:26.940 --> 01:34:30.660
Makes you appreciate the old clunker you have
back the office, doesn't it?

01:34:30.660 --> 01:34:35.100
No, printers may not always work when you
want or need them to, but they sure make for

01:34:35.100 --> 01:34:36.480
some excellent comedy.

01:34:36.480 --> 01:34:39.240
And that's what we're about here: comedy.

01:34:39.240 --> 01:34:40.620
We're here to make you laugh.

01:34:40.620 --> 01:34:41.760
We hope you laughed.

01:34:41.760 --> 01:34:43.260
Thank you for laughing with us.

01:34:43.260 --> 01:34:45.480
That's what we're about here, doesn't it,
folks?

01:34:45.480 --> 01:34:47.160
Come laugh with us again next time!

01:34:47.160 --> 01:34:51.600
And remember: LAUGH … IS … FUN!

01:34:51.600 --> 01:34:52.680
Good night!

01:34:52.680 --> 01:34:53.220
And laugh!

01:34:53.220 --> 01:34:53.760
And laugh!

01:34:53.760 --> 01:34:54.480
Just laugh!

01:34:54.480 --> 01:34:55.740
We love the make laugh.

01:34:55.740 --> 01:34:58.200
Make more for laughter so as to for laugh.

01:34:58.200 --> 01:34:59.280
Laugh with us.

01:34:59.280 --> 01:35:00.240
Laugh with us.

01:35:00.240 --> 01:35:01.320
Laugh with us!

01:35:01.320 --> 01:35:02.400
Laugh with us!

01:35:02.400 --> 01:35:03.000
Laugh with us!

01:35:03.000 --> 01:35:03.660
Laugh with us!

01:35:03.660 --> 01:35:04.440
Laugh!

01:35:04.440 --> 01:35:04.800
Laugh!

01:35:04.800 --> 01:35:07.320
Laugh and let us in!”

01:35:07.320 --> 01:35:08.400
Hmmm.

01:35:08.400 --> 01:35:12.540
I couldn't tell you what his face looks like,
though, because he's always framed from the

01:35:12.540 --> 01:35:13.020
neck down.

01:35:13.020 --> 01:35:17.220
Once the pranks are done for the episode,
he'll step up on that bright yellow stage

01:35:17.220 --> 01:35:21.840
and deliver his closing monologue, which always
ends with a pan over the audience as everyone

01:35:21.840 --> 01:35:23.400
chants “Laugh with us!”

01:35:23.400 --> 01:35:26.280
Then the show always just cuts to black.

01:35:26.280 --> 01:35:31.440
The closest thing the show ever has to credits
is a title card that says “Filmed in front

01:35:31.440 --> 01:35:35.880
of a studio audience in&nbsp;
partnership with YWTGTHFT.”

01:35:35.880 --> 01:35:42.120
For a show that doesn't advertise or even
air on TV, Laugh is Fun is still a beloved

01:35:42.120 --> 01:35:46.320
classic, and when you watch it, it's easy
to see why it's been around so long.

01:35:46.320 --> 01:35:50.160
Laughy and the team pull off some really classic
pranks on Laugh is Laugh!

01:35:50.160 --> 01:35:54.600
There's this one episode in season 13, where
an unsuspecting family is sitting down to

01:35:54.600 --> 01:35:54.840
dinner.

01:35:54.840 --> 01:35:58.500
The father starts choking, and the family
reacts with horror, until suddenly a pair

01:35:58.500 --> 01:36:01.020
of slits appear on the father's neck, allowing
him to breathe.

01:36:01.020 --> 01:36:04.920
Those slits soon become nostrils, and before
you know it, a full second head grows out

01:36:04.920 --> 01:36:05.340
of his neck.

01:36:05.340 --> 01:36:09.960
That head then grows its own neck, which grows
a nose, then a face, then another head, then

01:36:09.960 --> 01:36:10.560
another neck.

01:36:10.560 --> 01:36:14.820
This keeps happening a total of 18 times while
the rest of the family looks on in horror.

01:36:14.820 --> 01:36:19.080
But don't worry, before things go too far,
Laughy pops out from under the table and tells

01:36:19.080 --> 01:36:20.460
the family that they're on Laugh is Fun.

01:36:20.460 --> 01:36:25.320
Laughy points out the cameras, and the whole
family starts laughing, including all 18 of

01:36:25.320 --> 01:36:26.220
the father's heads.

01:36:26.220 --> 01:36:29.700
There's another great season 13 episode called
“Squirrels.”

01:36:29.700 --> 01:36:33.060
Oh, man, that one had me in stitches!

01:36:33.060 --> 01:36:37.500
Alright, so there's this couple lying in bed,
and the wife wakes up because she hears this

01:36:37.500 --> 01:36:38.280
squeaking noise.

01:36:38.280 --> 01:36:42.780
She taps her husband on the shoulder to wake
him up, but she draws her hand back in horror

01:36:42.780 --> 01:36:45.480
because she can feel something moving under
her husband's skin.

01:36:46.080 --> 01:36:47.940
Before she can ask if he's okay, boom!

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:52.080
Hundreds of squirrels explode out of his skin
and start running around the room while the

01:36:52.080 --> 01:36:52.680
wife screams.

01:36:52.680 --> 01:36:56.820
Then Laughy walks in and turns the lights
on, accompanied by the husband, who is perfectly

01:36:56.820 --> 01:36:59.340
okay even though he's been skinned from head
to toe.

01:36:59.340 --> 01:37:00.360
Everyone laughs.

01:37:00.960 --> 01:37:04.260
Then there's a more recent episode from season
24, called “Swelling.”

01:37:04.260 --> 01:37:08.040
There's this old lady sitting on a park bench,
feeding the pigeons, and a couple walks past

01:37:08.040 --> 01:37:08.400
her.

01:37:08.400 --> 01:37:11.820
While they walk past, the old lady gets swarmed
by the pigeons she's feeding.

01:37:11.820 --> 01:37:15.660
The pigeons force themselves into her mouth
and keep swarming until her stomach cavity

01:37:15.660 --> 01:37:16.380
bursts open.

01:37:16.380 --> 01:37:21.120
The couple, who get sprayed with guts as all
the pigeons fly off, are extremely distressed

01:37:21.120 --> 01:37:22.800
by this until Laughy shows up.

01:37:22.800 --> 01:37:27.360
Some people say that this episode is too similar
to Squirrels and that the show was starting

01:37:27.360 --> 01:37:31.680
to get stale at this point, but I think the
joke is unique enough to stand on its own.

01:37:31.680 --> 01:37:36.000
Plus, with a prank show as long-running as
Laugh, it's inevitable that you're gonna see

01:37:36.000 --> 01:37:37.500
some jokes get reused.

01:37:37.500 --> 01:37:42.300
Really, it's not about the prank itself, it's
about the reactions of the people, which are

01:37:42.300 --> 01:37:43.200
always priceless!

01:37:43.200 --> 01:37:46.860
And man, the way that Laughy McLaugherson
comes into the frame by climbing out of the

01:37:46.860 --> 01:37:48.120
old lady's open ribcage?

01:37:48.120 --> 01:37:49.980
I was rolling on the floor!

01:37:49.980 --> 01:37:53.400
Oh, you don't think the old “animals exploding
out of people” bit is funny?

01:37:54.060 --> 01:37:55.140
That's fair enough.

01:37:55.800 --> 01:37:58.920
Let me think - what are some of my other favorite
episodes of Laugh is Fun?

01:37:58.920 --> 01:38:03.060
Oh - the Margaret Thatcher episode from season
21!

01:38:03.060 --> 01:38:06.900
That's another great thing about Laugh is
Fun, it's not afraid to get a little political.

01:38:06.900 --> 01:38:11.100
So, this woman walks into her kitchen and
opens the cabinet, but instead of food being

01:38:11.100 --> 01:38:13.140
in there, it's just a big blob of flesh.

01:38:13.140 --> 01:38:16.920
The flesh blob rolls out onto the counter
and morphs into a misshapen caricature of

01:38:16.920 --> 01:38:19.260
Margaret Thatcher with a disproportionately
big head.

01:38:19.260 --> 01:38:23.040
The woman starts screaming, but the Thatcher
creature jumps onto her, pinning her down

01:38:23.040 --> 01:38:24.720
and shoving its tongue into her mouth.

01:38:24.720 --> 01:38:28.680
The deeper the tongue goes, the more Margaret
Thatcher faces start appearing all over the

01:38:28.680 --> 01:38:29.280
woman's skin.

01:38:29.280 --> 01:38:34.020
All of the faces start reciting Thatcher's
April 1986 speech on the bombing of Libya

01:38:34.020 --> 01:38:34.980
in perfect unison.

01:38:34.980 --> 01:38:38.640
You might watch it and think the woman's going
to choke based on how long the creature's

01:38:38.640 --> 01:38:43.440
tongue is down her throat, but good ol' Laughy
comes in, and the studio audience goes nuts!

01:38:43.440 --> 01:38:48.180
Laughy points out the cameras, says “You're
on Laugh is Fun!” and the woman starts laughing

01:38:48.180 --> 01:38:49.980
too, with the creature's tongue still in her
mouth.

01:38:49.980 --> 01:38:55.200
But I think my favorite prank I've ever seen
on Laugh is Fun was this one from season 37.

01:38:55.200 --> 01:38:56.640
So, it's in a hospital.

01:38:56.640 --> 01:38:59.700
There's a woman getting a c-section and all
the doctors are gathered around.

01:38:59.700 --> 01:39:04.020
One of the doctors makes a comment on how
much hair the baby has, then he screams and

01:39:04.020 --> 01:39:05.160
drops his surgical tools.

01:39:05.160 --> 01:39:08.880
The rest of the team all start screaming as
well, and then- this is where it gets really

01:39:08.880 --> 01:39:13.620
funny- you see Ryan Seacrest's head pop up
out from the woman.

01:39:14.760 --> 01:39:18.420
This is causing her a lot of pain, obviously,
so she starts screaming and crying as the

01:39:18.420 --> 01:39:23.100
Ryan Seacrest head fully emerges, at which
point you can tell that it's his head on an

01:39:23.100 --> 01:39:23.940
octopus' body!

01:39:23.940 --> 01:39:25.560
Classic bait-and-switch, right?

01:39:25.560 --> 01:39:30.480
But that's not the end- the Ryan Seacrest
octopus starts singing 'Row Row Row Your Boat'

01:39:30.480 --> 01:39:34.680
as it emerges, then three more octopi with
celebrities' heads come out of this woman.

01:39:34.680 --> 01:39:38.700
There's one that looks like Jack Nicholson,
one that looks like Johnny Cash, and one who

01:39:38.700 --> 01:39:42.420
looks like Martin Freeman, and when they've
all come out of her they start singing together

01:39:42.420 --> 01:39:43.320
in four-part harmony.

01:39:43.320 --> 01:39:44.760
It's hilarious!

01:39:44.760 --> 01:39:48.240
Laughy walks in to point out the cameras,
and all the doctors start laughing.

01:39:48.240 --> 01:39:50.520
Even the woman on the operating table is laughing.

01:39:50.520 --> 01:39:52.680
In fact, she laughs so hard she passes out!

01:39:52.680 --> 01:39:56.520
It's a real shame that there are never any
end credits because it would be so cool to

01:39:56.520 --> 01:39:58.440
find out more about the people who make this
show.

01:39:58.440 --> 01:40:02.520
I mean, they're total comedic geniuses, and
special effects wizards, too.

01:40:02.520 --> 01:40:04.920
I couldn't even guess how they do some of
those pranks!

01:40:04.920 --> 01:40:09.000
But sadly, there's very little information
about the show online, and you can't even

01:40:09.000 --> 01:40:12.720
ask the victims of the pranks about it, because
all of them are dead.

01:40:12.720 --> 01:40:13.200
What?

01:40:13.200 --> 01:40:15.660
No, no, not because of Laugh is Fun!

01:40:15.660 --> 01:40:17.880
That's just a prank show, it's all jokes.

01:40:17.880 --> 01:40:21.600
They always show everyone laughing and perfectly
okay at the end, no matter how badly they

01:40:21.600 --> 01:40:22.800
got mutilated in the episode.

01:40:22.800 --> 01:40:27.120
You can even see some people who had been
on previous episodes among the studio audience

01:40:27.120 --> 01:40:28.020
if you look hard enough!

01:40:28.020 --> 01:40:34.320
But, by total coincidence, everyone who features
on Laugh is Fun eventually dies, or is already

01:40:34.320 --> 01:40:36.900
dead, of totally unrelated causes.

01:40:36.900 --> 01:40:41.880
Like the Turman family- the dad, Gary Turman,
was the one who had 18 extra heads grow out

01:40:41.880 --> 01:40:42.300
of his neck.

01:40:42.300 --> 01:40:46.980
Gary, his wife Lindsey, and their two kids
tragically passed away in a car wreck on the

01:40:46.980 --> 01:40:48.780
28th of April, 1989.

01:40:48.780 --> 01:40:53.160
Melissa and Travis Englund- the couple from
the squirrel prank went missing and were last

01:40:53.160 --> 01:40:55.380
seen on the 12th of May, 1989.

01:40:55.380 --> 01:41:00.080
The couple from the pigeon prank- Macey Gersham
and Kyle Parker- were both killed by a hit

01:41:00.080 --> 01:41:02.100
and run driver in September of 2000.

01:41:02.100 --> 01:41:06.000
Doris Carter, the woman from the Thatcher
prank, passed away from ovarian cancer in

01:41:06.000 --> 01:41:11.220
1997, and Rebecca Nash, the woman who gave
birth to all those singing celebrity cephalopods,

01:41:11.220 --> 01:41:13.380
died in childbirth in 2013.

01:41:13.380 --> 01:41:18.180
You might think that last one had something
to do with her Laugh is Fun episode, due to

01:41:18.180 --> 01:41:22.020
the hospital connection, but the surgeons
present when she died didn't report anything

01:41:22.020 --> 01:41:23.520
strange happening at the time.

01:41:23.520 --> 01:41:27.720
All of these deaths happen in roughly the
same years that all of these people had their

01:41:27.720 --> 01:41:32.520
appearances on Laugh, but because it's only
available on streaming and video, it can't

01:41:32.520 --> 01:41:36.540
really be said what the connection is since
there aren't air dates for any of the episodes.

01:41:36.540 --> 01:41:41.400
While you might assume that these people died
after their appearances on Laugh, and the

01:41:41.400 --> 01:41:45.540
show is actually some kind of death curse,
it's just as likely that they died before,

01:41:45.540 --> 01:41:49.560
and Laugh is Fun is somehow filmed and distributed
from the great beyond.

01:41:49.560 --> 01:41:54.000
Some people even think that the people who
film Laugh actually kidnap all the people

01:41:54.000 --> 01:41:58.860
who appear on the show and fake their deaths
in some kind of big sinister conspiracy.

01:41:58.860 --> 01:42:03.480
Sure, I have heard that all of the people
who supposedly 'died' after being on the show

01:42:03.480 --> 01:42:07.680
have inconsistencies in their death records
and that many of their graves actually just

01:42:07.680 --> 01:42:11.880
hold empty coffins, but still, that feels
totally unrealistic to me.

01:42:11.880 --> 01:42:16.980
I mean, come on, can you really picture TV's
own Laughy Laugherson kidnapping anyone?

01:42:16.980 --> 01:42:18.600
He has such a friendly attitude!

01:42:18.600 --> 01:42:24.180
Like I said before, no information whatsoever
exists about the way this show is made.

01:42:24.180 --> 01:42:29.100
Not even the SCP foundation has any answers
- they're still actively investigating the

01:42:29.100 --> 01:42:33.660
ways in which people get chosen to be on Laugh
is Fun, as well as where it's filmed, and

01:42:33.660 --> 01:42:34.560
who produces it.

01:42:34.560 --> 01:42:36.600
Talk about a really niche cult hit!

01:42:36.600 --> 01:42:38.640
I hope I've sold you on Laugh is Fun.

01:42:38.640 --> 01:42:44.100
It may not be that well known, and you might
have trouble finding it, but trust me- it's

01:42:44.100 --> 01:42:46.620
the funniest prank show you'll probably ever
watch.

01:42:46.620 --> 01:42:51.300
I guarantee, once you get into it, it'll make
you laugh, and laugh, and laugh.

01:42:51.300 --> 01:42:52.320
Laugh with us!

01:42:52.320 --> 01:42:53.460
Laugh with us!

01:42:53.460 --> 01:42:54.720
Laugh with us!

01:42:54.720 --> 01:42:56.280
Laugh with us!

01:42:56.280 --> 01:42:58.560
Have you ever wanted to be a cartoon?

01:42:58.560 --> 01:43:01.260
They have it easier than we do, that’s for
sure.

01:43:01.260 --> 01:43:06.900
The characters in your favorite shows never
seem to experience boring days, and the world

01:43:06.900 --> 01:43:10.560
around them always seems so wonderful and
imaginative.

01:43:10.560 --> 01:43:14.100
Animation is an idealized, stylized form of&nbsp;

01:43:14.100 --> 01:43:17.700
reality, where anything can&nbsp;
happen, and entertainment

01:43:17.700 --> 01:43:20.580
is at the forefront of the lived experience.

01:43:20.580 --> 01:43:25.140
If you’re understanding the appeal behind
what we’re talking about, you’ll definitely

01:43:25.140 --> 01:43:32.100
be interested in hearing about SCP-6080, a
strange anomaly known as Eric’s Cartoon

01:43:32.100 --> 01:43:32.640
Box.

01:43:32.640 --> 01:43:38.940
The story of Eric’s Cartoon Box is elaborate
and complex, but catalogs one of the Foundation’s

01:43:38.940 --> 01:43:42.360
most imaginative and surreal anomalies on
file.

01:43:42.360 --> 01:43:48.600
It started on Parawatch.net, a message board
dedicated to the supernatural and paranormal

01:43:48.600 --> 01:43:51.840
that the Foundation likes to keep tabs on
in their spare time.

01:43:51.840 --> 01:43:56.520
While sifting through countless fake posts
about being abducted by aliens or hunting

01:43:56.520 --> 01:44:01.140
for bigfoot, the Foundation noticed something
less conventional that caught their eye.

01:44:01.140 --> 01:44:07.620
A series of posts that talked about a “cursed”
VHS tape of the mid-90s classic Nickelodeon

01:44:07.620 --> 01:44:10.140
cartoon “Rocko’s Modern Life”.

01:44:10.140 --> 01:44:15.060
Now, a cursed cartoon seems like your standard
internet horror fiction fare, something the

01:44:15.060 --> 01:44:19.980
Foundation could easily dismiss as a story
crafted by an amateur author, but what caught

01:44:19.980 --> 01:44:25.380
their attention were the pictures of strange
entities that appeared as a result of watching

01:44:25.380 --> 01:44:29.880
the tape, as well as information on how the
original poster obtained the tape.

01:44:29.880 --> 01:44:31.800
It seemed oddly specific.

01:44:31.800 --> 01:44:36.120
A tape purchased on eBay through a seller
named “tooncollector”.

01:44:36.120 --> 01:44:41.280
The Foundation conducted a brief investigation,
and found that “tooncollector” was a resident

01:44:41.280 --> 01:44:47.400
of Bakersfield, California named Jacob Sawyer,
who had gone missing some time in the past.

01:44:47.400 --> 01:44:54.180
Inside his house, the Foundation found several
VHS tapes and DVD cases, as well as a television.

01:44:54.180 --> 01:45:01.080
A hard drive on Sawyer’s computer contained
161 hours of brief, edited videos, all reviewing

01:45:01.080 --> 01:45:03.300
and commentating on various cartoons.

01:45:03.300 --> 01:45:08.700
Seems like Sawyer had a video essay hobby,
but the Foundation was unable to locate any

01:45:08.700 --> 01:45:11.460
of this content on major video hosting sites.

01:45:11.460 --> 01:45:16.380
In Sawyer’s basement, the Foundation discovered
a large, worn-out cardboard box.

01:45:16.380 --> 01:45:22.140
On its right side were the words “Eric’s
Cartoon Box”, written in black sharpie.

01:45:22.140 --> 01:45:27.120
On its front was a simplistic smiley face,
also drawn in sharpie.

01:45:27.120 --> 01:45:31.920
Originally, the Foundation thought it was
just an ordinary box, but the object quickly

01:45:31.920 --> 01:45:38.100
proved itself to be alive, capable of speaking
despite a lack of vocal cords and also possessing

01:45:38.100 --> 01:45:42.360
a degree of mobility, propelling itself short
distances to move.

01:45:42.360 --> 01:45:48.660
When the Foundation first came across SCP-6080
in Sawyer’s basement, the object was clearly

01:45:48.660 --> 01:45:49.320
distressed.

01:45:49.320 --> 01:45:55.920
SCP-6080 and all of the other items found
were taken into Foundation custody, and moved

01:45:55.920 --> 01:46:01.860
to Site-433, where they would be researched
by Dr. Rowan Raster, one of the Foundation’s

01:46:01.860 --> 01:46:06.300
leading figures in the study of animated anomalous
media and art.

01:46:06.300 --> 01:46:12.720
Raster’s research found that SCP-6080, when
emotionally distressed, could bend reality

01:46:12.720 --> 01:46:15.960
to alter their surroundings,&nbsp;
which were transformed&nbsp;

01:46:15.960 --> 01:46:17.940
into reflections of its psyche.

01:46:17.940 --> 01:46:24.360
Raster discovered that there were a few ways
to pacify SCP-6080, but the most practical

01:46:24.360 --> 01:46:27.240
method was to evoke a sense of nostalgia in&nbsp;

01:46:27.240 --> 01:46:29.940
the object by placing it&nbsp;
in a familiar environment.

01:46:29.940 --> 01:46:37.380
To ensure that the box is kept passive, SCP-6080
is kept inside a containment chamber resembling

01:46:37.380 --> 01:46:42.480
a child’s bedroom, an environment SCP-6080
finds comforting.

01:46:42.480 --> 01:46:49.380
On top of their reality-bending abilities,
SCP-6080 also produces DVDs and VHS tapes,

01:46:49.380 --> 01:46:51.840
labeled SCP-6080-1.

01:46:51.840 --> 01:46:56.940
The Foundation would later discover that the
tapes found within Sawyer’s home and the

01:46:56.940 --> 01:47:03.480
“cursed” Rocko’s Modern Life tape discussed
on Parawatch.net were all products of SCP-6080.

01:47:03.480 --> 01:47:07.920
But what’s so special about the cartoon
reproductions made by SCP-6080?

01:47:07.920 --> 01:47:12.480
On the outside, they’re unaltered copies
of animated children’s television shows

01:47:12.480 --> 01:47:17.040
and films, and their contents are exactly
the same as their ordinary counterparts.

01:47:17.040 --> 01:47:23.400
However, once an instance of SCP-6080-1 is
viewed, the subject watching it will experience

01:47:23.400 --> 01:47:29.220
a reality-altering anomalous experience dubbed
by the research team as a “Rerun Event”.

01:47:29.220 --> 01:47:34.740
Sometimes, the Rerun Event occurs immediately,
though research has shown that as much time

01:47:34.740 --> 01:47:37.860
as 3 weeks can pass before one occurs.

01:47:37.860 --> 01:47:43.860
Rerun events often reflect visual or thematic
elements of the media that induced them, and

01:47:43.860 --> 01:47:49.200
their severity ranges from inconsequential
to dangerous and harmful to their subjects.

01:47:49.200 --> 01:47:54.480
Because of the variety found in a Rerun Event’s
content, it’s difficult to generalize them,

01:47:54.480 --> 01:47:58.080
but we’ll be sure to get into the specifics
of a few later on.

01:47:58.080 --> 01:48:00.480
Still, even if the Event isn’t immediately&nbsp;

01:48:00.480 --> 01:48:03.120
dangerous to the subject,&nbsp;
the Foundation discovered

01:48:03.120 --> 01:48:08.580
that a Rerun Event may leave involved persons
with lingering physical and psychological

01:48:08.580 --> 01:48:14.700
alterations, some of which are unable to be
fully alleviated or treated, even with amnestics.

01:48:14.700 --> 01:48:21.420
Soon after SCP-6080 was brought into Site-433
and the immediate characteristics of the anomaly

01:48:21.420 --> 01:48:26.220
were recorded, an interview between the box
and Researcher Rowan Raster was held.

01:48:26.220 --> 01:48:32.700
Rowan tried to make sure the anomaly was as
comfortable as possible, but SCP-6080 continued

01:48:32.700 --> 01:48:36.840
to shuffle against the floor rapidly, unsettled
in their new environment.

01:48:36.840 --> 01:48:43.200
SCP-6080 wanted to get out, and the object
continued to stress themselves out, to the

01:48:43.200 --> 01:48:45.960
point where its speech degraded&nbsp;
into unintelligibility,&nbsp;

01:48:45.960 --> 01:48:48.360
the lights in the containment chamber began

01:48:48.360 --> 01:48:53.400
flickering on and off, and the face on the
front of the box rapidly flipped between different

01:48:53.400 --> 01:48:54.300
expressions.

01:48:54.300 --> 01:49:01.320
Rowan continued trying to get through to SCP-6080,
and eventually they began to speak.They said

01:49:01.320 --> 01:49:05.100
that they were scared, and that they missed
someone that they called Eric.

01:49:05.100 --> 01:49:10.980
SCP-6080, once calmed down, explained that
Eric was the center of their universe.

01:49:10.980 --> 01:49:17.280
In their own words, SCP-6080 felt, when it
first started existing, that their sole purpose

01:49:17.280 --> 01:49:20.580
for being here on earth was to exist for Eric.

01:49:20.580 --> 01:49:25.920
They remembered waking up in a bedroom filled
with toys and posters, and immediately knowing

01:49:25.920 --> 01:49:30.600
the feeling that they knew what they were
made for and who they were made by.

01:49:30.600 --> 01:49:36.960
At first, this was overwhelming, and SCP-6080
began to freak out, but Eric showed concern,

01:49:36.960 --> 01:49:40.140
and the box realized that they didn’t want
to upset him.

01:49:40.140 --> 01:49:46.080
The most important thing for SCP-6080 was
to not upset Eric.

01:49:46.080 --> 01:49:52.140
When Raster asked if they could describe Eric,
the box was unable to, and said that whenever

01:49:52.140 --> 01:49:56.100
they tried to talk about Eric’s appearance,
people became confused.

01:49:56.100 --> 01:50:01.860
They said that Eric’s skin was blue, and
that he had a purple nose, with dark red eyes

01:50:01.860 --> 01:50:05.040
and four thin strands of hair on the top of
his head.

01:50:05.040 --> 01:50:09.720
From the way they described Eric, he sounded
exactly like a cartoon character.

01:50:09.720 --> 01:50:16.020
Raster asked if this was how SCP-6080 always
saw people, and that seemed to be the case.

01:50:16.020 --> 01:50:22.080
SCP-6080 then explained how they used their
abilities to make Eric happy, namely through

01:50:22.080 --> 01:50:27.240
producing tapes of Eric’s favorite cartoons
and then playing inside the worlds of the

01:50:27.240 --> 01:50:27.900
shows together.

01:50:27.900 --> 01:50:29.940
And this continued for a while.

01:50:29.940 --> 01:50:36.360
SCP-6080 and Eric would meet the characters
from their favorite shows and adventure through

01:50:36.360 --> 01:50:39.600
their worlds, and the two developed a powerful
bond.

01:50:39.600 --> 01:50:44.340
But eventually, as SCP-6080 explained, Eric
left.

01:50:44.340 --> 01:50:48.120
One day, the box woke up, and their friend
was gone.

01:50:48.120 --> 01:50:53.640
SCP-6080 even searched for Eric’s friends,
but found that they were gone too.

01:50:53.640 --> 01:50:58.800
Without Eric, SCP-6080 was alone, and they
panicked.

01:50:58.800 --> 01:51:03.660
They ran outside the house and began asking
anyone they could find if they knew where

01:51:03.660 --> 01:51:04.320
Eric went.

01:51:04.320 --> 01:51:08.400
Naturally, the people SCP-6080 spoke to were
terrified.

01:51:08.400 --> 01:51:13.320
And SCP-6080 quickly became overwhelmed by
the chaos of the outside world.

01:51:13.320 --> 01:51:16.320
And then they found Jacob Sawyer.

01:51:16.320 --> 01:51:21.420
Sawyer was, at first, just as scared of SCP-6080
as everyone else was.

01:51:21.420 --> 01:51:28.500
But once SCP-6080 opened its flaps and revealed
a trove of old tapes, Sawyer smiled to himself,

01:51:28.500 --> 01:51:33.840
and told the box that it would be “useful”,
before picking them up and taking them to

01:51:33.840 --> 01:51:34.320
his house.

01:51:34.320 --> 01:51:38.460
SCP-6080 was scared, and attempted to flee
from Sawyer.

01:51:38.460 --> 01:51:42.180
The more time the box spent with Sawyer, the
sicker it felt.

01:51:42.180 --> 01:51:47.940
When Sawyer threw the box in the empty, white
walls of his basement, SCP-6080 was forced

01:51:47.940 --> 01:51:51.300
by Jacob to open themselves up and produce
tapes.

01:51:51.300 --> 01:51:54.420
For months, this is how SCP-6080 lived.

01:51:54.420 --> 01:51:59.040
Alone, in a white-walled room, with white
floors and white ceilings.

01:51:59.040 --> 01:52:05.040
Being forced open and used as a dispenser
for the tapes, which Sawyer would sell online.

01:52:05.040 --> 01:52:10.800
SCP-6080 stopped their account, and began
crying a substance that resembled blueberry

01:52:10.800 --> 01:52:11.400
syrup.

01:52:11.400 --> 01:52:16.800
Raster put a stop to the interview, realizing
that the object was clearly experiencing a

01:52:16.800 --> 01:52:17.880
form of trauma.

01:52:17.880 --> 01:52:22.860
After a break, Raster asked SCP-6080 if they
knew what happened to Sawyer.

01:52:22.860 --> 01:52:29.160
The box explained that one day, Sawyer rushed
down into the basement, and demanded SCP-6080

01:52:29.160 --> 01:52:31.860
to explain what it was doing to the cartoons
it made.

01:52:31.860 --> 01:52:36.960
Apparently, some of the buyers were realizing
that SCP-6080’s tapes were anything but

01:52:36.960 --> 01:52:37.260
normal.

01:52:37.260 --> 01:52:41.940
Sawyer brutalized the box, and pulled a DVD
from inside them.

01:52:41.940 --> 01:52:48.120
But when Sawyer watched the DVD for himself,
all SCP-6080 heard from beneath the living

01:52:48.120 --> 01:52:52.320
room was a sharp burst of static and a horrible
scream.

01:52:52.320 --> 01:52:54.720
After that, Jacob was gone.

01:52:54.720 --> 01:52:59.880
SCP-6080 couldn’t recall what tape they
produced that caused Sawyer to disappear,

01:52:59.880 --> 01:53:04.320
nor was one matching the box’s description
found inside the house.

01:53:04.320 --> 01:53:10.620
After the interview, the Foundation began
its testing with SCP-6080 inside its containment

01:53:10.620 --> 01:53:11.100
chamber.

01:53:11.100 --> 01:53:17.400
SCP-6080 would produce a tape, a D-Class personnel
would watch it, and the ensuing “Rerun Event”

01:53:17.400 --> 01:53:18.540
was recorded.

01:53:18.540 --> 01:53:25.020
During testing, SCP-6080 was escorted from
the room so as to not cause it emotional distress.

01:53:25.020 --> 01:53:27.660
Most of these tests were routine.

01:53:27.660 --> 01:53:33.900
A D-Class would watch an episode of a cartoon,
such as ChalkZone or the Rugrats, and strange,

01:53:33.900 --> 01:53:35.820
reality-altering events would occur.

01:53:35.820 --> 01:53:37.980
For example, after watching an episode of&nbsp;

01:53:37.980 --> 01:53:40.740
the Rugrats where Chuckie had&nbsp;
difficulty differentiating

01:53:40.740 --> 01:53:47.340
his dreams from reality, D-1711 experienced
a Rerun Event in which he was disoriented.

01:53:47.340 --> 01:53:52.800
D-1711 claimed everything in the room looked
“tall”, and that they needed to sleep

01:53:52.800 --> 01:53:54.540
off the effects of the anomaly.

01:53:54.540 --> 01:54:00.480
After laying in the bed, the chamber’s lights
turned blue and purple, and a humanoid entity

01:54:00.480 --> 01:54:05.580
wearing a researcher’s outfit was seen facing
the corner of the room, against the wall.

01:54:05.580 --> 01:54:10.320
The figure disappeared as quickly as it appeared,
with laughter heard in the distance.

01:54:10.320 --> 01:54:16.200
As soon as the figure was gone, D-1711 jolted
awake, and began screaming.

01:54:16.200 --> 01:54:19.440
After this, the researcher&nbsp;
observing the experiment&nbsp;

01:54:19.440 --> 01:54:22.620
rushed in, and like a concerned parent, began

01:54:22.620 --> 01:54:24.480
comforting D-1711.

01:54:24.480 --> 01:54:29.520
The researcher assured the D-Class that “nothing
in a dream could hurt them, because it isn’t

01:54:29.520 --> 01:54:34.560
real” which was a direct quote from the
Rugrats episode D-1711 watched.

01:54:34.560 --> 01:54:39.120
After this, the D-Class and the researcher
came out of their trance state, wondering

01:54:39.120 --> 01:54:42.720
how they got inside the containment chamber
and what they were doing there.

01:54:42.720 --> 01:54:49.020
The Rerun event had finished, and D-1711 explained
that while they were asleep, they dreamed

01:54:49.020 --> 01:54:52.380
events similar to the dreams shown in the
Rugrats episode.

01:54:52.380 --> 01:54:57.300
This is just one example of how odd and surreal
rerun events can be.

01:54:57.300 --> 01:55:02.460
Another test involving an obscure cartoon
by the name of “King”, resulted in the

01:55:02.460 --> 01:55:08.160
entirety of Site-433 undergoing changes that
made it look as if it were a location from

01:55:08.160 --> 01:55:09.120
the show itself.

01:55:09.120 --> 01:55:14.280
However, the most notable rerun event occurred
when the Foundation tested a tape of “Cartoon

01:55:14.280 --> 01:55:19.740
All Stars to the Rescue”, a special featuring
cartoon characters from various shows banding

01:55:19.740 --> 01:55:23.160
together to talk about the dangers of children
using drugs.

01:55:23.160 --> 01:55:29.880
In order to conduct this test, D-9926 was
sent to a quarantined community away from

01:55:29.880 --> 01:55:36.120
Site-433, with only a helmet-mounted camera
and a communication headset. 10 hours after

01:55:36.120 --> 01:55:42.660
watching the tape, D-9926 slept inside a bedroom
of the vacant building the Foundation had

01:55:42.660 --> 01:55:43.380
brought him to.

01:55:43.380 --> 01:55:48.000
He was woken by the sound of the doorknob
jittering, and then several knocks.

01:55:48.000 --> 01:55:51.660
D-9926 got up from the bed and opened the&nbsp;

01:55:51.660 --> 01:55:54.660
door to find four crude-looking&nbsp;
entities resembling

01:55:54.660 --> 01:55:56.400
cartoon characters from the special.

01:55:56.400 --> 01:56:01.080
One was Kermit the Frog, who was puppeteered
by a man in a gray hoodie.

01:56:01.080 --> 01:56:03.900
Another was a cardboard cutout of Bugs Bunny.

01:56:03.900 --> 01:56:09.180
There was a tall, mascot-like Garfield costume,
and a sentient ALF plush.

01:56:09.180 --> 01:56:15.780
The four entities crowded D-9926 and began
singing to him in a shrill, high pitched voice

01:56:15.780 --> 01:56:18.360
a song about the dangers of doing drugs.

01:56:18.360 --> 01:56:25.020
D-9926 was confused, but the trance induced
by the anomaly made it difficult for him to

01:56:25.020 --> 01:56:25.500
think straight.

01:56:25.500 --> 01:56:30.720
Then, from the open windowsill in the room
came a black boot attached to a long leg.

01:56:30.720 --> 01:56:36.120
A fifth entity made its way into the room,
crawling through the windowsill.

01:56:36.120 --> 01:56:41.640
It was a tall, gray-skinned humanoid with
wrinkled trousers, a creased shirt, and a

01:56:41.640 --> 01:56:42.420
large necktie.

01:56:42.420 --> 01:56:48.300
As much as D-9926 could understand, the gray
entity was not from a cartoon.

01:56:48.300 --> 01:56:55.140
The gray entity signaled D-9926 towards them,
and the two left the building and the strange

01:56:55.140 --> 01:56:56.760
cartoon entities behind.

01:56:56.760 --> 01:57:02.700
Outside, the environment was strange, it was
dark out, with only dim street lights illuminating

01:57:02.700 --> 01:57:05.460
the spaces between trees at a low angle.

01:57:05.460 --> 01:57:10.500
The asphalt path the duo walked on slowly
transitioned to a field of grass.

01:57:10.500 --> 01:57:15.600
The gray entity explained that the very nature
of the environment was built around the message

01:57:15.600 --> 01:57:20.820
of the cartoon, which was trying to get D-9926
to avoid using drugs.

01:57:20.820 --> 01:57:25.140
Somehow, D-9926 needed to find a way to get
out.

01:57:25.140 --> 01:57:31.560
As the two moved through the forest area,
D-9926 and the Gray Entity continued to recognize

01:57:31.560 --> 01:57:32.640
something was wrong.

01:57:32.640 --> 01:57:39.060
Gray explained that it didn’t understand
where they were either, and that when D-9926’s

01:57:39.060 --> 01:57:45.000
rerun event began altering reality, it was
pulled away from the place it regularly existed,

01:57:45.000 --> 01:57:47.040
and into reality.

01:57:47.040 --> 01:57:52.320
D-9926 did not understand, but he was short
on options.

01:57:52.320 --> 01:57:57.600
Both of them smelled something horrible, and
the area around them was starting to resemble

01:57:57.600 --> 01:57:59.460
the real world less and less.

01:57:59.460 --> 01:58:02.640
The sky above the forest was smokey orange-red,&nbsp;

01:58:02.640 --> 01:58:07.260
and in the distance, D-9926&nbsp;
could see a luminescent

01:58:07.260 --> 01:58:11.040
object resembling a window recessed into a
concrete wall.

01:58:11.040 --> 01:58:16.560
The two also heard music, and as they moved
through this dream-like world, they came across

01:58:16.560 --> 01:58:20.700
the building in the distance, which was small
enough for them to climb the roof of.

01:58:20.700 --> 01:58:26.100
On the roof, there appeared to be a parking
lot, and beyond it, more forest, as if they

01:58:26.100 --> 01:58:28.680
never elevated from the ground in the first
place.

01:58:28.680 --> 01:58:30.480
The two continued walking.

01:58:30.480 --> 01:58:36.120
As the parking lot sloped downwards, they
recognized what appeared to be a city in the

01:58:36.120 --> 01:58:36.300
distance.

01:58:36.300 --> 01:58:41.160
D-9926 knew that with the altered state of
reality, this city was going to be anything

01:58:41.160 --> 01:58:44.520
but normal, but he felt compelled to keep
moving.

01:58:44.520 --> 01:58:47.220
It was the only thing the two of them could
do.

01:58:47.220 --> 01:58:53.460
D-9926 and the Gray Entity began talking about
the nature of the reality they found themselves

01:58:53.460 --> 01:58:58.320
in, and the entity explained that it felt
that trusting its gut and exploring anything

01:58:58.320 --> 01:59:03.600
that stood out was probably the right thing
to do in a place that made little actual sense.

01:59:03.600 --> 01:59:08.940
They concluded that the location they were
inside had some sort of feelings and goals,

01:59:08.940 --> 01:59:14.340
and they just had to go along with its own
internal logic in order to find a way out.

01:59:14.340 --> 01:59:18.060
In the distance, the red lights of the city
twinkled in the darkness.

01:59:18.060 --> 01:59:19.500
They continued walking.

01:59:19.500 --> 01:59:24.540
After about 30 minutes, the red lights of
the city illuminated all of their surroundings,

01:59:24.540 --> 01:59:29.760
and they began to see the edifices of concrete
buildings on the other side of the road they

01:59:29.760 --> 01:59:30.480
were walking on.

01:59:30.480 --> 01:59:37.800
D-9926 didn’t notice them at first, but
when the entity pointed them out, D-9926 realized

01:59:37.800 --> 01:59:42.600
they were standing in the middle of a mass
of buildings, with rubble and litter strewn

01:59:42.600 --> 01:59:44.100
on the streets beneath them.

01:59:44.100 --> 01:59:46.620
Gray wondered if they could use them as shelter.

01:59:46.620 --> 01:59:51.780
The two didn’t realize they were being watched
by a figure in the distance.

01:59:51.780 --> 01:59:55.200
D-9926 was still confused.

01:59:55.200 --> 02:00:00.660
The city was empty, Gray seemed oddly optimistic
about their situation, and things weren’t

02:00:00.660 --> 02:00:03.780
looking like they were going to return to
normal any time soon.

02:00:03.780 --> 02:00:08.700
Gray commented that they were somewhat interested
in exploring a location different from the

02:00:08.700 --> 02:00:11.220
realities and dreams they were used to exploring.

02:00:11.220 --> 02:00:16.860
After minutes of nothing, the duo came across
a dead body, laying on top of a rusted metal

02:00:16.860 --> 02:00:17.640
wheelbarrow.

02:00:17.640 --> 02:00:23.820
D-9926, who was familiar with seeing sights
like these, sighed and continued moving.

02:00:23.820 --> 02:00:28.800
Gray told him he could simply walk away, into
the darkness, where they could forget about

02:00:28.800 --> 02:00:30.960
this place and emerge somewhere else.

02:00:30.960 --> 02:00:35.580
D-9926 wondered if the entity really had that
power.

02:00:35.580 --> 02:00:38.640
They came across more bodies, and continued
forward.

02:00:38.640 --> 02:00:45.420
If this entity could really whisk D-9926 away
to another place, he was seriously considering

02:00:45.420 --> 02:00:45.600
it.

02:00:45.600 --> 02:00:49.440
Life as a D-Class was unimaginably dehumanizing.

02:00:49.440 --> 02:00:53.280
The figure watching the two of them continued
to observe from a rooftop.

02:00:53.280 --> 02:00:58.500
Suddenly, a large sheet metal shelving unit
fell from the overhanging roof of a building,

02:00:58.500 --> 02:01:01.080
tipped from its edge by the observing figure.

02:01:01.080 --> 02:01:06.000
The shelving unit hit Gray directly, and severely
injured the entity on impact.

02:01:06.000 --> 02:01:08.520
D-9926 ran.

02:01:08.520 --> 02:01:13.740
He ran through the buildings, only catching
glimpses of the orange figure wielding a thick

02:01:13.740 --> 02:01:16.560
pipe running across the rooftops beside him.

02:01:16.560 --> 02:01:22.140
The chase continued through the insides of
the buildings, and once D-9926 caught a glimpse

02:01:22.140 --> 02:01:24.660
of the pursuing figure, he ran back out.

02:01:24.660 --> 02:01:30.420
The entire time, he heard the figure’s high
pitched breathing audibly chasing after him.

02:01:30.420 --> 02:01:36.240
More bodies, more red lighting, an endless
city, all while being pursued.

02:01:36.240 --> 02:01:42.540
D-9926 found rest inside a warehouse, which
he ran through in an attempt to escape the

02:01:42.540 --> 02:01:42.780
entity.

02:01:42.780 --> 02:01:49.260
At the far side of the warehouse was a room
resembling SCP-6080’s containment chamber,

02:01:49.260 --> 02:01:51.840
but heavily damaged and littered with trash.

02:01:51.840 --> 02:01:57.060
The television set in the room switched on,
showing an all white room, similar to the

02:01:57.060 --> 02:01:59.880
basement SCP-6080 was found in.

02:01:59.880 --> 02:02:06.480
The door to the room was about to be opened,
and D-9926 screamed, before smashing the TV

02:02:06.480 --> 02:02:08.820
with a book he threw from across the room.

02:02:08.820 --> 02:02:12.840
Upon shattering the screen, the camera feed
went dark.

02:02:12.840 --> 02:02:19.140
When it resumed, D-9926 found himself in a
red, misty area, with the figure that had

02:02:19.140 --> 02:02:21.660
been pursuing him standing in front of him.

02:02:21.660 --> 02:02:26.760
The two stared at each other before the figure
lunged forward at D-9926.

02:02:26.760 --> 02:02:32.580
But it then suddenly stopped, and the voice
of SCP-6080 was heard.

02:02:32.580 --> 02:02:37.440
The box called out to the figure, which it
recognized as something that resembled Jacob

02:02:37.440 --> 02:02:37.980
Sawyer.

02:02:37.980 --> 02:02:43.320
SCP-6080 ordered the Sawyer-entity to leave
D-9926 alone.

02:02:43.320 --> 02:02:48.540
Except this time, SCP-6080 wasn’t a cardboard
box.

02:02:48.540 --> 02:02:54.660
They were a pale-skinned child, drawn like
a cartoon, wearing ordinary clothes.

02:02:54.660 --> 02:02:58.200
The box realized that this&nbsp;
place was a manifestation&nbsp;

02:02:58.200 --> 02:03:01.560
of its own traumatic experience as it slowly

02:03:01.560 --> 02:03:05.940
realized that the entity resembling Sawyer
was stuck inside its own mind.

02:03:05.940 --> 02:03:11.640
The box and the entity fought each other,
punching with large, cartoon-like fists.

02:03:11.640 --> 02:03:15.720
Until the Sawyer-entity fell to the ground,
coughing up blood.

02:03:15.720 --> 02:03:20.100
SCP-6080 realized that this wasn’t actually
Sawyer.

02:03:20.100 --> 02:03:25.320
Like everything, this was all a manifestation
of the damage that Sawyer caused.

02:03:25.320 --> 02:03:31.200
And with that, the entity left its own body,
flattening until all that was left was its

02:03:31.200 --> 02:03:31.740
empty skin.

02:03:31.740 --> 02:03:38.880
D-9926 thanked SCP-6080 for saving him, and
the two introduced themselves to one another.

02:03:38.880 --> 02:03:44.640
SCP-6080 offered to take D-9926 away from
the Foundation forever.

02:03:44.640 --> 02:03:49.680
He took off his recording equipment, and the
two walked away into the sunset.

02:03:49.680 --> 02:03:57.180
Inside SCP-6080’s containment chamber, the
object, and D-9926, were nowhere to be found.

02:03:57.180 --> 02:04:03.840
Instead, there was a single disc, containing
a message from SCP-6080 to the Foundation.

02:04:03.840 --> 02:04:09.180
SCP-6080 told the Foundation that they were
coming to terms with what they experienced

02:04:09.180 --> 02:04:13.320
in their life, and that for now, they were
in a place where they could exist without

02:04:13.320 --> 02:04:13.920
issue.

02:04:13.920 --> 02:04:20.400
Behind them was a playground, where SCP-6080
could act like a kid again, something they

02:04:20.400 --> 02:04:23.100
never got to experience as much as they wanted
to.

02:04:23.100 --> 02:04:30.120
They told the Foundation that D-9926 was safe,
and happy somewhere else, and that they shouldn’t

02:04:30.120 --> 02:04:33.000
look for either of them, because they’d
never find them.

02:04:33.000 --> 02:04:39.360
For now, all SCP-6080 needed to do was to
play on the swings, and live at peace with

02:04:39.360 --> 02:04:42.120
themselves, until they could grow up.

02:04:42.120 --> 02:04:46.620
Inside the disc that the message was contained
on was a sticky note that simply read:

02:04:46.620 --> 02:04:48.420
“I hope you understand.”

02:04:48.420 --> 02:04:51.840
Video games have come a long way over the
years.

02:04:51.840 --> 02:04:58.800
We've gone from the days of Atari and "Pong"
to virtual reality headsets, PS5s, and massively

02:04:58.800 --> 02:05:03.780
multiplayer online games where you can fight
horror movies villains, or wage war in a fantasy

02:05:03.780 --> 02:05:03.960
land.

02:05:03.960 --> 02:05:09.540
You can mine virtual minerals and avoid creepers
in "Minecraft," compete against people all

02:05:09.540 --> 02:05:13.440
over the world in "Fortnite" and do whatever
it is people do in "Roblox."

02:05:13.440 --> 02:05:19.080
Games used to be a pixelated hero battling
blobby monsters with no discernible facial

02:05:19.080 --> 02:05:24.060
features, and now they have graphics so detailed
you can see the freckles on a protagonist's

02:05:24.060 --> 02:05:27.000
face and each individual blade of grass.

02:05:27.000 --> 02:05:32.040
But there's one thing that hasn't changed,
no matter how much progress games may make:

02:05:32.040 --> 02:05:37.020
whenever a shiny new console comes out, everyone
wants to get their hands on it.

02:05:37.020 --> 02:05:42.720
It was Christmas season in 1986, "Walk Like
an Egyptian" by the Bangles was at the top

02:05:42.720 --> 02:05:46.800
of the charts, Eddie Murphy's "The Golden
Child" was the number one movie in America,

02:05:46.800 --> 02:05:52.140
and hundreds of thousands of kids were writing
to Santa - and begging their parents - for

02:05:52.140 --> 02:05:55.440
one thing: the Nintendo Entertainment System.

02:05:55.440 --> 02:06:00.780
The NES was an 8-bit home video game console,
and the only place to journey through the

02:06:00.780 --> 02:06:05.820
land of Hyrule in "The Legend of Zelda" or
help Samus hunt Space Pirates in "Metroid."

02:06:05.820 --> 02:06:10.980
But with such an extreme demand for the system,
stores were selling out all over the country.

02:06:10.980 --> 02:06:15.960
Anyone who didn't beat the crowds was doomed
to disappoint their kids with socks, with

02:06:15.960 --> 02:06:18.240
less flashy toys like dolls or tiny plastic&nbsp;

02:06:18.240 --> 02:06:21.060
trucks, or with boring,&nbsp;
non-electric, violence-free

02:06:21.060 --> 02:06:21.720
board games.

02:06:21.720 --> 02:06:25.440
Well, board games are only violence-free if
you're not playing "Monopoly."

02:06:25.440 --> 02:06:27.600
But back to 1986.

02:06:27.600 --> 02:06:32.940
Beverly Harrington was a single mom, doing
her best to provide for her son, Jason, and

02:06:32.940 --> 02:06:34.980
give him the Christmas he deserved.

02:06:34.980 --> 02:06:39.660
She had been picking up extra shifts at the
local diner, making sure to save up all of

02:06:39.660 --> 02:06:44.220
the extra money for a real Christmas tree
from the farm just out of town, for a beautiful

02:06:44.220 --> 02:06:48.300
honey-baked ham, and, of course, for presents.

02:06:48.300 --> 02:06:53.520
She knew that, though he was a shy kid and
would never demand it, Jason desperately wanted

02:06:53.520 --> 02:06:56.700
to find an NES under the tree on Christmas
morning.

02:06:56.700 --> 02:06:59.700
She got so absorbed in her work, so exhausted&nbsp;

02:06:59.700 --> 02:07:01.980
from the extra shifts and&nbsp;
trying to make everything

02:07:01.980 --> 02:07:07.020
perfect, that she forgot to do her actual
shopping until the very last minute.

02:07:07.020 --> 02:07:12.000
She woke up the morning of December 24 with
a start, her heart jumping into her throat.

02:07:12.000 --> 02:07:14.340
She hadn't picked up Jason's present yet!

02:07:14.340 --> 02:07:19.320
She threw on her coat and rushed out into
the snowy streets, making her way to the store

02:07:19.320 --> 02:07:23.940
and saying a silent prayer that they would
still have one more NES in stock.

02:07:23.940 --> 02:07:26.040
Lucky for her, they did!

02:07:26.040 --> 02:07:29.040
Less lucky, two parents were already wrestling&nbsp;

02:07:29.040 --> 02:07:31.980
for it, fighting each other&nbsp;
with the raw animalistic

02:07:31.980 --> 02:07:35.400
rage of two wolves competing for the same
piece of meat.

02:07:35.400 --> 02:07:40.020
If she tried to grab it and join in, she could
get hurt, or the console could break apart.

02:07:40.020 --> 02:07:43.980
She asked an employee if they had any in the
back, but she already knew the answer from

02:07:43.980 --> 02:07:45.360
the exhausted look in his eyes.

02:07:45.360 --> 02:07:47.220
There were no more in the back.

02:07:47.220 --> 02:07:51.660
There were no more in town, or the next town
over, or the next town over from that.

02:07:51.660 --> 02:07:56.340
Not only would she have to get Jason's present
at the very last minute, but she would also

02:07:56.340 --> 02:07:58.800
have to get him something he didn't even want.

02:07:58.800 --> 02:08:03.540
He would smile and thank her, but she knew
how sad it would make him to go back to school

02:08:03.540 --> 02:08:06.420
and hear his friends bragging about their
Christmases.

02:08:06.420 --> 02:08:11.820
She shoved her hands into the pockets of her
coat, and began her slow, sad march back through

02:08:11.820 --> 02:08:14.160
the snow, trying to come up with what to do
next.

02:08:14.160 --> 02:08:19.860
Then, something caught her eye, a brightly-colored
sign reading "Wondertaining Toys."

02:08:19.860 --> 02:08:24.660
Beneath it, was a storefront she had never
seen before, and right there in the window,

02:08:24.660 --> 02:08:26.520
a video game console.

02:08:26.520 --> 02:08:31.680
It wasn't the NES, not exactly, but it looked
so much like it…

02:08:31.680 --> 02:08:35.280
She couldn't help herself, she had to go inside
and get a better look.

02:08:35.280 --> 02:08:38.640
The store was filled with toys, games, and
puzzles.

02:08:38.640 --> 02:08:43.260
They were all inviting, with vivid packaging
and labels depicting children having fun,

02:08:43.260 --> 02:08:45.600
but she had never seen any of them before.

02:08:45.600 --> 02:08:50.340
Perhaps it was a new brand, and she was one
of the first people to happen upon the store?

02:08:50.340 --> 02:08:55.260
It seemed odd for a toy store to open this
close to Christmas, rather than taking advantage

02:08:55.260 --> 02:09:00.480
of the shopping season and opening earlier
in the year...But she couldn't dwell on that,

02:09:00.480 --> 02:09:02.880
not when she was this close to a solution.

02:09:02.880 --> 02:09:06.360
Toward the back of the store, she spotted
the video game console from the window.

02:09:06.360 --> 02:09:08.580
The label read, "Pretendo."

02:09:08.580 --> 02:09:14.520
So the resemblance to the NES was no coincidence,
this console was intended as a knockoff of

02:09:14.520 --> 02:09:14.940
some kind.

02:09:14.940 --> 02:09:20.280
She asked the sales clerk, a man with a strangely
wide, unmoving smile, if the console was capable

02:09:20.280 --> 02:09:21.660
of running Nintendo games.

02:09:21.660 --> 02:09:23.340
"You bet your bottom dollar!"

02:09:23.340 --> 02:09:23.940
He replied.

02:09:24.600 --> 02:09:25.920
"We don't carry those here, though.

02:09:25.920 --> 02:09:27.660
But can I interest you in some of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:27.660 --> 02:09:28.740
Wondertainment
originals?

02:09:28.740 --> 02:09:32.880
We have all kinds of games here, and they're
just as good or your money back!

02:09:32.880 --> 02:09:35.280
There's a whole world inside each and every
one."

02:09:35.280 --> 02:09:40.080
It wasn't what she'd set out to buy, but this
was clearly the best thing Beverly was going

02:09:40.080 --> 02:09:41.640
to find this close to the holiday.

02:09:41.640 --> 02:09:46.320
She paid the man for the Pretendo console
and a selection of the store's most popular

02:09:46.320 --> 02:09:47.160
video games.

02:09:47.160 --> 02:09:52.260
He even gift-wrapped it for her in a colorful
box, with a shiny red bow on top.

02:09:52.260 --> 02:09:56.520
The next morning, Jason and Beverly sat in
the living room, sipping cocoa and opening

02:09:56.520 --> 02:09:57.180
presents together.

02:09:57.180 --> 02:10:02.100
She started small, with the classic pair of
new wool socks, and worked her way up to the

02:10:02.100 --> 02:10:03.360
big box in the corner.

02:10:03.360 --> 02:10:06.120
As Jason tore the paper off, his eyes lit
up with glee.

02:10:06.120 --> 02:10:06.900
"Thank you, mom!

02:10:06.900 --> 02:10:07.500
Thank you!"

02:10:07.500 --> 02:10:11.220
He gave her a huge hug, then looked at her
with nearly manic excitement.

02:10:11.220 --> 02:10:12.180
"Can I play it now?

02:10:12.180 --> 02:10:12.600
Can I?"

02:10:12.600 --> 02:10:16.680
She laughed and promised that after he helped
her clean up the wrapping paper, he could

02:10:16.680 --> 02:10:17.820
play his new games.

02:10:17.820 --> 02:10:20.580
She was just so glad he was happy with it.

02:10:20.580 --> 02:10:25.380
He didn't care that it wasn't the name-brand
console, he knew that this gift was special.

02:10:25.380 --> 02:10:30.060
So she was perfectly glad to let him enjoy
it, and she could get started on that ham

02:10:30.060 --> 02:10:30.600
for later.

02:10:30.600 --> 02:10:35.520
Jason plugged in his brand new Pretendo, and
looked through the games his mom had picked

02:10:35.520 --> 02:10:35.940
out for him.

02:10:35.940 --> 02:10:37.680
There was "Farmtastic!

02:10:37.680 --> 02:10:43.680
Farming simulator", "Historical House Hunter",
"Magic Wizard Quest," "Super Fighters," and

02:10:43.680 --> 02:10:45.900
one simply called, "Spooky."

02:10:45.900 --> 02:10:51.000
It was emblazoned with the image of a cartoon
ghost in front of an abandoned cabin, and

02:10:51.000 --> 02:10:52.500
it piqued Jason's interest.

02:10:52.500 --> 02:10:57.360
He had always loved scary stories and legends
about ghosts and things that go bump in the

02:10:57.360 --> 02:10:57.540
night.

02:10:57.540 --> 02:10:59.820
He decided this would be his first game.

02:11:00.780 --> 02:11:04.740
He placed the cartridge in the console and
hit the power button to turn it on.

02:11:04.740 --> 02:11:08.640
As he clutched the controller in his hands,
he began to notice something strange.

02:11:08.640 --> 02:11:12.900
His room, once decorated in posters of his
favorite movies, turned dark.

02:11:12.900 --> 02:11:15.480
The light green walls became rotting wood.

02:11:15.480 --> 02:11:20.880
And, for lack of a better way to describe
it, the entire world's resolution began to

02:11:20.880 --> 02:11:21.120
shift.

02:11:21.120 --> 02:11:24.660
If the world around him had graphics, they
were getting worse.

02:11:24.660 --> 02:11:29.940
He could no longer see the screen depicting
the game's main menu, and in fact, the console

02:11:29.940 --> 02:11:31.320
itself had vanished.

02:11:31.320 --> 02:11:35.040
Somehow, he was standing in the haunted cabin
from the cover of the game.

02:11:35.040 --> 02:11:38.160
He looked down at his hands and found the
controller still there.

02:11:38.160 --> 02:11:41.820
So, not everything had changed...

02:11:41.820 --> 02:11:45.960
Before he could think any more about this
strange new place he found himself in, he

02:11:45.960 --> 02:11:49.860
heard the sound of ominous music, seemingly
coming from nowhere.

02:11:49.860 --> 02:11:54.780
After a moment, he realized why the sound
of its melody filled him with dread...it was

02:11:54.780 --> 02:11:58.740
the kind of music that always played in a
game when an enemy was getting close.

02:11:58.740 --> 02:12:00.240
He had to move.

02:12:00.240 --> 02:12:04.080
He took a step forward, and he moved through
the space as if it was a real house.

02:12:04.080 --> 02:12:06.420
For the time being, it was.

02:12:06.420 --> 02:12:09.600
Somehow, he was inside the game.

02:12:09.600 --> 02:12:13.320
Which meant...the ghost was coming for him
soon.

02:12:13.320 --> 02:12:17.880
He pressed a button on the controller to activate
a flashlight, and followed its beam through

02:12:17.880 --> 02:12:18.780
the darkened cabin.

02:12:18.780 --> 02:12:23.820
As he walked, looking for clues or any object
he could interact with, he could hear the

02:12:23.820 --> 02:12:24.900
music getting louder.

02:12:24.900 --> 02:12:27.600
Whatever was coming, it was getting closer.

02:12:27.600 --> 02:12:31.260
The flashlight's beam illuminated a scrap
of paper on a small table.

02:12:31.260 --> 02:12:35.760
He moved to take a closer look, and could
just barely make out what it said.

02:12:35.760 --> 02:12:38.280
"They hide in the darkness.

02:12:38.280 --> 02:12:39.840
Find the exit.

02:12:39.840 --> 02:12:41.700
Don't stop moving.

02:12:41.700 --> 02:12:42.180
"

02:12:42.180 --> 02:12:46.080
He heard a sound behind him, and the music
reached a deafening volume.

02:12:46.080 --> 02:12:51.180
He whirled around, and saw nothing but the
hall in front of him, bending around a corner.

02:12:51.180 --> 02:12:55.260
He knew, from the heavy feeling in the pit
of his stomach and the music that threatened

02:12:55.260 --> 02:13:00.900
to rupture his eardrums, that there was something
horrible waiting for him around that corner.

02:13:00.900 --> 02:13:07.020
He froze for a moment, then remembered the
letter's words of warning, "don't stop moving."

02:13:07.020 --> 02:13:10.500
He took a step toward the corner, then another,
and another.

02:13:10.500 --> 02:13:14.700
He shone the light of the flashlight around
the corner, but there was nothing there.

02:13:14.700 --> 02:13:16.800
No ghost, no monster.

02:13:16.800 --> 02:13:17.700
Nothing.

02:13:17.700 --> 02:13:19.620
And then he felt it.

02:13:19.620 --> 02:13:21.360
A chill on the back of his neck.

02:13:21.360 --> 02:13:23.520
He whirled around, and there it was.

02:13:23.520 --> 02:13:25.080
The ghost of the cabin.

02:13:25.080 --> 02:13:30.720
It was so much bigger than it had looked before,
wispy white form stretching up to the ceiling,

02:13:30.720 --> 02:13:36.600
from wall to wall, its wide dark eyes and
gaping maw of a mouth inches from his own

02:13:36.600 --> 02:13:37.020
face.

02:13:37.020 --> 02:13:42.180
He screamed and instinctively swung the flashlight
at the spirit, cutting through it with a beam

02:13:42.180 --> 02:13:42.540
of light.

02:13:42.540 --> 02:13:45.840
The ghost let out a shriek and shrank away
from the glow.

02:13:45.840 --> 02:13:46.680
That was it!

02:13:46.680 --> 02:13:50.700
He just needed to keep the light on it, and
the ghost couldn't hurt him.

02:13:50.700 --> 02:13:55.260
Jason was so thrilled by this little victory
that he didn't notice the boss music starting

02:13:55.260 --> 02:13:58.140
to play again, growing louder and louder.

02:13:58.140 --> 02:14:03.420
He didn't see the movement out of the corner
of his eye, the silvery pale figure of a woman

02:14:03.420 --> 02:14:05.760
stepping out of a portrait on the wall.

02:14:05.760 --> 02:14:12.120
All he felt was a sudden, overwhelming cold,
like ice in his veins, and then everything

02:14:12.120 --> 02:14:12.960
went black.

02:14:12.960 --> 02:14:18.480
He opened his eyes back in his bedroom, looking
at a dark screen with the words GAME OVER

02:14:18.480 --> 02:14:20.520
written in dripping red font.

02:14:20.520 --> 02:14:26.040
He couldn't tell for a moment if that had
been real or a dream, but either way, he only

02:14:26.040 --> 02:14:28.860
wanted to do one thing: tell his mom.

02:14:28.860 --> 02:14:31.620
Beverly listened to her son's story, and couldn't&nbsp;

02:14:31.620 --> 02:14:34.140
help but write it off as&nbsp;
the overactive imagination

02:14:34.140 --> 02:14:34.860
of a child.

02:14:34.860 --> 02:14:39.840
But still, she humored him and agreed to try
the console for herself and see what happened.

02:14:39.840 --> 02:14:43.800
Choosing at random, she selected "Farmtastic!"
and pressed "play."

02:14:43.800 --> 02:14:48.660
All of a sudden, she felt the heat of the
sun on her face, heard the mooing of cows,

02:14:48.660 --> 02:14:50.280
and smelled fresh-cut hay.

02:14:50.280 --> 02:14:55.140
She was steering a tractor through a wide-open
field, where a moment ago she had been standing

02:14:55.140 --> 02:14:56.460
in her house in the dead of winter.

02:14:56.460 --> 02:15:01.680
Jason shut off the game, pulling her back
into reality, and as Beverly gaped in shock,

02:15:01.680 --> 02:15:03.660
Jason said "Best!

02:15:03.660 --> 02:15:04.500
Present!

02:15:04.500 --> 02:15:05.040
EVER!"

02:15:05.040 --> 02:15:10.020
Unfortunately for Jason, word was already
spreading about the Pretendo, all the way

02:15:10.020 --> 02:15:11.580
to the SCP Foundation.

02:15:11.580 --> 02:15:16.500
The console, and all other consoles that the
Foundation could track down, were seized and

02:15:16.500 --> 02:15:18.960
designated SCP-591.

02:15:18.960 --> 02:15:22.920
SCP-591 is a line of video&nbsp;
game consoles originally&nbsp;

02:15:22.920 --> 02:15:25.440
developed as a counterfeit of the Nintendo

02:15:25.440 --> 02:15:29.940
Entertainment System, before being bought
and distributed by Doctor Wondertainment,

02:15:29.940 --> 02:15:35.160
a toy company responsible for a wide variety
of anomalous objects contained by the Foundation

02:15:35.160 --> 02:15:36.000
over the years.

02:15:36.000 --> 02:15:41.400
The console is capable of playing NES titles
as well as games from Doctor Wondertainment's

02:15:41.400 --> 02:15:47.220
original lineup of 8-bit games, many of which
the Foundation has in its possession as well.

02:15:47.220 --> 02:15:53.040
Some of the titles in the SCP Foundation library
include, but are not limited to, arcade shooter

02:15:53.040 --> 02:15:58.500
"Whap 'Em!", platformer "Dusky's Adventures
in Staedland," survival horror game "You Can

02:15:58.500 --> 02:16:04.680
Do That on Television," puzzle game "WWI Ace
Trench Digger," music/racing game "Led Zeppelin

02:16:04.680 --> 02:16:10.920
Air Racers," fightings games "Super Kick Karate"
and "Super Kick Karate 2010," and the educational

02:16:10.920 --> 02:16:13.440
game "Reading Rainbow Sit and Listen."

02:16:13.440 --> 02:16:18.540
Whenever one of the official Doctor Wondertainment
cartridges is inserted into the console and

02:16:18.540 --> 02:16:24.900
activated, it results in a localized CK-class
reality restructuring scenario, which rearranges

02:16:24.900 --> 02:16:28.440
reality in the immediate area to resemble
the game's setting.

02:16:28.440 --> 02:16:33.180
The player inside of the game's affected radius
will take on the role of the game's protagonist,

02:16:33.180 --> 02:16:38.160
and navigate the events of the game until
they either win or the console is deactivated.

02:16:38.160 --> 02:16:42.240
Don't worry, this isn't one of those "if you
die in the game you die in real life" scenarios.

02:16:42.240 --> 02:16:47.280
If the player happens to be a less than adept
gamer, they will simply respawn or start at

02:16:47.280 --> 02:16:50.099
the beginning of the game like any video game
character would.

02:16:50.100 --> 02:16:55.440
That doesn't, however, mean that SCP-591 isn't
dangerous.

02:16:55.439 --> 02:17:00.719
Like any piece of vintage technology, the
components of the Pretendo system have degraded

02:17:00.720 --> 02:17:03.720
over time, their functionality&nbsp;
steadily decreasing.

02:17:03.720 --> 02:17:08.880
For an ordinary game console, this would be
a simple inconvenience, something that would

02:17:08.880 --> 02:17:12.180
impact the quality of the graphics or the
responsiveness of the controls.

02:17:12.180 --> 02:17:16.860
It would be annoying, and maybe eventually
unplayable, but otherwise harmless.

02:17:16.859 --> 02:17:21.719
For the Pretendo, however, this degradation
has had catastrophic effects.

02:17:21.720 --> 02:17:28.020
When a game cartridge is inserted into SCP-591,
there is now a chance of the CK-class reality

02:17:28.020 --> 02:17:34.500
restructuring scenario going wrong, and resulting
in a ZK-class reality failure scenario instead.

02:17:34.500 --> 02:17:39.240
Essentially, instead of just transporting
the player into the world of the game, reality

02:17:39.240 --> 02:17:44.399
will crumble in the designated area and the
laws of time, space, and physics will be bent

02:17:44.399 --> 02:17:45.420
or even broken.

02:17:45.420 --> 02:17:50.880
While the CK-class scenarios dissipate as
soon as the console is deactivated, the ZK-class

02:17:50.880 --> 02:17:53.040
scenarios refuse to play by the rules.

02:17:53.040 --> 02:17:56.399
They remain in place wherever&nbsp;
they first manifested,&nbsp;

02:17:56.399 --> 02:17:58.019
seemingly permanently.

02:17:58.020 --> 02:18:02.400
The ZK-class scenarios were first observed
during play-testing by the research staff

02:18:02.399 --> 02:18:04.559
assigned to SCP-591.

02:18:04.560 --> 02:18:09.840
After learning about the nature of the Pretendo
game system, Dr. Furakawa has volunteered

02:18:09.840 --> 02:18:14.160
as a test subject, specifically requesting
that he be given the chance to play the Dr.

02:18:14.160 --> 02:18:20.040
Wondertainment title "The Legend of Sword
Master," because, quote, "It just looks really

02:18:20.040 --> 02:18:20.460
cool."

02:18:20.460 --> 02:18:26.160
This particular game came with not just the
cartridge, but a special sword-shaped controller,

02:18:26.160 --> 02:18:28.740
to be used during the sword fight sequences.

02:18:28.740 --> 02:18:34.500
Dr. Furakawa stepped into the containment
cell where one instance of SCP-591 had been

02:18:34.500 --> 02:18:38.700
placed, with his chosen game cartridge and
controller waiting for him.

02:18:38.700 --> 02:18:42.960
As soon as he inserted the cartridge into
the console and pressed the "start" button,

02:18:42.960 --> 02:18:48.600
the room around him began to transform, reality
itself warping and reshaping to fit the world

02:18:48.600 --> 02:18:49.260
of the game.

02:18:49.260 --> 02:18:54.960
Fluorescent lights and stark white walls were
replaced by pixelated trees, and a winding

02:18:54.960 --> 02:18:58.620
cobblestone path leading to a massive stone
castle.

02:18:58.620 --> 02:19:04.559
The first enemy, a knight in black armor,
leaped into frame, and Dr. Furakawa raised

02:19:04.560 --> 02:19:05.940
his sword, ready for combat.

02:19:05.939 --> 02:19:10.439
But before he had a chance to deal the first
blow, it became clear that something wasn't

02:19:10.439 --> 02:19:10.799
right.

02:19:10.800 --> 02:19:13.560
The enemy knight suddenly disappeared, glitching
out of sight.

02:19:13.560 --> 02:19:18.900
The cobblestones slowly began to drift into
the air, and so did Dr. Furakawa.

02:19:18.899 --> 02:19:22.859
Just as suddenly as he lifted off, he dropped
back to the ground with a heavy thud.

02:19:22.859 --> 02:19:27.179
Gravity was rapidly fluctuating, and he could
scarcely keep his balance.

02:19:27.180 --> 02:19:31.320
In front of him, he could see random cubic
structures popping up out of nowhere, made

02:19:31.320 --> 02:19:33.480
up of wood, stone, and ice.

02:19:33.479 --> 02:19:34.500
"End the game!"

02:19:34.500 --> 02:19:35.820
He called out desperately.

02:19:35.820 --> 02:19:37.679
"Pull the plug, someone, please!"

02:19:37.680 --> 02:19:42.480
Outside, one of the researchers watching the
experiment cut the power to the console, but

02:19:42.479 --> 02:19:43.200
nothing happened.

02:19:43.200 --> 02:19:45.720
The world refused to return to normal.

02:19:45.720 --> 02:19:50.760
Security officers entered the area, and attempted
to remove Dr. Furakawa, but found that his

02:19:50.760 --> 02:19:53.520
body still resembled an&nbsp;
8-bit video game character.

02:19:53.520 --> 02:19:58.800
When they attempted to drag him out of the
ZK-class zone, he cried out in pain as his

02:19:58.800 --> 02:20:01.080
body began to dissolve into static.

02:20:01.080 --> 02:20:05.100
They had turned off the console, but they
were unable to pull him out.

02:20:05.100 --> 02:20:07.800
The rest of the research team, horrified by&nbsp;

02:20:07.800 --> 02:20:10.080
what they had witnessed,&nbsp;
began to take measurements

02:20:10.080 --> 02:20:12.660
of the reality failure scenario from outside
of the room.

02:20:12.660 --> 02:20:17.939
The area now displayed a complete absence
of naturally occurring radiation, including

02:20:17.939 --> 02:20:19.559
cosmic background radiation.

02:20:19.560 --> 02:20:24.300
There was also evidence of time dilation,
reduction of light speed, and the gravity

02:20:24.300 --> 02:20:27.180
fluctuations that Dr. Furakawa had first noticed.

02:20:27.180 --> 02:20:32.220
With no way to remove Furakawa from this broken
reality, the area would have to be sealed

02:20:32.220 --> 02:20:34.440
off, and he would be left inside.

02:20:34.439 --> 02:20:36.780
There was no other option.

02:20:36.780 --> 02:20:41.819
So, they constructed a concrete dome around
the testing site, and relocated the remaining

02:20:41.819 --> 02:20:44.099
instances of SCP-591.

02:20:44.100 --> 02:20:49.020
From that point on, testing was only conducted
using D-Class, no matter how much research

02:20:49.020 --> 02:20:54.180
staff loved video games, or how vehemently
they insisted that they were "willing to roll

02:20:54.180 --> 02:20:54.840
the dice."

02:20:54.840 --> 02:20:59.460
They were barred from testing and encouraged
to pick up a VR headset for a much lower-risk

02:20:59.460 --> 02:21:01.200
immersive gaming experience.

02:21:01.200 --> 02:21:06.599
Over the years of testing following the Dr.
Furakawa incident, four more ZK-class scenarios

02:21:06.600 --> 02:21:08.340
were created and contained.

02:21:08.340 --> 02:21:13.500
Some of the individuals survived the incident
but were physically altered so greatly that

02:21:13.500 --> 02:21:17.760
they would not be able to survive outside
of the ZK-class reality failure.

02:21:17.760 --> 02:21:22.560
The only people who survived were unable to
readjust to their lives, continuing to live

02:21:22.560 --> 02:21:25.140
as the main character of the game they were
playing.

02:21:25.140 --> 02:21:30.540
At this point, testing was officially suspended
indefinitely, due not only to the loss of

02:21:30.540 --> 02:21:35.100
human life but also the unpredictable and
highly dangerous nature of the damage being

02:21:35.100 --> 02:21:37.920
done to reality on such a grand scale.

02:21:37.920 --> 02:21:43.620
All instances of SCP-591 confiscated by the
Foundation are kept in a storage containment

02:21:43.620 --> 02:21:48.180
vault, kept separate from any civilians or
their infrastructure by a distance of at least

02:21:48.180 --> 02:21:49.320
500m.

02:21:49.319 --> 02:21:52.439
It is also kept separate from&nbsp;
other Foundation-controlled&nbsp;

02:21:52.439 --> 02:21:53.519
containment facilities.

02:21:53.520 --> 02:21:58.920
Unless it is being used for official, approved
testing by Level-3 staff, it is to remain

02:21:58.920 --> 02:22:00.540
deactivated at all times.

02:22:00.540 --> 02:22:06.420
Any ZK-class scenarios created by an instance
of SCP-591 will be contained via the construction

02:22:06.420 --> 02:22:09.540
of a closed concrete dome,&nbsp;
and given the designation&nbsp;

02:22:09.540 --> 02:22:10.740
of Sector-W#.

02:22:11.460 --> 02:22:17.460
Sectors W1 through W5, the currently existing
ZK-class scenarios, are monitored remotely

02:22:17.460 --> 02:22:18.899
and kept highly classified.

02:22:18.899 --> 02:22:21.839
Any personnel or test subjects located inside&nbsp;

02:22:21.840 --> 02:22:24.660
one of these sectors will&nbsp;
remain there indefinitely,

02:22:24.660 --> 02:22:27.240
and be officially considered lost in action.

02:22:27.240 --> 02:22:33.599
Any additional instances of SCP-591 located
in civilian stores, households, or any public

02:22:33.600 --> 02:22:36.900
place are to be removed and contained immediately.

02:22:36.899 --> 02:22:42.780
As the years have gone by, testing has indicated
a steady increase in the rake of a ZK-class

02:22:42.780 --> 02:22:45.899
scenario being generated from a CK-class scenario.

02:22:45.899 --> 02:22:50.580
Where it was once only a 1% chance, there
is now a 32% chance.

02:22:50.580 --> 02:22:54.899
According to Wondertainment company records,
seized during a Foundation raid of one of

02:22:54.899 --> 02:23:00.000
their toy factories, there are still plenty
of SCP-591 instances out there in the wild,

02:23:00.000 --> 02:23:05.880
just waiting for some unsuspecting gamer to
fire them up and get lost in the story, literally.

02:23:05.880 --> 02:23:11.819
If the documents found are accurate, there
are around 243 consoles and 1,300 consoles

02:23:11.819 --> 02:23:12.780
still in circulation.

02:23:12.780 --> 02:23:18.420
These also may or may not include a "Pocket
Pretendo" model that was developed as a prototype

02:23:18.420 --> 02:23:20.340
but never officially released on the market.

02:23:20.340 --> 02:23:25.740
If it was, and the chaos of the Pretendo system
is available in a portable form...well, it's

02:23:25.740 --> 02:23:27.719
best not to think about that for too long.

02:23:27.720 --> 02:23:32.400
The Foundation is doing its best to track
down the remaining Pretendo systems, but there

02:23:32.399 --> 02:23:36.839
is no way to know for sure what distant corners
of the world they've made their way to.

02:23:36.840 --> 02:23:42.120
So, when you're picking up a shiny new game
console, or a nostalgic vintage model to relive

02:23:42.120 --> 02:23:46.740
some of your favorite childhood memories with,
take an extra close look at the label and

02:23:46.740 --> 02:23:48.420
make sure you're getting the real thing.

02:23:48.420 --> 02:23:53.280
Because if you're not careful...it just might
be Game Over.

02:23:53.280 --> 02:23:59.070
Modern cognitive neuroscience tells us that
our memories are about as reliable as as the

02:23:59.070 --> 02:24:00.660
weatherman at your local news channel.

02:24:00.660 --> 02:24:03.359
When a couple is asked How did you first meet?

02:24:03.359 --> 02:24:06.420
they are lured into romanticized reflection.

02:24:06.420 --> 02:24:09.120
They might recall locking eyes at a school
dance.

02:24:09.120 --> 02:24:14.519
They might recall the night being just perfect…
despite the truth being far from it.

02:24:14.520 --> 02:24:17.041
We don’t remember the spilled punch, the&nbsp;

02:24:17.040 --> 02:24:20.340
stepped-on toes, or the&nbsp;
stuh—stuh--stuh-stuttering.

02:24:20.340 --> 02:24:23.220
We only remember the x’s and o’s.

02:24:23.220 --> 02:24:28.680
Our memory doesn’t find the needle by sifting
through the haystack, but rather by lighting

02:24:28.680 --> 02:24:34.500
a match and setting the barn ablaze until
all that remains is that small piece of silver,

02:24:34.500 --> 02:24:36.180
shining through smoke.

02:24:36.180 --> 02:24:42.420
In this way, our memories are our best friends
– they know exactly when to lie to us, feeding

02:24:42.420 --> 02:24:46.140
us misinformation about the past to satisfy
ourselves in the present.

02:24:46.140 --> 02:24:50.400
Because if humans were capable of writing
and recalling accurate personal accounts of

02:24:50.399 --> 02:24:55.379
their history, our memoirs would be stiff,
un-marketable literature.

02:24:55.380 --> 02:24:58.620
And when a book gets boring, we tend to tap
out.

02:24:58.620 --> 02:25:02.700
Just the same, when our lives get monotonous,
we tend to give up.

02:25:02.700 --> 02:25:08.939
So, we have our faulty, biased brains to thank
for our perceived happiness and fulfillment.

02:25:08.939 --> 02:25:15.059
Our inaccurate memory systems consequently
help us hide trauma, contrive purpose, and

02:25:15.060 --> 02:25:18.600
infuse unique importance into an otherwise
banal existence.

02:25:18.600 --> 02:25:22.560
But let it be known, not&nbsp;
all cerebral restructurings&nbsp;

02:25:22.560 --> 02:25:24.600
are gifts from psychology.

02:25:24.600 --> 02:25:27.720
Some are curses from SCPs.

02:25:27.720 --> 02:25:29.880
Which of these two possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:29.880 --> 02:25:33.120
is more existentially
terrifying is up to you.

02:25:33.120 --> 02:25:37.019
SCP-5040 is a nonexistent Japanese horror&nbsp;

02:25:37.020 --> 02:25:40.440
film entitled "Tears of&nbsp;
Blood", which spontaneously

02:25:40.439 --> 02:25:42.359
manifests in human memories.

02:25:42.359 --> 02:25:45.839
SCP-5040 is a master of persuasion.

02:25:45.840 --> 02:25:49.140
Those affected by SCP-5040 will remember going&nbsp;

02:25:49.140 --> 02:25:51.781
to see the film even when&nbsp;
their supposed attendance

02:25:51.780 --> 02:25:53.759
would contradict empirical evidence.

02:25:53.760 --> 02:25:57.360
It manipulates the mind into unwavering faith.

02:25:57.359 --> 02:26:02.040
While humans occasionally stumble upon the
self-awareness to question their recollection,

02:26:02.040 --> 02:26:07.319
there are no documented instances of an individual
doubting this specific memory.

02:26:07.319 --> 02:26:14.099
Somehow, beyond The Foundation’s understanding,
SCP-5040 manages to suspend the skepticism

02:26:14.100 --> 02:26:15.180
of its victims.

02:26:15.180 --> 02:26:17.820
They accept the memory as canon.

02:26:17.819 --> 02:26:18.960
As brute fact.

02:26:18.960 --> 02:26:25.680
Victims of SCP-5040 do not need a ticket stub
to know they had been to the theater, and

02:26:25.680 --> 02:26:29.940
SCP-5040 afflictions can occur any place where
movies are shown.

02:26:29.939 --> 02:26:33.599
It even transcends laws and navigates social
norms.

02:26:33.600 --> 02:26:38.701
In cultures where its content would usually
be prohibited by regulations and frowned upon

02:26:38.700 --> 02:26:43.380
by the public, it still inserts itself into
the memory of its prey.

02:26:43.380 --> 02:26:47.819
And once that memory is planted, it’s an
event that can’t be forgotten.

02:26:47.819 --> 02:26:49.620
Like the birth of your first child.

02:26:49.620 --> 02:26:52.800
Like your favorite football team winning the
Super Bowl.

02:26:52.800 --> 02:26:57.300
Like the employee number you punch in and
out of work every day for the rest of your

02:26:57.300 --> 02:26:57.600
life.

02:26:57.600 --> 02:27:03.661
It stamps itself into the forefront of the
subject’s brain, waving its arm, pleading

02:27:03.660 --> 02:27:06.660
to be recognized, begging for attention.

02:27:06.660 --> 02:27:12.300
Descriptions of the film are always similar
in nature, as are the circumstances and events

02:27:12.300 --> 02:27:13.560
surrounding the viewing.

02:27:13.560 --> 02:27:16.741
However, reports of SCP-5040's&nbsp;
story and characters&nbsp;

02:27:16.740 --> 02:27:19.319
are never fully consistent from one subject

02:27:19.319 --> 02:27:24.240
to the next, and the film's setting, subplots,
character names, and much of the dialogue

02:27:24.240 --> 02:27:25.859
are different for each viewer.

02:27:25.859 --> 02:27:30.780
Whether this is SCP-5040’s intent or inadequacy
is unclear.

02:27:30.780 --> 02:27:36.420
Casting also varies and appears largely arbitrary;
a broad variety of Japanese performers and

02:27:36.420 --> 02:27:41.280
entertainment personalities - both living
and deceased - have been said to star in the

02:27:41.280 --> 02:27:45.840
film, even when the actor in question has
no real-life associations with the horror

02:27:45.840 --> 02:27:46.260
genre.

02:27:46.260 --> 02:27:51.900
Imagine famous Japanese actor, Pat Morita,
better known as Mr. Miyagi from The Karate

02:27:51.899 --> 02:27:54.420
Kid, being the lead in a horror film.

02:27:54.420 --> 02:28:00.660
Despite the fact this would be a noticeably
strange casting decision, SCP-5040 would still

02:28:00.660 --> 02:28:01.800
be taken seriously.

02:28:01.800 --> 02:28:09.300
Despite any and all deviations from expectations
of the medium, SCP-5040 is seen as legitimate.

02:28:09.300 --> 02:28:14.580
Kinks in its design are overlooked by the
viewer, similar to how our sleeping selves

02:28:14.580 --> 02:28:19.319
most often don’t recognize we are dreaming
– even when we encounter flying horses,

02:28:19.319 --> 02:28:24.840
friends with banana fingers, and leprechauns
playing pot-limit Omaha in Chris Hemsworth’s

02:28:24.840 --> 02:28:25.140
basement.

02:28:25.140 --> 02:28:30.420
This may be because, despite all its quirks,
the film's core is sturdy.

02:28:30.420 --> 02:28:33.300
Its beginning, middle, and end are always
in agreement.

02:28:33.300 --> 02:28:37.500
It doesn’t stray from Robert Mckee's universal
structure of Story.

02:28:37.500 --> 02:28:43.500
Not even the sick and twisted M. Night Shyamalan
himself could have created a cinematic experience

02:28:43.500 --> 02:28:44.399
quite like this.

02:28:44.399 --> 02:28:47.280
After conducting more than&nbsp;
three hundred interviews,&nbsp;

02:28:47.280 --> 02:28:50.099
researchers have constructed a detailed synopsis

02:28:50.100 --> 02:28:56.100
of SCP-5040's most consistent story elements
and the most common sequence of events associated

02:28:56.100 --> 02:28:58.680
with the viewer's memory of their screening
attendance.

02:28:58.680 --> 02:29:00.840
There is no matinee with this movie.

02:29:00.840 --> 02:29:03.540
Screenings always begin at sunset.

02:29:03.540 --> 02:29:09.300
If there are obstacles standing in the way
of a subject's attendance, SCP-5040 will see

02:29:09.300 --> 02:29:10.080
them removed.

02:29:10.080 --> 02:29:15.479
Dentist appointments, drinks with a friend,
a long-procrastinated date with the treadmill,

02:29:15.479 --> 02:29:21.479
all these commitments for that day and time
will be pushed down the subject’s list of

02:29:21.479 --> 02:29:24.359
priorities, making way for the movie.

02:29:24.359 --> 02:29:28.979
They will learn that their plans have been
canceled or resolved one way or another.

02:29:28.979 --> 02:29:31.200
The dentist's office burned to the ground.

02:29:31.200 --> 02:29:33.420
Your friend is suddenly sober.

02:29:33.420 --> 02:29:36.600
The model of treadmills at your gym has been
recalled.

02:29:36.600 --> 02:29:40.680
Somehow, someway, time is freed up.

02:29:40.680 --> 02:29:46.320
And when life is kind enough to offer respite
from personal and social obligations, it is

02:29:46.319 --> 02:29:49.380
modern humans’ instinct to sit down and
veg out.

02:29:49.380 --> 02:29:53.700
At this moment, the subjects decide to see
a movie at a nearby theater.

02:29:53.700 --> 02:29:57.720
Upon arrival, the theater looks to be an entity
in its own right.

02:29:57.720 --> 02:30:02.340
Long lines of patrons are like limbs extending
from its center box office.

02:30:02.340 --> 02:30:07.440
Bright, blocky letters forming the film’s
title run across the marquee like a cheap

02:30:07.439 --> 02:30:07.740
tattoo.

02:30:07.740 --> 02:30:10.620
It’s madness.

02:30:10.620 --> 02:30:15.960
A frenzied crowd gathers at the box office,
pushing and shoving, clawing at the glass

02:30:15.960 --> 02:30:17.220
partition for a ticket.

02:30:17.220 --> 02:30:23.160
The entire theater has been reserved for this
special event: a one-time-only screening of

02:30:23.160 --> 02:30:24.746
a rare, critically acclaimed film.

02:30:24.746 --> 02:30:24.840
Drawn in by the excitement, the subject indulges
their curiosity and gets in line for a ticket.

02:30:24.840 --> 02:30:27.420
Admission is entirely free.

02:30:27.420 --> 02:30:29.520
But is it actually free?

02:30:29.520 --> 02:30:32.520
Or has the cost just not declared itself?

02:30:32.520 --> 02:30:37.320
When the subject reaches the auditorium, they
scan their eyes over the sea of people.

02:30:37.319 --> 02:30:40.380
Heads emerge from the backs of almost every
seat.

02:30:40.380 --> 02:30:45.480
They can only find one empty space, and they
have to shuffle like a crab to get past the

02:30:45.479 --> 02:30:46.500
mass of other viewers.

02:30:46.500 --> 02:30:50.819
In their seat, they notice that a large number
of people throughout the audience are wearing

02:30:50.819 --> 02:30:52.259
disposable face masks.

02:30:52.260 --> 02:30:56.760
The woman sitting next to the subject’s
right wears one such mask, as does the woman

02:30:56.760 --> 02:30:57.360
on their left.

02:30:57.359 --> 02:31:01.679
And before you think, “Well, that isn’t
so weird”, it’s important to note that

02:31:01.680 --> 02:31:05.940
all of these accounts were taken years before
certain recent events.

02:31:05.939 --> 02:31:08.280
Patrons continue to pack into the theater.

02:31:08.280 --> 02:31:12.000
Despite every chair being filled, more and
more people enter.

02:31:12.000 --> 02:31:14.220
The aisles become overflow seating.

02:31:14.220 --> 02:31:17.400
Regulations of Max Occupancy are dismissed.

02:31:17.399 --> 02:31:22.200
In the darkness of the room, all that can
be clearly seen are shapes.

02:31:22.200 --> 02:31:23.939
The rectangle that is the screen.

02:31:23.939 --> 02:31:27.120
The hundreds of circles that are the heads
of the audience.

02:31:27.120 --> 02:31:30.359
But also there’s a shape that feels misplaced.

02:31:30.359 --> 02:31:33.599
What appears to be the letter “P” sticks
up from the crowd.

02:31:33.600 --> 02:31:36.480
A thin line with a bulge protruding from the
top.

02:31:36.479 --> 02:31:39.359
The specificity of its origin is difficult
to define.

02:31:39.960 --> 02:31:45.180
But once the subject’s eyes adjust to the
dark, they notice it’s an IV pole carrying

02:31:45.180 --> 02:31:46.980
a bag of unknown fluid.

02:31:46.979 --> 02:31:50.399
However, there is no clear indication of who
it is connected to.

02:31:50.399 --> 02:31:54.780
Furthermore, they notice one of the masked
audience members is wearing a hospital gown.

02:31:54.780 --> 02:31:59.519
There is little time to assess any of these
strange visuals, because there are no trailers

02:31:59.520 --> 02:32:01.260
or advertisements before the film.

02:32:01.260 --> 02:32:06.180
As soon as the audience is settled into whatever
space they might find temporarily habitable,

02:32:06.180 --> 02:32:09.300
the theater goes silent and the film begins.

02:32:09.300 --> 02:32:14.640
The film opens with the female protagonist
going about mundane activities in her day-to-day

02:32:14.640 --> 02:32:15.000
life.

02:32:15.000 --> 02:32:16.920
A phone call interrupts her.

02:32:16.920 --> 02:32:22.200
It’s an unknown party, who tells her that
a loved one has been hospitalized, for one

02:32:22.200 --> 02:32:22.979
reason or another.

02:32:22.979 --> 02:32:26.639
The protagonist drops what she’s doing to
accept the call to action.

02:32:26.640 --> 02:32:29.340
On her way to the hospital, however, she is&nbsp;

02:32:29.340 --> 02:32:31.920
attacked by a male assailant&nbsp;
and loses consciousness.

02:32:31.920 --> 02:32:36.420
The protagonist wakes up in a fog, unable
to make sense of her surroundings.

02:32:36.420 --> 02:32:41.460
But the audience understands that wherever
she is, she’s in deep trouble.

02:32:41.460 --> 02:32:45.720
She’s in a desolate and unfamiliar building
with her arms and legs bound.

02:32:45.720 --> 02:32:51.120
She is accompanied by a number of other female
captives, some of whom still remain seemingly

02:32:51.120 --> 02:32:53.099
unconscious or possibly dead.

02:32:53.100 --> 02:32:57.781
The women briefly discuss their strategy to
escape, but they are interrupted when the

02:32:57.780 --> 02:32:58.859
kidnapper appears.

02:32:58.859 --> 02:33:03.540
Descriptions of hairstyle, eye color, and
wardrobe would fall short in showing us who

02:33:03.540 --> 02:33:04.859
this character really is.

02:33:04.859 --> 02:33:08.219
Instead, his swift and decisive actions do
the talking.

02:33:08.220 --> 02:33:11.880
He sees one of the women crying and kills
her without hesitation.

02:33:11.880 --> 02:33:16.020
The kidnapper reveals to the women the code
he intended to operate under.

02:33:16.020 --> 02:33:21.960
He would release the captives after 24 hours,
but only under the condition that they do

02:33:21.960 --> 02:33:22.620
not cry.

02:33:22.620 --> 02:33:26.880
During the film, the kidnapper demonstrates
various forms of physical and psychological

02:33:26.880 --> 02:33:28.200
torture on the group of women.

02:33:28.200 --> 02:33:33.359
Despite their best efforts, the captives are
unable to hold back their tears, and one by

02:33:33.359 --> 02:33:37.139
one they are murdered until only the protagonist
remains.

02:33:37.140 --> 02:33:42.540
Frustrated by the protagonist's unremarkable
resolve, the kidnapper takes more extreme

02:33:42.540 --> 02:33:45.420
measures, increasing the violence and intensity
of her torture.

02:33:45.420 --> 02:33:48.660
However, the protagonist is not one to be
messed with.

02:33:48.660 --> 02:33:52.920
Despite being tormented, she seems in control
of the situation.

02:33:52.920 --> 02:33:56.160
She doesn’t succumb to the traditional dynamics
of torture.

02:33:56.160 --> 02:34:00.180
Even while on the wrong end of the bat, she
isn’t afraid to swing.

02:34:00.180 --> 02:34:02.100
She fights back how she can.

02:34:02.100 --> 02:34:04.320
She mocks and insults her captive.

02:34:04.319 --> 02:34:06.660
And it's then that she finds his weakness.

02:34:06.660 --> 02:34:10.740
Although a physically empowering villain,
he is emotionally fragile.

02:34:10.740 --> 02:34:16.139
Her grit frustrates him, causing him to lose
focus and become noticeably rattled.

02:34:16.140 --> 02:34:20.940
As the protagonist continues to weaken her
kidnapper with words, the subject in the audience

02:34:20.939 --> 02:34:24.000
notices what seems to be a slight echo in
the dialogue.

02:34:24.000 --> 02:34:26.760
They look up to see if something is wrong
with the speakers.

02:34:26.760 --> 02:34:30.720
They even wonder if this is a stylistic decision
made by the director.

02:34:30.720 --> 02:34:33.180
But then they notice where it is coming from.

02:34:33.180 --> 02:34:38.281
They eventually realize that the two masked
women sitting beside them are quietly repeating

02:34:38.280 --> 02:34:40.679
every line of dialogue as it occurs.

02:34:40.680 --> 02:34:44.100
Their lips moving causes their masks to shake
softly.

02:34:44.100 --> 02:34:48.960
With each word, the air from their whispers
blows the cloth away from their mouth, only

02:34:48.960 --> 02:34:52.140
for the mask to collapse back in when the
syllable is completed.

02:34:52.140 --> 02:34:57.000
If the subject looks even closer, they will
see that the lower half of the women's masks

02:34:57.000 --> 02:34:58.680
are saturated with saliva.

02:34:58.680 --> 02:35:02.940
Things don’t get any more pleasant when
the subject shifts their gaze down the women’s

02:35:02.939 --> 02:35:03.120
body.

02:35:03.120 --> 02:35:08.700
Just near their waists, their hands are clasped
together so tightly that their fingernails

02:35:08.700 --> 02:35:10.019
are digging into their skin.

02:35:10.020 --> 02:35:15.541
They tremble and fidget as if having taken
on the burden of the world’s anxiety; and

02:35:15.540 --> 02:35:17.460
no medication can calm them down.

02:35:17.460 --> 02:35:23.160
Fingernails dig deeper into their skin, drawing
blood and foreshadowing the film’s finale.

02:35:23.160 --> 02:35:28.559
At the film's climax, the kidnapper approaches
the protagonist with a double edge razor blade

02:35:28.560 --> 02:35:31.380
and announces that even if she is freed, she&nbsp;

02:35:31.380 --> 02:35:34.020
will spend the rest of her&nbsp;
life horribly disfigured.

02:35:34.020 --> 02:35:38.820
This leads to an argument between the two,
the subtext of which alludes to themes such

02:35:38.819 --> 02:35:44.219
as the nature of inner and outer beauty, the
value of women in society, and the societal

02:35:44.220 --> 02:35:46.920
stigma against expressions of vulnerability.

02:35:46.920 --> 02:35:50.940
The argument on the surface, however, does
not sound sophisticated or profound.

02:35:50.939 --> 02:35:54.660
There is no consolation for a fight having
emotional depths.

02:35:54.660 --> 02:35:59.580
At the end of the day, the blows will always
feel more physical than intellectual.

02:35:59.580 --> 02:36:03.660
Eventually, the kidnapper loses patience,
and grabs the protagonist by the shoulders

02:36:03.660 --> 02:36:04.920
and slams her to the floor.

02:36:04.920 --> 02:36:07.620
He reaches out his hands and grabs her by
the face.

02:36:07.620 --> 02:36:11.519
The subject’s attention is again pulled
from the screen when they hear groaning from

02:36:11.520 --> 02:36:12.106
every direction.

02:36:12.105 --> 02:36:12.936
It sounds like the accumulative grumblings
of every chore in human history ever moaned

02:36:12.936 --> 02:36:12.954
and groaned about.

02:36:12.954 --> 02:36:17.580
These wordless hums fill the theater with
a sense of worry and uneasiness, so much so

02:36:17.580 --> 02:36:23.399
that the subject feels the room getting smaller
as the air polluted with angst grows thicker.

02:36:23.399 --> 02:36:27.839
As the subject brings their eyes back to the
film, they see the kidnapper gripping the

02:36:27.840 --> 02:36:33.300
protagonist's lower lip between his thumb
and forefinger, squeezing tight as to pop

02:36:33.300 --> 02:36:34.560
her lip like a balloon.

02:36:34.560 --> 02:36:37.860
The kidnapper then takes the razor blade to
the victim’s lips.

02:36:37.859 --> 02:36:39.960
He pauses to mock the protagonist.

02:36:39.960 --> 02:36:45.180
And while he does this, blinded by pride and
arrogance, she jolts into action, grabbing

02:36:45.180 --> 02:36:46.920
the razor right out of his hands with her
teeth.

02:36:46.920 --> 02:36:49.500
She follows up her burglary with assault.

02:36:49.500 --> 02:36:53.220
Her neck stabs forward and in a blur, she
stabs the kidnappers' eyes out.

02:36:53.220 --> 02:36:55.200
It happens in an instant.

02:36:55.200 --> 02:36:57.179
The kidnapper has no time to react.

02:36:57.180 --> 02:36:58.920
Blood spills from his face.

02:36:58.920 --> 02:37:00.899
Screams pour out of his mouth.

02:37:00.899 --> 02:37:05.040
During this time, the protagonist maneuvers
the razor into her fingers.

02:37:05.040 --> 02:37:09.720
She hacks away at her bindings, but they are
stubborn and resist her sawing.

02:37:09.720 --> 02:37:14.460
The battle continues, and the kidnapper is
able to tear off the victim’s lips entirely.

02:37:14.460 --> 02:37:16.620
She looks like a monster now.

02:37:16.620 --> 02:37:21.240
As the kidnapper smiles and celebrates his
wrongdoings, the protagonist finishes freeing

02:37:21.240 --> 02:37:24.179
herself and slits the kidnapper's throat with
the razor.

02:37:24.180 --> 02:37:29.100
The protagonist locates the exit and scrabbles
toward it as the kidnapper bleeds to death

02:37:29.100 --> 02:37:30.420
on the cold, hard floor.

02:37:30.420 --> 02:37:32.940
The protagonist then speaks her final words.

02:37:32.939 --> 02:37:36.540
Due to her injuries, her voice is muffled
and raspy.

02:37:36.540 --> 02:37:38.760
She bears down and calmly speaks.

02:37:38.760 --> 02:37:45.180
One last time, she mocks the kidnapper, telling
him that he "cried tears of blood" and therefore

02:37:45.180 --> 02:37:47.220
had to die according to his own rules.

02:37:47.220 --> 02:37:52.920
During this climax, it is reported that subjects
experience a sense of dread unique to any

02:37:52.920 --> 02:37:54.120
other terror ever felt.

02:37:54.120 --> 02:37:58.500
Strangely, the feeling appeared not to correspond
to the scene in the film at all.

02:37:58.500 --> 02:38:03.720
It is a feeling entirely separate from the
viewing experience, as if for a moment the

02:38:03.720 --> 02:38:08.640
subject is taken out of the moment in time
and transported to a realm composed only of

02:38:08.640 --> 02:38:09.781
fear and anxiety.

02:38:09.780 --> 02:38:13.080
But just as quickly as they left, they returned.

02:38:13.080 --> 02:38:17.880
The Foundation sees it worth mentioning that
this peculiar moment happens to occur during

02:38:17.880 --> 02:38:20.160
the film’s most clearly recalled scene.

02:38:20.160 --> 02:38:25.319
While descriptions of violence vary from one
viewer to the next, the climax is unshakable

02:38:25.319 --> 02:38:26.519
in its consistency.

02:38:26.520 --> 02:38:32.400
This is further evidence that SCP-5040 has
a core foundation that remains intact.

02:38:32.399 --> 02:38:35.099
False memories are given validity by our peers.

02:38:35.100 --> 02:38:38.820
When we tell a story, the inaccuracies are
often the best parts.

02:38:38.819 --> 02:38:40.439
The exaggerated drama.

02:38:40.439 --> 02:38:42.419
The rearranged sequence of events.

02:38:42.420 --> 02:38:46.740
The jokes you thought of only in retrospect
but weaved into later accounts.

02:38:46.740 --> 02:38:52.019
While a storyteller knows when to lie, a good
storyteller knows how not to get caught.

02:38:52.020 --> 02:38:58.081
SCP-5040 captivates its audience, but it seems
to know how much it can get away with.

02:38:58.080 --> 02:39:03.720
By providing a consistent, objective truth
– a climax that is reported the same across

02:39:03.720 --> 02:39:11.160
all accounts – SCP-5040 insists, even if
only briefly, on being a credible film, not

02:39:11.160 --> 02:39:14.040
just a random sequence of 24 frames per second.

02:39:14.040 --> 02:39:18.720
Eyes back to the screen, the film abruptly
cuts to an unspecified point in the future.

02:39:18.720 --> 02:39:24.180
Now wearing a face mask to hide her disfigured
mouth, no different than the ones seen worn

02:39:24.180 --> 02:39:28.860
in theater, the protagonist walks down the
street to her apartment, indifferent to the

02:39:28.859 --> 02:39:30.599
crowd of paparazzi that follows her.

02:39:30.600 --> 02:39:33.121
It’s clear that this is now just a part
of her life.

02:39:33.120 --> 02:39:34.979
The lights flash on her face.

02:39:34.979 --> 02:39:39.240
When she reaches her bedroom, the protagonist
slowly pulls down her mask.

02:39:39.240 --> 02:39:42.899
Face free from cloth, she stares at herself
in the mirror.

02:39:42.899 --> 02:39:43.920
It is silent.

02:39:43.920 --> 02:39:46.620
What she sees is less notable than what she
doesn’t.

02:39:46.620 --> 02:39:49.380
The lower portion of her face is gone.

02:39:49.380 --> 02:39:51.240
She focuses on only that.

02:39:51.240 --> 02:39:54.599
It’s hard to know if she will ever see into
her own eyes again.

02:39:54.600 --> 02:39:58.140
Her gaze might forever be fixed on what she
can’t get back.

02:39:58.140 --> 02:40:03.000
And through those eyes that long to see more,
she sheds a single tear.

02:40:03.000 --> 02:40:08.640
Over the course of several minutes, her weeping
gradually builds into frenzied sobs and shrieks.

02:40:08.640 --> 02:40:13.320
It can first be misinterpreted as bad acting,
a case of doing too much.

02:40:13.319 --> 02:40:18.780
But as the crying carries onward, it’s understood
that the horror in her wallowing is all too

02:40:18.780 --> 02:40:18.960
real.

02:40:18.960 --> 02:40:24.240
The film cuts to black and the credits roll,
but the sound of the protagonist's cries continue

02:40:24.240 --> 02:40:30.899
to play with no other audio until the credit
reel ends, and even then, in silence, it’s

02:40:30.899 --> 02:40:33.299
hard to say if she ever stopped crying.

02:40:33.300 --> 02:40:38.460
Audience members remain silent after the movie
ends, exchanging only whispers as they exit

02:40:38.460 --> 02:40:38.819
the theater.

02:40:38.819 --> 02:40:43.799
Feet step over small red puddles and stains
on the theater floor as if they are sticky

02:40:43.800 --> 02:40:44.760
puddles of soda.

02:40:44.760 --> 02:40:49.980
Those who remain past this point experience
an escalating feeling of unwelcomeness, until

02:40:49.979 --> 02:40:53.879
sitting and staying is more physically demanding
than getting up and walking out.

02:40:53.880 --> 02:40:59.640
Some experiences will stay with you forever,
even if they never actually happened.

